 The\nIdempotencyKey uniqueness scope is too narrow (currently described as\n(idempotency_key, tenant_id)); update the design text and any related references\nto scope idempotency by the full ingestion context — e.g., (tenant_id,\nsource_id, usage_type, resource_id, idempotency_key) — so duplicates are only\ndetected per stream/resource/type; ensure you update the IdempotencyKey\ndefinition and all other occurrences of the old tuple (including the other\ninstance noted in the doc) to reflect the new composite key.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`727-728`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Privacy section should not assert “not personal data.”**\n\nThis wording is unsafe for compliance since subject/resource identifiers may be personal data depending on tenant configuration and jurisdiction. Please switch to conditional classification language and reference tenant/operator controls.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/usage-collector/docs/DESIGN.md` around lines 727 - 728, The\nPrivacy/GDPR statement labeled \"**Not applicable — Privacy/GDPR**: Usage records\ncontain tenant, resource, and subject identifiers but not personal data...\" is\ntoo categorical; update this text to use conditional classification language and\npoint to tenant/operator controls and the authn/authz and platform-level\npolicies instead. Replace the assertion that identifiers \"are not personal data\"\nwith wording such as \"may be personal data depending on tenant configuration and\njurisdiction\" and add a sentence referencing tenant/operator responsibility and\nrelevant modules (authn/authz and platform-level policies) for data minimization\nand retention controls so compliance depends on deployment and configuration.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/usage-query/docs/DESIGN.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`501-502`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Privacy statement is too absolute for identifier-bearing usage data.**\n\nSaying usage records “do not contain PII” is risky because identifier fields can become personal data depending on tenant mapping and jurisdiction. Reword this section to “may contain personal data” and point to tenant/operator responsibilities for classification, minimization, and controls.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/usage-query/docs/DESIGN.md` around lines 501 - 502, Update the\nPrivacy Architecture paragraph so it no longer states usage records “do not\ncontain PII”; instead say usage records “may contain personal data” and\nexplicitly call out that identifier-bearing fields can become personal data\ndepending on tenant mapping and jurisdiction, and that tenants/operators are\nresponsible for classification, minimization, retention, and access controls;\nkeep the existing mention of tenant isolation\n(`cpt-cf-usage-query-fr-tenant-isolation`) as the privacy boundary but add a\nshort line directing readers to platform-level controls and operator\nresponsibilities if PII is present.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/usage-collector/docs/DESIGN-old.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-3`: **Prefer a single canonical design doc for Usage Collector.**\n\nKeeping both `DESIGN-old.md` and `DESIGN.md` for the same module risks divergence and contradictory implementation guidance. Consider archiving this file under an explicit historical folder or replacing it with a short pointer to the canonical design.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/usage-collector/docs/DESIGN-old.md` 