rifies filtering entities by isSchema=false (instances only).\n> +    Verifies filtering entities by is_schema=false (instances only).\n>      \"\"\"\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> \n> `363-377`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Use snake_case `segment_scope` parameter in test to match existing pattern.**\n> \n> The test uses `params={\"segmentScope\": \"primary\"}` (camelCase), which is inconsistent with the pattern established by the `is_schema` parameter. The `test_list_entities_filter_by_is_schema_true` test uses `params={\"is_schema\": \"true\"}` (snake_case). Both parameters should follow the same naming convention in the test code—update to `params={\"segment_scope\": \"primary\"}` for consistency.\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@testing/docker/hyperspot.Dockerfile`:\n- Line 21: The Dockerfile currently copies apps/gts-docs-validator into the\nbuilder stage with COPY apps/gts-docs-validator but never builds or installs\nit—only hyperspot-server is built via cargo build—so either remove that COPY\nline to avoid unused files, or add the missing build/install and runtime copy:\nupdate the build step that runs cargo in the builder to also build the\ngts-docs-validator crate (e.g., include it in the cargo build target or run\ncargo build --package gts-docs-validator), and then COPY the resulting binary\nfrom the builder into the runtime stage (similar to how hyperspot-server is\ncopied) so the validator is present in the final image.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/types_registry/test_types_registry_list.py`:\n- Around line 329-331: The assertion message uses the old field name `isSchema`;\nupdate the failure message in the loop that iterates over `entities` (the block\nwith `for entity in entities:` and the assertion `assert entity[\"is_schema\"] is\nTrue`) to reference the correct field `is_schema` (e.g., change f\"Expected\nisSchema=True: {entity}\" to f\"Expected is_schema=True: {entity}\") so the error\ntext matches the actual key being checked.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>config/e2e-docker.yaml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`114-115`: **Contradictory auth configuration settings.**\n\n`auth_disabled: true` and `require_auth_by_default: true` appear contradictory. When `auth_disabled` is true, the `require_auth_by_default` setting likely has no effect. Consider removing `require_auth_by_default` or adding a comment explaining that it's preserved for when auth is re-enabled.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/docker/hyperspot.Dockerfile (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`31-35`: **Debug build used for runtime image.**\n\nThe cargo build runs without `--release` flag, and line 43 copies from `target/debug/`. For e2e tests this may be intentional (faster builds, debug symbols), but consider adding a comment clarifying this is deliberate, or use `--release` for a smaller, faster binary.\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 43-43\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file-parser/src/config.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`11-14`: **Consider compile-time guard for additional safety.**\n\nFor defense-in-depth, you could gate this feature behind a cargo feature flag to prevent accidental enablement in production builds.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Optional enhancement</summary>\n\nIn `Cargo.toml`:\n```toml\n[features]\nallow-insecure-http = []\n```\n\nIn `config.rs`:\n```rust\n#[cfg(feature = \"allow-insecure-http\")]\n#[serde(default)]\npub allow_insecure_http: bool,\n\n#[cfg(not(feature = \"allow-insecure-http\"))]\n#[serde(default, skip)]\nallow_insecure_http: bool,\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/609#pullrequestre