table/releases.html?utm_source=openai))\n\nCitations:\n\n- 1: https://blog.rust-lang.org/releases/?utm_source=openai\n- 2: https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/releases.html?utm_source=openai\n\n---\n\n🌐 Web query:\n\n`release-plz action latest version 2026`\n\n💡 Result:\n\nAs of **February 2026**, the **Release-plz GitHub Action** is used via the moving tag:\n\n- **`release-plz/action@v0.5`** (this tag tracks the latest `0.5.x` release in the `v0.5` series) [1].\n\nIf you want to **pin exactly what “v0.5” pointed to most recently**, a downstream pin update on **2026-02-03** shows `v0.5` being pinned to commit:\n\n- **`release-plz/action@e592230ad39e3ec735402572601fc621aa24355c`** [2].\n\nRelated (often confused): the **release-plz CLI crate** latest visible on docs.rs in early 2026 is **`release-plz 0.3.153`** [3].\n\n**Sources:** [1] [2] [3]\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n**Update Rust toolchain and release-plz/action to current versions.**\n\nThe code uses outdated versions. Rust 1.92.0 should be updated to 1.93.0 (released January 22, 2026), and release-plz/action v0.3.155 should be updated to v0.5 (as of February 2026). Pinning commit hashes with version comments remains good practice, but the pinned versions themselves are behind current releases.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn @.github/workflows/release-plz.yml around lines 44 - 48, Update the GitHub\nActions workflow to use the newer Rust toolchain and release-plz action: change\nthe dtolnay/rust-toolchain reference currently pinned to 1.92.0 to the 1.93.0\nrelease (update the commit hash to the one for Rust 1.93.0) and update the\nrelease-plz/action reference from v0.3.155 to v0.5 (replace the commit hash to\nthe v0.5 tag commit); keep the pattern of pinning commit hashes while updating\nthe comment/version labels so dtolnay/rust-toolchain and release-plz/action\nreflect the new versions.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:poseidon:ocelot -->\n\n<!-- This is an au