url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-02-13T13:55:36Z","updated_at":"2026-02-13T13:55:36Z","body":"There are unresolved review comments awaiting response.\n\nUnanswered human review threads: 6\nUnresolved comments: 6","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/comments/3897378649/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":{"id":15368,"client_id":"Iv1.05c79e9ad1f6bdfa","slug":"github-actions","node_id":"MDM6QXBwMTUzNjg=","owner":{"login":"github","id":9919,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjk5MTk=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9919?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/github","html_url":"https://github.com/github","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/received_events","type":"Organization","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"name":"GitHub Actions","description":"Automate your workflow from idea to production","external_url":"https://help.github.com/en/actions","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/github-actions","created_at":"2018-07-30T09:30:17Z","updated_at":"2026-05-05T14:51:38Z","permissions":{"actions":"write","administration":"read","artifact_metadata":"write","attestations":"write","checks":"write","code_quality":"write","contents":"write","copilot_requests":"write","deployments":"write","discussions":"write","issues":"write","merge_queues":"write","metadata":"read","models":"read","packages":"write","pages":"write","pull_requests":"write","repository_hooks":"write","repository_projects":"write","security_events":"write","statuses":"write","vulnerability_alerts":"read"},"events":["branch_protection_rule","check_run","check_suite","create","delete","deployment","deployment_status","discussion","discussion_comment","fork","gollum","issues","issue_comment","label","merge_group","milestone","page_build","public","pull_request","pull_request_review","pull_request_review_comment","push","registry_package","release","repository","repository_dispatch","status","watch","workflow_dispatch","workflow_run"]}}],"source_review_comments":[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786178531","pull_request_review_id":3777103758,"id":2786178531,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEbXj","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Priority-Based Load Shedding\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-load-shedding`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support priority classification of usage event types (e.g., billing-critical counters vs. analytics metrics) and, when operating under sustained overload beyond rate limits, **MUST** preferentially accept higher-priority events while shedding lower-priority ones. The priority classification **MUST** be configurable per usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Under extreme load, indiscriminate rejection causes billing-critical data loss. Priority-based load shedding ensures that the most business-critical usage data (which affects revenue accuracy) is preserved even when the system cannot accept all traffic.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Observability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-observability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-tenant and per-source rate limit consumption as metrics (current usage vs. limit, rejection counts, throttle duration) for operator dashboards. The system **MUST** emit alerts when tenants approach configured warning thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90% of capacity).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operators need visibility into rate limit utilization to proactively adjust limits before tenants experience rejections. Approaching-limit alerts enable capacity planning.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### High Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-availability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** maintain 99.95% monthly availability for usage collection endpoints.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 99.95% uptime per calendar month\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection is on the critical path for all billable operations.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Throughput\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-throughput`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support ingestion of at least 100,000 usage records per minute under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: >= 100,000 records/minute sustained\n+**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-ingestion-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage record ingestion within 200ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 200ms\n+**Rationale**: Low ingestion latency prevents blocking in usage source services.\n+\n+#### Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage queries for 30-day ranges within 500ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 500ms for 30-day range queries\n+**Rationale**: Billing and quota enforcement systems require fast query responses.\n+\n+#### Exactly-Once Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-exactly-once`\n+\n+The system **MUST** guarantee exactly-once processing; zero usage records lost or duplicated under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss or duplication under normal operation\n+**Rationale**: Duplicate or missing records directly impact billing accuracy.\n+\n+#### Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"graphite-app[bot]","id":96075541,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOBbn_FQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/158384?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/graphite-app","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Duplicate requirement ID `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation` conflicts with the functional requirement at line 223. This breaks requirement traceability and will cause issues in any requirement tracking system.\n\n```markdown\n# Change the ID to something unique, e.g.:\n- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n```\n```suggestion\n- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n```\n  \n\n*Spotted by [Graphite Agent](https://app.graphite.com/diamond/?org=cyberfabric&ref=ai-review-comment)*<i class='graphite__hidden'><br /><br /><a href=\"https://app.graphite.com/github/pr/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/536?chatWithGeneratedComment=f06adfa8-0679-4aa7-b08f-41b19fc56447\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Fix in Graphite\" src=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-dark.svg\"></picture></a></i><i class='graphite__hidden'><br /><br />Is this helpful? React 👍 or 👎 to let us know.</i>","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:08:26Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T07:08:30Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786178531","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786178531"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786178531"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786178531/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":493,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":493,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786178679","pull_request_review_id":3777103758,"id":2786178679,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEbZ3","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Priority-Based Load Shedding\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-load-shedding`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support priority classification of usage event types (e.g., billing-critical counters vs. analytics metrics) and, when operating under sustained overload beyond rate limits, **MUST** preferentially accept higher-priority events while shedding lower-priority ones. The priority classification **MUST** be configurable per usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Under extreme load, indiscriminate rejection causes billing-critical data loss. Priority-based load shedding ensures that the most business-critical usage data (which affects revenue accuracy) is preserved even when the system cannot accept all traffic.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Observability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-observability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-tenant and per-source rate limit consumption as metrics (current usage vs. limit, rejection counts, throttle duration) for operator dashboards. The system **MUST** emit alerts when tenants approach configured warning thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90% of capacity).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operators need visibility into rate limit utilization to proactively adjust limits before tenants experience rejections. Approaching-limit alerts enable capacity planning.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### High Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-availability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** maintain 99.95% monthly availability for usage collection endpoints.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 99.95% uptime per calendar month\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection is on the critical path for all billable operations.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Throughput\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-throughput`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support ingestion of at least 100,000 usage records per minute under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: >= 100,000 records/minute sustained\n+**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-ingestion-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage record ingestion within 200ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 200ms\n+**Rationale**: Low ingestion latency prevents blocking in usage source services.\n+\n+#### Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage queries for 30-day ranges within 500ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 500ms for 30-day range queries\n+**Rationale**: Billing and quota enforcement systems require fast query responses.\n+\n+#### Exactly-Once Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-exactly-once`\n+\n+The system **MUST** guarantee exactly-once processing; zero usage records lost or duplicated under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss or duplication under normal operation\n+**Rationale**: Duplicate or missing records directly impact billing accuracy.\n+\n+#### Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce complete tenant isolation at API, storage, and query layers; no cross-tenant data leakage under any circumstance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data access\n+**Rationale**: Tenant isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** preserve immutable audit records for all usage data including source, timestamps, and any corrections.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of usage operations audited\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for billing disputes and compliance.\n+\n+#### Authentication Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authentication`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require authentication (OAuth 2.0, mTLS, or API key) for all API operations.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthenticated API access\n+**Rationale**: Usage data is sensitive and must be protected.\n+\n+#### Authorization Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce authorization for read/write operations based on tenant context and usage type permissions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized data access\n+**Rationale**: Authorization prevents unauthorized usage data manipulation.\n+\n+#### Horizontal Scalability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-scalability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** scale horizontally to handle increased load without architectural changes.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Linear throughput scaling with added instances\n+**Rationale**: Usage volume grows with platform adoption.\n+\n+#### Storage Fault Tolerance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-fault-tolerance`\n+\n+The system **MUST** buffer usage records during storage backend failures and recover without data loss.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss during storage backend failures\n+**Rationale**: Storage outages must not result in lost usage data.\n+\n+#### Configurable Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support retention periods from 7 days to 7 years depending on usage type and compliance requirements.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Configurable retention from 7 days to 7 years\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different compliance and operational retention needs.\n+\n+#### Graceful Degradation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-graceful-degradation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** continue accepting usage records even if downstream consumers (billing, monitoring) are unavailable.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero ingestion failures due to downstream consumer unavailability\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection must not be blocked by consumer outages.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Usage Ingestion SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-sdk`\n+\n+**Type**: Client library\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Client-side SDK with automatic batching for high-throughput usage emission. Primary ingestion path for platform services.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Usage REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: HTTP API for usage record ingestion (low-volume/external), querying, and administration (unit registration, retention configuration).\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Collector Agent\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-collector-agent`\n+\n+**Type**: Sidecar/agent process\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Sidecar deployment pattern for collecting usage from co-located services without SDK integration.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### Storage Adapter Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-storage-adapter`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Rust trait / plugin interface\n+**Compatibility**: Backward-compatible within major version; adapters implement a defined trait for read/write operations.\n+\n+#### Types Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-types-registry`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Internal API\n+**Compatibility**: Schema validation contract; UC depends on Types Registry for unit and type definitions.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+#### UC: High-Volume Usage Emission via SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-emission`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Usage type registered in Types Registry\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Service calls SDK to record usage event\n+2. SDK queues event in memory\n+3. SDK batches events (by count or time threshold)\n+4. SDK sends batch to UC\n+5. UC validates each record against type schema\n+6. UC persists to configured storage backend\n+7. SDK receives acknowledgment\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Usage records persisted with tenant/resource attribution; available for query and aggregation\n+\n+#### UC: Configure Custom Measuring Unit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-unit`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Unit name is unique\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Operator defines unit schema (name, type: counter/gauge, base unit)\n+2. Operator submits via API\n+3. UC validates unit definition\n+4. UC registers unit with Types Registry\n+5. UC confirms registration\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- New unit immediately available for usage collection; sources can emit records with new unit type\n+\n+#### UC: Query Tenant Usage for Billing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-billing-query`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Aggregation window closed\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Billing System queries usage API for billing period\n+2. UC retrieves aggregated usage for tenant and time window\n+3. UC returns usage grouped by usage type\n+4. Billing System processes for rating\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Billing System has accurate, deduplicated usage data for invoice generation\n+\n+#### UC: Real-Time Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Quota Enforcement System queries UC API for current usage\n+2. UC returns aggregated usage for tenant and usage type\n+3. Quota Enforcement System compares against tenant limits\n+4. Quota Enforcement System triggers enforcement if threshold exceeded\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Tenant usage enforced before exceeding quota; no over-consumption\n+\n+#### UC: Add New Storage Backend\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-add-storage`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Storage adapter plugin implemented\n+- Backend accessible\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Operator deploys adapter plugin\n+2. Operator configures adapter connection in UC\n+3. Operator defines routing rules (which usage types to which backend)\n+4. UC discovers and validates adapter\n+5. UC begins routing matching usage types to new backend\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- New storage backend active; usage routed per configuration; existing data unaffected\n+\n+#### UC: Backfill Usage After Outage\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-after-outage`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Gap detected via reconciliation (missing heartbeats, stalled watermarks, or count mismatch)\n+- Replacement usage data available from secondary source (infrastructure metrics, API gateway logs, or source service replay)\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Reconciliation system detects gap and alerts operator with affected time range, tenant(s), and usage type(s)\n+2. Operator prepares replacement events from secondary source\n+3. Operator submits backfill request specifying time range, tenant, and replacement events\n+4. UC validates time range is within backfill window\n+5. UC checks if time range overlaps with an already-invoiced period; if so, requires explicit operator confirmation\n+6. UC archives existing events in the time range (if any)\n+7. UC validates and persists replacement events with backfill idempotency namespace\n+8. UC creates audit record for the backfill operation\n+9. UC re-aggregates affected time windows\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Gap filled with corrected data; archived events retained for audit; aggregations updated; downstream consumers can query corrected data\n+\n+#### UC: View Tenant Usage Report\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-report`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Administrator requests usage for time period\n+2. UC queries aggregated usage for tenant only\n+4. UC returns usage breakdown by type and resource","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"graphite-app[bot]","id":96075541,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOBbn_FQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/158384?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/graphite-app","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Missing step 3 in the use case flow. The sequence jumps from step 2 directly to step 4, making the use case incomplete.\n\n```markdown\n# Add the missing step:\n2. UC queries aggregated usage for tenant only\n3. UC filters and formats usage data\n4. UC returns usage breakdown by type and resource\n```\n```suggestion\n\n2. UC queries aggregated usage for tenant only\n3. UC filters and formats usage data\n4. UC returns usage breakdown by type and resource\n\n```\n  \n\n*Spotted by [Graphite Agent](https://app.graphite.com/diamond/?org=cyberfabric&ref=ai-review-comment)*<i class='graphite__hidden'><br /><br /><a href=\"https://app.graphite.com/github/pr/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/536?chatWithGeneratedComment=c55cdc3c-4eec-48aa-8c81-dc83a5faab57\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Fix in Graphite\" src=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-dark.svg\"></picture></a></i><i class='graphite__hidden'><br /><br />Is this helpful? React 👍 or 👎 to let us know.</i>","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:08:29Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T07:08:30Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786178679","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786178679"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786178679"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786178679/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":739,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":740,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":740,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184368","pull_request_review_id":3777110342,"id":2786184368,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEcyw","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Clarify pull-based collection scope and priority.**\n\nLine 144 states \"Pull-based collection: P2 future capability\" under Out of Scope, but lines 162-170 define it as a P2 functional requirement with detailed rationale and actors. This creates ambiguity about whether pull-based collection is planned for the initial release or deferred.\n\nConsider either:\n1. Removing it from \"Out of Scope\" if it's a P2 requirement for the initial release\n2. Removing the detailed functional requirement if it's truly deferred to a future phase\n3. Clarifying in both sections that P2 requirements may be implemented after initial launch\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 162-170\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` at line 144, The document is\ninconsistent about \"Pull-based collection\": it appears under \"Out of Scope\" and\nalso as a P2 functional requirement; reconcile by choosing one of three fixes\nand applying it to both places—either remove the \"Pull-based collection\" entry\nfrom the Out of Scope section if you intend it as a P2 for initial release, or\nremove the detailed P2 functional requirement (the \"Pull-based collection\"\nblock) if it is truly deferred, or explicitly state in both the Out of Scope\nsection and the P2 functional requirement block that P2 items (including\n\"Pull-based collection\") are planned for post-launch prioritization; update the\ntext for the phrase \"Pull-based collection\" and both the Out of Scope and P2\nfunctional requirement sections so they convey the same scope/priority.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit c471acf","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:18Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T08:07:50Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184368","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184368"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184368"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184368/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":144,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":144,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184371","pull_request_review_id":3777110342,"id":2786184371,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEcyz","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Resolve inconsistency between requirements and open questions.**\n\nThe grace period and backfill window are specified with concrete defaults in the functional requirements:\n- Line 285: \"default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type\"\n- Line 314: \"default 90 days\"\n\nHowever, the Open Questions section (lines 793-794) lists these same values as \"proposed\" and unresolved:\n- \"Default grace period duration (proposed 24 hours, configurable per tenant)\"\n- \"Maximum backfill window (proposed 90 days)\"\n\nIf these defaults are confirmed, remove them from Open Questions. If they're still under discussion, update the requirements to reflect this uncertainty by using language like \"proposed default\" or \"to be determined.\"\n\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 314-314, 793-794\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` at line 285, The PRD currently\ncontradicts itself by stating concrete defaults (\"default 24 hours\" and \"default\n90 days\") in the functional requirements sentence containing \"The system MUST\naccept usage events...\" while the Open Questions section still lists those same\nvalues as \"proposed\"; update the document to be consistent by either (A)\nremoving the \"proposed\" entries from the Open Questions (lines referencing\n\"Default grace period duration (proposed 24 hours...)\" and \"Maximum backfill\nwindow (proposed 90 days)\") if the defaults are confirmed, or (B) changing the\nrequirement text that contains \"default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and\nper usage type\" and the \"default 90 days\" occurrence to prefix them with\n\"proposed default\" or \"to be determined\" so the requirements match the\nunresolved status in the Open Questions; ensure you modify the exact strings\n\"default 24 hours\" and \"default 90 days\" and the Open Questions entries so both\nplaces convey the same confirmed or proposed status.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:19Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:20Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184371","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184371"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184371"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184371/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":325,"original_line":285,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":285,"position":325,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184377","pull_request_review_id":3777110342,"id":2786184377,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEcy5","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n# First, let's find and examine the file\nfind . -name \"PRD.md\" -path \"*/usage_collector/*\" | head -5\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 116\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n# Once we locate the file, let's read the relevant sections\ncat -n \"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md\" | sed -n '280,330p'\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 3996\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n# Let's also search for any mention of atomicity, isolation, locking, or concurrent handling\nrg -n \"atomic|isolation|lock|concurrent|race|transaction\" \"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md\" -A 2 -B 2\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 10640\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n# Check for any sections about backfill handling and error recovery\nrg -n \"backfill|archive|real-time.*ingestion\" \"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md\" -C 3\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 9991\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n**Specify concurrent write handling semantics for backfill operations.**\n\nThe requirement states backfill must \"atomically archive and insert\" (line 294) and operate on a separate path from real-time ingestion (line 296), but does not explicitly define how real-time events are handled when they arrive during backfill for the same time range. This creates ambiguity around isolation guarantees and the risk of race conditions between the archive and insert phases.\n\nWithout explicit semantics, implementation teams must infer whether \"atomic\" implies transaction-level isolation, leading to potential inconsistencies. Recommend adding to the requirement one of these explicit strategies:\n1. **Reject real-time events**: Real-time ingestion returns specific error code for affected time range during active backfill; sources retry post-completion\n2. **Queue and replay**: Real-time events buffer during backfill, replay after completion\n3. **Transaction isolation**: Define specific isolation level (serializable, snapshot, etc.) ensuring archive + insert is atomic with concurrent writes\n4. **Backfill-first ordering**: Document that concurrent real-time events are deterministically ordered relative to backfill completion\n\nDocument the chosen approach in the requirement along with the corresponding API/SDK behavior during active backfill operations.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` around lines 291 - 300, Update\nthe fdd-uc-req-backfill-api requirement to explicitly state concurrent-write\nsemantics: pick one strategy (e.g., \"Reject real-time events\" with a defined\nerror code and retry guidance, or \"Queue and replay\" with buffering guarantees,\nor a specific transaction isolation level like \"snapshot\nisolation/serializable\", or \"Backfill-first deterministic ordering\") and\ndescribe exactly how real-time ingestion (the real-time path) must behave during\nan active backfill, including API/SDK behavior, error codes, retry/backoff\nexpectations, and how atomicity is achieved across the archive + insert phases\nreferenced in \"atomically archive and insert\". Ensure the text references\nfdd-uc-req-backfill-api and clarifies interaction with the real-time ingestion\npath so implementers know the required isolation/ordering model.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit 7411662","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:19Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T13:50:50Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184377","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184377"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184377"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184377/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":331,"original_start_line":291,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":345,"original_line":300,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":300,"position":345,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184384","pull_request_review_id":3777110342,"id":2786184384,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEczA","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Clarify relationship between event amendment and backfill archival.**\n\nThe document defines two correction mechanisms:\n1. Individual event amendment (lines 302-309): updating properties of specific events\n2. Backfill archival (lines 320-327): archiving replaced events during backfill\n\nHowever, the relationship between these mechanisms is unclear:\n- Can an amended event later be archived during a backfill operation?\n- If an event was previously amended, does backfill preserve the amendment history?\n- Are amendment and archival mutually exclusive states, or can they coexist?\n\nConsider adding clarification about how these mechanisms interact and whether the audit trail preserves both amendment and archival history for the same event.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` around lines 302 - 309, Update\nthe PRD to explicitly define how individual event amendments\n(fdd-uc-req-event-amendment) interact with backfill archival: state that an\namended event may later be archived during a backfill, that archival does not\ndelete amendment history but instead marks the original event as\n\"archived/replaced\" while retaining its amendment records and timestamps, and\nthat amendment and archival are not mutually exclusive states but sequential\nflags that can coexist (e.g., amended → archived); ensure the audit model\nrecords the full sequence (original event, amendment entries, archival entry\nwith backfill id and replacement mapping) and reference the actor\nfdd-uc-actor-platform-operator for permitted operations.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:19Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:21Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184384","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184384"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184384"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184384/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":347,"original_start_line":302,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":356,"original_line":309,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":309,"position":356,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184386","pull_request_review_id":3777110342,"id":2786184386,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEczC","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Reconsider SDK buffer overflow behavior for billing-critical data.**\n\nLine 420 specifies that when the SDK's in-memory buffer is full, it \"MUST drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics.\" While the requirement correctly mandates non-blocking behavior (line 419), silent data loss via dropped events conflicts with the system's exactly-once semantics guarantee (line 486-490) and billing accuracy goals.\n\nFor billing-critical usage data, dropped events translate directly to revenue loss. Emitting metrics about the loss doesn't prevent it and may not be noticed until after billing periods close.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Alternative approaches to consider</summary>\n\n1. **Backpressure signal**: Instead of dropping silently, expose a circuit breaker or callback to the calling service when the buffer reaches a high-water mark (e.g., 80% full), allowing the service to implement application-specific backpressure\n2. **Priority-aware dropping**: Drop only events marked as lower priority (analytics/monitoring) while preserving billing-critical events even if it requires blocking\n3. **Overflow to disk**: Spill to a bounded disk-based queue when memory buffer fills, with clear limits to prevent unbounded disk growth\n4. **Fail-fast**: Return an error to the caller when the buffer is full (for billing-critical events only), making data loss explicit rather than silent\n\nDocument the chosen strategy and specify which approach applies to billing-critical vs. analytics events.\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` around lines 416 - 423, Update\nrequirement fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry to avoid silent loss of billing-critical events\nby replacing the blanket \"drop oldest events\" behavior with a defined overflow\nstrategy: preserve billing-critical events (exactly-once semantics) via one or\nmore of these options—priority-aware dropping (evict only low-priority events),\nbounded disk spillover, or explicit backpressure/fail-fast for billing\nevents—and ensure the SDK exposes a backpressure/circuit-breaker callback and\nmetric/error signals when high-water marks are reached; document which approach\napplies to billing vs analytics events and how Retry-After, exponential backoff\nwith jitter, and metric reporting interact with the chosen overflow policy.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit 7411662","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:19Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T13:50:57Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184386","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184386"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184386"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184386/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":472,"original_start_line":416,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":479,"original_line":423,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":423,"position":479,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184389","pull_request_review_id":3777110342,"id":2786184389,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEczF","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Priority-Based Load Shedding\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-load-shedding`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support priority classification of usage event types (e.g., billing-critical counters vs. analytics metrics) and, when operating under sustained overload beyond rate limits, **MUST** preferentially accept higher-priority events while shedding lower-priority ones. The priority classification **MUST** be configurable per usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Under extreme load, indiscriminate rejection causes billing-critical data loss. Priority-based load shedding ensures that the most business-critical usage data (which affects revenue accuracy) is preserved even when the system cannot accept all traffic.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Observability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-observability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-tenant and per-source rate limit consumption as metrics (current usage vs. limit, rejection counts, throttle duration) for operator dashboards. The system **MUST** emit alerts when tenants approach configured warning thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90% of capacity).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operators need visibility into rate limit utilization to proactively adjust limits before tenants experience rejections. Approaching-limit alerts enable capacity planning.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### High Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-availability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** maintain 99.95% monthly availability for usage collection endpoints.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 99.95% uptime per calendar month\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection is on the critical path for all billable operations.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Throughput\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-throughput`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support ingestion of at least 100,000 usage records per minute under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: >= 100,000 records/minute sustained\n+**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-ingestion-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage record ingestion within 200ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 200ms\n+**Rationale**: Low ingestion latency prevents blocking in usage source services.\n+\n+#### Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage queries for 30-day ranges within 500ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 500ms for 30-day range queries\n+**Rationale**: Billing and quota enforcement systems require fast query responses.\n+\n+#### Exactly-Once Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-exactly-once`\n+\n+The system **MUST** guarantee exactly-once processing; zero usage records lost or duplicated under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss or duplication under normal operation\n+**Rationale**: Duplicate or missing records directly impact billing accuracy.\n+\n+#### Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce complete tenant isolation at API, storage, and query layers; no cross-tenant data leakage under any circumstance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data access\n+**Rationale**: Tenant isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** preserve immutable audit records for all usage data including source, timestamps, and any corrections.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of usage operations audited\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for billing disputes and compliance.\n+\n+#### Authentication Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authentication`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require authentication (OAuth 2.0, mTLS, or API key) for all API operations.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthenticated API access\n+**Rationale**: Usage data is sensitive and must be protected.\n+\n+#### Authorization Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce authorization for read/write operations based on tenant context and usage type permissions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized data access\n+**Rationale**: Authorization prevents unauthorized usage data manipulation.\n+\n+#### Horizontal Scalability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-scalability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** scale horizontally to handle increased load without architectural changes.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Linear throughput scaling with added instances\n+**Rationale**: Usage volume grows with platform adoption.\n+\n+#### Storage Fault Tolerance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-fault-tolerance`\n+\n+The system **MUST** buffer usage records during storage backend failures and recover without data loss.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss during storage backend failures\n+**Rationale**: Storage outages must not result in lost usage data.\n+\n+#### Configurable Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support retention periods from 7 days to 7 years depending on usage type and compliance requirements.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Configurable retention from 7 days to 7 years\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different compliance and operational retention needs.\n+\n+#### Graceful Degradation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-graceful-degradation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** continue accepting usage records even if downstream consumers (billing, monitoring) are unavailable.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero ingestion failures due to downstream consumer unavailability\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection must not be blocked by consumer outages.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Usage Ingestion SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-sdk`\n+\n+**Type**: Client library\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Client-side SDK with automatic batching for high-throughput usage emission. Primary ingestion path for platform services.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Usage REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: HTTP API for usage record ingestion (low-volume/external), querying, and administration (unit registration, retention configuration).\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Collector Agent\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-collector-agent`\n+\n+**Type**: Sidecar/agent process\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Sidecar deployment pattern for collecting usage from co-located services without SDK integration.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### Storage Adapter Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-storage-adapter`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Rust trait / plugin interface\n+**Compatibility**: Backward-compatible within major version; adapters implement a defined trait for read/write operations.\n+\n+#### Types Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-types-registry`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Internal API\n+**Compatibility**: Schema validation contract; UC depends on Types Registry for unit and type definitions.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+#### UC: High-Volume Usage Emission via SDK","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Fix markdown heading structure for use cases.**\n\nAll use case headings (lines 614, 636, 654, 672, 687, 707, 732) are using h4 (`####`) under the h2 section heading (`## 8. Use Cases`), skipping the h3 level. Markdown best practices require headings to increment by only one level at a time.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\nChange all use case headings from h4 to h3:\n\n```diff\n-#### UC: High-Volume Usage Emission via SDK\n+### UC: High-Volume Usage Emission via SDK\n\n-#### UC: Configure Custom Measuring Unit\n+### UC: Configure Custom Measuring Unit\n\n-#### UC: Query Tenant Usage for Billing\n+### UC: Query Tenant Usage for Billing\n\n-#### UC: Real-Time Quota Enforcement\n+### UC: Real-Time Quota Enforcement\n\n-#### UC: Add New Storage Backend\n+### UC: Add New Storage Backend\n\n-#### UC: Backfill Usage After Outage\n+### UC: Backfill Usage After Outage\n\n-#### UC: View Tenant Usage Report\n+### UC: View Tenant Usage Report\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 636-636, 654-654, 672-672, 687-687, 707-707, 732-732\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 markdownlint-cli2 (0.20.0)</summary>\n\n[warning] 614-614: Heading levels should only increment by one level at a time\nExpected: h3; Actual: h4\n\n(MD001, heading-increment)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` at line 614, Update the Markdown\nheading levels for the use case entries so they increment correctly under the\n\"## 8. Use Cases\" section: replace the h4 headings (####) for each use case\ntitle such as \"UC: High-Volume Usage Emission via SDK\" and the other listed use\ncase headings with h3 headings (###) so they sit directly beneath the h2\nsection; ensure all occurrences mentioned (the headings at lines referenced in\nthe review) are changed consistently from #### to ###.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit c471acf","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:19Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T08:07:59Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184389","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184389"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184389"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184389/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":614,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":614,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184395","pull_request_review_id":3777110342,"id":2786184395,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEczL","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Priority-Based Load Shedding\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-load-shedding`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support priority classification of usage event types (e.g., billing-critical counters vs. analytics metrics) and, when operating under sustained overload beyond rate limits, **MUST** preferentially accept higher-priority events while shedding lower-priority ones. The priority classification **MUST** be configurable per usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Under extreme load, indiscriminate rejection causes billing-critical data loss. Priority-based load shedding ensures that the most business-critical usage data (which affects revenue accuracy) is preserved even when the system cannot accept all traffic.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Observability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-observability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-tenant and per-source rate limit consumption as metrics (current usage vs. limit, rejection counts, throttle duration) for operator dashboards. The system **MUST** emit alerts when tenants approach configured warning thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90% of capacity).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operators need visibility into rate limit utilization to proactively adjust limits before tenants experience rejections. Approaching-limit alerts enable capacity planning.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### High Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-availability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** maintain 99.95% monthly availability for usage collection endpoints.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 99.95% uptime per calendar month\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection is on the critical path for all billable operations.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Throughput\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-throughput`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support ingestion of at least 100,000 usage records per minute under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: >= 100,000 records/minute sustained\n+**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-ingestion-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage record ingestion within 200ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 200ms\n+**Rationale**: Low ingestion latency prevents blocking in usage source services.\n+\n+#### Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage queries for 30-day ranges within 500ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 500ms for 30-day range queries\n+**Rationale**: Billing and quota enforcement systems require fast query responses.\n+\n+#### Exactly-Once Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-exactly-once`\n+\n+The system **MUST** guarantee exactly-once processing; zero usage records lost or duplicated under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss or duplication under normal operation\n+**Rationale**: Duplicate or missing records directly impact billing accuracy.\n+\n+#### Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce complete tenant isolation at API, storage, and query layers; no cross-tenant data leakage under any circumstance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data access\n+**Rationale**: Tenant isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** preserve immutable audit records for all usage data including source, timestamps, and any corrections.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of usage operations audited\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for billing disputes and compliance.\n+\n+#### Authentication Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authentication`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require authentication (OAuth 2.0, mTLS, or API key) for all API operations.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthenticated API access\n+**Rationale**: Usage data is sensitive and must be protected.\n+\n+#### Authorization Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce authorization for read/write operations based on tenant context and usage type permissions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized data access\n+**Rationale**: Authorization prevents unauthorized usage data manipulation.\n+\n+#### Horizontal Scalability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-scalability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** scale horizontally to handle increased load without architectural changes.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Linear throughput scaling with added instances\n+**Rationale**: Usage volume grows with platform adoption.\n+\n+#### Storage Fault Tolerance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-fault-tolerance`\n+\n+The system **MUST** buffer usage records during storage backend failures and recover without data loss.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss during storage backend failures\n+**Rationale**: Storage outages must not result in lost usage data.\n+\n+#### Configurable Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support retention periods from 7 days to 7 years depending on usage type and compliance requirements.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Configurable retention from 7 days to 7 years\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different compliance and operational retention needs.\n+\n+#### Graceful Degradation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-graceful-degradation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** continue accepting usage records even if downstream consumers (billing, monitoring) are unavailable.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero ingestion failures due to downstream consumer unavailability\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection must not be blocked by consumer outages.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Usage Ingestion SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-sdk`\n+\n+**Type**: Client library\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Client-side SDK with automatic batching for high-throughput usage emission. Primary ingestion path for platform services.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Usage REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: HTTP API for usage record ingestion (low-volume/external), querying, and administration (unit registration, retention configuration).\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Collector Agent\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-collector-agent`\n+\n+**Type**: Sidecar/agent process\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Sidecar deployment pattern for collecting usage from co-located services without SDK integration.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### Storage Adapter Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-storage-adapter`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Rust trait / plugin interface\n+**Compatibility**: Backward-compatible within major version; adapters implement a defined trait for read/write operations.\n+\n+#### Types Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-types-registry`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Internal API\n+**Compatibility**: Schema validation contract; UC depends on Types Registry for unit and type definitions.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+#### UC: High-Volume Usage Emission via SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-emission`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Usage type registered in Types Registry\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Service calls SDK to record usage event\n+2. SDK queues event in memory\n+3. SDK batches events (by count or time threshold)\n+4. SDK sends batch to UC\n+5. UC validates each record against type schema\n+6. UC persists to configured storage backend\n+7. SDK receives acknowledgment\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Usage records persisted with tenant/resource attribution; available for query and aggregation\n+\n+#### UC: Configure Custom Measuring Unit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-unit`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Unit name is unique\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Operator defines unit schema (name, type: counter/gauge, base unit)\n+2. Operator submits via API\n+3. UC validates unit definition\n+4. UC registers unit with Types Registry\n+5. UC confirms registration\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- New unit immediately available for usage collection; sources can emit records with new unit type\n+\n+#### UC: Query Tenant Usage for Billing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-billing-query`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Aggregation window closed\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Billing System queries usage API for billing period\n+2. UC retrieves aggregated usage for tenant and time window\n+3. UC returns usage grouped by usage type\n+4. Billing System processes for rating\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Billing System has accurate, deduplicated usage data for invoice generation\n+\n+#### UC: Real-Time Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Quota Enforcement System queries UC API for current usage\n+2. UC returns aggregated usage for tenant and usage type\n+3. Quota Enforcement System compares against tenant limits\n+4. Quota Enforcement System triggers enforcement if threshold exceeded\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Tenant usage enforced before exceeding quota; no over-consumption\n+\n+#### UC: Add New Storage Backend\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-add-storage`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Storage adapter plugin implemented\n+- Backend accessible\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Operator deploys adapter plugin\n+2. Operator configures adapter connection in UC\n+3. Operator defines routing rules (which usage types to which backend)\n+4. UC discovers and validates adapter\n+5. UC begins routing matching usage types to new backend\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- New storage backend active; usage routed per configuration; existing data unaffected\n+\n+#### UC: Backfill Usage After Outage\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-after-outage`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Gap detected via reconciliation (missing heartbeats, stalled watermarks, or count mismatch)\n+- Replacement usage data available from secondary source (infrastructure metrics, API gateway logs, or source service replay)\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Reconciliation system detects gap and alerts operator with affected time range, tenant(s), and usage type(s)\n+2. Operator prepares replacement events from secondary source\n+3. Operator submits backfill request specifying time range, tenant, and replacement events\n+4. UC validates time range is within backfill window\n+5. UC checks if time range overlaps with an already-invoiced period; if so, requires explicit operator confirmation\n+6. UC archives existing events in the time range (if any)\n+7. UC validates and persists replacement events with backfill idempotency namespace\n+8. UC creates audit record for the backfill operation\n+9. UC re-aggregates affected time windows\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Gap filled with corrected data; archived events retained for audit; aggregations updated; downstream consumers can query corrected data\n+","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Define mechanism for tracking already-invoiced periods.**\n\nLine 722 states \"UC checks if time range overlaps with an already-invoiced period; if so, requires explicit operator confirmation,\" but the document doesn't specify how the Usage Collector knows which periods have been invoiced.\n\nThis requires either:\n1. Integration with the Billing System to receive invoiced period notifications\n2. Explicit operator marking of billing periods as \"closed\" via an admin API\n3. A separate Billing Period Registry service\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Recommended additions</summary>\n\nAdd a functional requirement for tracking invoiced/closed billing periods:\n- Define the contract between UC and the Billing System for period closure notifications\n- Specify whether this is push (Billing System notifies UC) or pull (UC queries Billing System)\n- Add to section 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n- Update Dependencies (section 10) to reflect this integration\n\nConsider adding a UC requirement like:\n```markdown\n#### Billing Period Closure Tracking\n- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-billing-period-tracking`\n\nThe system **MUST** track which billing periods have been closed/invoiced to prevent unacknowledged backfill operations on finalized periods.\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` around lines 707 - 730, Add a\nclear functional requirement and integration contract for tracking\ninvoiced/closed billing periods so the Usage Collector can detect overlap with\ninvoiced windows: add a new requirement \"Billing Period Closure Tracking\" with\nID fdd-uc-req-billing-period-tracking that mandates the system MUST track\nclosed/invoiced billing periods and prohibit unacknowledged backfills on them;\ndescribe supported mechanisms (Billing System push notifications, UC pull API,\nor operator/admin API/Registry) and specify the contract details (API\nendpoints/events, auth, idempotency, SLA/latency, and reconciliation semantics)\nand whether notifications are push or pull; update section 7.2 External\nIntegration Contracts to include the Billing System contract and update\nDependencies (section 10) to list the Billing System or Billing Period Registry,\nand reference this requirement from the \"Backfill Usage After Outage\" flow where\n\"UC checks if time range overlaps with an already-invoiced period.\"\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:19Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:21Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184395","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184395"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184395"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184395/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":707,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":730,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":730,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184397","pull_request_review_id":3777110342,"id":2786184397,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEczN","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Priority-Based Load Shedding\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-load-shedding`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support priority classification of usage event types (e.g., billing-critical counters vs. analytics metrics) and, when operating under sustained overload beyond rate limits, **MUST** preferentially accept higher-priority events while shedding lower-priority ones. The priority classification **MUST** be configurable per usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Under extreme load, indiscriminate rejection causes billing-critical data loss. Priority-based load shedding ensures that the most business-critical usage data (which affects revenue accuracy) is preserved even when the system cannot accept all traffic.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Observability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-observability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-tenant and per-source rate limit consumption as metrics (current usage vs. limit, rejection counts, throttle duration) for operator dashboards. The system **MUST** emit alerts when tenants approach configured warning thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90% of capacity).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operators need visibility into rate limit utilization to proactively adjust limits before tenants experience rejections. Approaching-limit alerts enable capacity planning.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### High Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-availability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** maintain 99.95% monthly availability for usage collection endpoints.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 99.95% uptime per calendar month\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection is on the critical path for all billable operations.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Throughput\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-throughput`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support ingestion of at least 100,000 usage records per minute under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: >= 100,000 records/minute sustained\n+**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-ingestion-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage record ingestion within 200ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 200ms\n+**Rationale**: Low ingestion latency prevents blocking in usage source services.\n+\n+#### Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage queries for 30-day ranges within 500ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 500ms for 30-day range queries\n+**Rationale**: Billing and quota enforcement systems require fast query responses.\n+\n+#### Exactly-Once Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-exactly-once`\n+\n+The system **MUST** guarantee exactly-once processing; zero usage records lost or duplicated under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss or duplication under normal operation\n+**Rationale**: Duplicate or missing records directly impact billing accuracy.\n+\n+#### Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce complete tenant isolation at API, storage, and query layers; no cross-tenant data leakage under any circumstance.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data access\n+**Rationale**: Tenant isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** preserve immutable audit records for all usage data including source, timestamps, and any corrections.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of usage operations audited\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for billing disputes and compliance.\n+\n+#### Authentication Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authentication`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require authentication (OAuth 2.0, mTLS, or API key) for all API operations.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthenticated API access\n+**Rationale**: Usage data is sensitive and must be protected.\n+\n+#### Authorization Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce authorization for read/write operations based on tenant context and usage type permissions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized data access\n+**Rationale**: Authorization prevents unauthorized usage data manipulation.\n+\n+#### Horizontal Scalability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-scalability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** scale horizontally to handle increased load without architectural changes.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Linear throughput scaling with added instances\n+**Rationale**: Usage volume grows with platform adoption.\n+\n+#### Storage Fault Tolerance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-fault-tolerance`\n+\n+The system **MUST** buffer usage records during storage backend failures and recover without data loss.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss during storage backend failures\n+**Rationale**: Storage outages must not result in lost usage data.\n+\n+#### Configurable Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support retention periods from 7 days to 7 years depending on usage type and compliance requirements.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Configurable retention from 7 days to 7 years\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different compliance and operational retention needs.\n+\n+#### Graceful Degradation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-graceful-degradation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** continue accepting usage records even if downstream consumers (billing, monitoring) are unavailable.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero ingestion failures due to downstream consumer unavailability\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection must not be blocked by consumer outages.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Usage Ingestion SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-sdk`\n+\n+**Type**: Client library\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Client-side SDK with automatic batching for high-throughput usage emission. Primary ingestion path for platform services.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Usage REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: HTTP API for usage record ingestion (low-volume/external), querying, and administration (unit registration, retention configuration).\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Collector Agent\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-collector-agent`\n+\n+**Type**: Sidecar/agent process\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Sidecar deployment pattern for collecting usage from co-located services without SDK integration.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### Storage Adapter Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-storage-adapter`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Rust trait / plugin interface\n+**Compatibility**: Backward-compatible within major version; adapters implement a defined trait for read/write operations.\n+\n+#### Types Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-types-registry`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Internal API\n+**Compatibility**: Schema validation contract; UC depends on Types Registry for unit and type definitions.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+#### UC: High-Volume Usage Emission via SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-emission`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Usage type registered in Types Registry\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Service calls SDK to record usage event\n+2. SDK queues event in memory\n+3. SDK batches events (by count or time threshold)\n+4. SDK sends batch to UC\n+5. UC validates each record against type schema\n+6. UC persists to configured storage backend\n+7. SDK receives acknowledgment\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Usage records persisted with tenant/resource attribution; available for query and aggregation\n+\n+#### UC: Configure Custom Measuring Unit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-unit`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Unit name is unique\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Operator defines unit schema (name, type: counter/gauge, base unit)\n+2. Operator submits via API\n+3. UC validates unit definition\n+4. UC registers unit with Types Registry\n+5. UC confirms registration\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- New unit immediately available for usage collection; sources can emit records with new unit type\n+\n+#### UC: Query Tenant Usage for Billing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-billing-query`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Aggregation window closed\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Billing System queries usage API for billing period\n+2. UC retrieves aggregated usage for tenant and time window\n+3. UC returns usage grouped by usage type\n+4. Billing System processes for rating\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Billing System has accurate, deduplicated usage data for invoice generation\n+\n+#### UC: Real-Time Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Quota Enforcement System queries UC API for current usage\n+2. UC returns aggregated usage for tenant and usage type\n+3. Quota Enforcement System compares against tenant limits\n+4. Quota Enforcement System triggers enforcement if threshold exceeded\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Tenant usage enforced before exceeding quota; no over-consumption\n+\n+#### UC: Add New Storage Backend\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-add-storage`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Storage adapter plugin implemented\n+- Backend accessible\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Operator deploys adapter plugin\n+2. Operator configures adapter connection in UC\n+3. Operator defines routing rules (which usage types to which backend)\n+4. UC discovers and validates adapter\n+5. UC begins routing matching usage types to new backend\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- New storage backend active; usage routed per configuration; existing data unaffected\n+\n+#### UC: Backfill Usage After Outage\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-after-outage`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Gap detected via reconciliation (missing heartbeats, stalled watermarks, or count mismatch)\n+- Replacement usage data available from secondary source (infrastructure metrics, API gateway logs, or source service replay)\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Reconciliation system detects gap and alerts operator with affected time range, tenant(s), and usage type(s)\n+2. Operator prepares replacement events from secondary source\n+3. Operator submits backfill request specifying time range, tenant, and replacement events\n+4. UC validates time range is within backfill window\n+5. UC checks if time range overlaps with an already-invoiced period; if so, requires explicit operator confirmation\n+6. UC archives existing events in the time range (if any)\n+7. UC validates and persists replacement events with backfill idempotency namespace\n+8. UC creates audit record for the backfill operation\n+9. UC re-aggregates affected time windows\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Gap filled with corrected data; archived events retained for audit; aggregations updated; downstream consumers can query corrected data\n+\n+#### UC: View Tenant Usage Report\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-report`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Administrator requests usage for time period\n+2. UC queries aggregated usage for tenant only\n+4. UC returns usage breakdown by type and resource\n+5. Administrator reviews consumption","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Fix step numbering in use case.**\n\nThe main flow skips from step 2 to step 4, missing step 3. This appears to be a simple numbering error.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n 1. Administrator requests usage for time period\n 2. UC queries aggregated usage for tenant only\n-4. UC returns usage breakdown by type and resource\n-5. Administrator reviews consumption\n+3. UC returns usage breakdown by type and resource\n+4. Administrator reviews consumption\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` around lines 737 - 741, In the\nPRD.md \"Main Flow\" section rename the bullet numbers so they are sequential (no\nskip): make the list read 1. Administrator requests usage for time period, 2. UC\nqueries aggregated usage for tenant only, 3. UC returns usage breakdown by type\nand resource, 4. Administrator reviews consumption (i.e., decrement the current\n\"4\" to \"3\" and \"5\" to \"4\"). Update the numbered items under the \"Main Flow\"\nheader accordingly so there is no missing step number.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit c471acf","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:19Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T08:08:02Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184397","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184397"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184397"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184397/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":737,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":741,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":741,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184973","pull_request_review_id":3777110966,"id":2786184973,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mEc8N","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Pull-based collection**: P2 future capability; adapters built on-demand for specific source types that require polling integration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Priority-Based Load Shedding\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-load-shedding`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support priority classification of usage event types (e.g., billing-critical counters vs. analytics metrics) and, when operating under sustained overload beyond rate limits, **MUST** preferentially accept higher-priority events while shedding lower-priority ones. The priority classification **MUST** be configurable per usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Under extreme load, indiscriminate rejection causes billing-critical data loss. Priority-based load shedding ensures that the most business-critical usage data (which affects revenue accuracy) is preserved even when the system cannot accept all traffic.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Observability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-observability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-tenant and per-source rate limit consumption as metrics (current usage vs. limit, rejection counts, throttle duration) for operator dashboards. The system **MUST** emit alerts when tenants approach configured warning thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90% of capacity).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operators need visibility into rate limit utilization to proactively adjust limits before tenants experience rejections. Approaching-limit alerts enable capacity planning.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### High Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-availability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** maintain 99.95% monthly availability for usage collection endpoints.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 99.95% uptime per calendar month\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection is on the critical path for all billable operations.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Throughput\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-throughput`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support ingestion of at least 100,000 usage records per minute under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: >= 100,000 records/minute sustained\n+**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-ingestion-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage record ingestion within 200ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 200ms\n+**Rationale**: Low ingestion latency prevents blocking in usage source services.\n+\n+#### Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage queries for 30-day ranges within 500ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 500ms for 30-day range queries\n+**Rationale**: Billing and quota enforcement systems require fast query responses.\n+\n+#### Exactly-Once Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-exactly-once`\n+\n+The system **MUST** guarantee exactly-once processing; zero usage records lost or duplicated under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss or duplication under normal operation\n+**Rationale**: Duplicate or missing records directly impact billing accuracy.\n+\n+#### Tenant Data Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce complete tenant isolation at API, storage, and query layers; no cross-tenant data leakage under any circumstance.\n+","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","original_commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53","user":{"login":"qodo-code-review[bot]","id":151058649,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCQD42Q","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/484649?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/qodo-code-review","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"<img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/v2-action-required.svg\" height=\"20\" alt=\"Action required\">\n\n1\\. Duplicate fdd id <code>🐞 Bug</code> <code>✓ Correctness</code>\n\n<pre>\n• The PRD defines <b><i>fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation</i></b> twice (Functional Requirements and again under\n  NFRs), creating ambiguous traceability for any downstream DESIGN/ADR/feature references.\n• Repo guidance explicitly requires FDD IDs to be unique and notes validation will detect duplicate\n  definitions; this PRD would fail that check once tooling is enabled.\n</pre>\n\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Agent Prompt</strong></summary>\n\n```\n### Issue description\nThe PRD defines the FDD ID `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation` twice (once in Functional Requirements and once in Non-Functional Requirements). FDD IDs are required to be unique; duplicates create ambiguous traceability and will be flagged by validation tooling.\n\n### Issue Context\nFDD ID uniqueness is documented in `docs/spec-templates/README.md`.\n\n### Fix Focus Areas\n- modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md[221-228]\n- modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md[491-498]\n```\n\n<code>ⓘ Copy this prompt and use it to remediate the issue with your preferred AI generation tools</code>\n</details>","created_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:30Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:30Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184973","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184973"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786184973"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786184973/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":491,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":496,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"NONE","original_position":496,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786419419","pull_request_review_id":3777349797,"id":2786419419,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mFWLb","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,786 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Priority-Based Load Shedding\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-load-shedding`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support priority classification of usage event types (e.g., billing-critical counters vs. analytics metrics) and, when operating under sustained overload beyond rate limits, **MUST** preferentially accept higher-priority events while shedding lower-priority ones. The priority classification **MUST** be configurable per usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Under extreme load, indiscriminate rejection causes billing-critical data loss. Priority-based load shedding ensures that the most business-critical usage data (which affects revenue accuracy) is preserved even when the system cannot accept all traffic.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Observability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-observability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-tenant and per-source rate limit consumption as metrics (current usage vs. limit, rejection counts, throttle duration) for operator dashboards. The system **MUST** emit alerts when tenants approach configured warning thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90% of capacity).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operators need visibility into rate limit utilization to proactively adjust limits before tenants experience rejections. Approaching-limit alerts enable capacity planning.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### High Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-availability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** maintain 99.95% monthly availability for usage collection endpoints.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 99.95% uptime per calendar month\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection is on the critical path for all billable operations.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Throughput\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-throughput`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support ingestion of at least 100,000 usage records per minute under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: >= 100,000 records/minute sustained\n+**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"c471acf48bd6a8bc2c93081ce13dd24045d2446d","user":{"login":"graphite-app[bot]","id":96075541,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOBbn_FQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/158384?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/graphite-app","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Critical throughput inconsistency. The requirement specifies 100,000 records per minute (~1,667 events/sec), but the Business Goals (line 29) and Acceptance Criteria (line 741) both require 10,000+ events per second. This is a 6x discrepancy that will cause the system to fail its acceptance criteria.\n\n**Impact**: Implementation teams will build to the lower threshold (1,667 events/sec) and fail to meet the stated business goal of 10,000+ events/sec.\n\n**Fix**: Align the throughput requirement with the business goal:\n```markdown\nThe system **MUST** support ingestion of at least 10,000 usage records per second (600,000 records per minute) under normal operation.\n\n**Threshold**: >= 10,000 records/second sustained\n```\n```suggestion\n**Threshold**: >= 10,000 records/second sustained\n**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.\n```\n  \n\n*Spotted by [Graphite Agent](https://app.graphite.com/diamond/?org=cyberfabric&ref=ai-review-comment)*<i class='graphite__hidden'><br /><br /><a href=\"https://app.graphite.com/github/pr/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/536?chatWithGeneratedComment=c98bd2ca-3e12-406f-8bcf-6cfa18a60f2c\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Fix in Graphite\" src=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-dark.svg\"></picture></a></i><i class='graphite__hidden'><br /><br />Is this helpful? React 👍 or 👎 to let us know.</i>","created_at":"2026-02-10T08:10:16Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T08:10:18Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786419419","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786419419"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786419419"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786419419/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":516,"original_start_line":459,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":518,"original_line":461,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":461,"position":518,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786422415","pull_request_review_id":3777353097,"id":2786422415,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mFW6P","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,786 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","original_commit_id":"c471acf48bd6a8bc2c93081ce13dd24045d2446d","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Resolve conflicting throughput targets.**\n\nLine 155 sets a target of 10,000+ events/sec (~600k/min), while Line 456-460 requires only 100,000 records/min. These are materially inconsistent and will drive different architecture and capacity plans. Please align to a single target (or explicitly define “burst” vs “sustained” with numbers for both).  \n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 456-460\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` around lines 153 - 156, The PRD\ncurrently has conflicting throughput targets for the usage ingestion requirement\n`fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion` (one place says 10,000+ events/sec, another says\n100,000 records/min); update the document to pick a single clear throughput spec\nor explicitly define both “sustained” and “burst” rates (e.g., sustained X\nevents/sec and burst Y events/sec with duration and frequency), and apply the\nchosen numbers consistently wherever `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion` or the usage\ningestion throughput is referenced (including the later section that currently\nstates 100,000 records/min) so architecture and capacity planning use the same\ntarget.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:eagle -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-10T08:10:56Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T08:10:57Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786422415","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786422415"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786422415"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786422415/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":153,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":156,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":156,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786422424","pull_request_review_id":3777353097,"id":2786422424,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mFW6Y","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,786 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption.\n+","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"6b96400637b00f691ffb73d6d8b4c8e31614fd65","original_commit_id":"c471acf48bd6a8bc2c93081ce13dd24045d2446d","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>\n\n🌐 Web query:\n\n`HTTP Retry-After header standard format RFC specification`\n\n💡 Result:\n\nThe **HTTP `Retry-After` response header** is specified in **RFC 9110 (HTTP Semantics), Section 10.2.3**.\n\n**Standard field-value format (ABNF):**\n- `Retry-After = HTTP-date / delay-seconds`  \n- `delay-seconds = 1*DIGIT` (a non-negative decimal integer number of seconds) [1]\n\n**Examples (from the RFC):**\n- `Retry-After: Fri, 31 Dec 1999 23:59:59 GMT`\n- `Retry-After: 120` [1]\n\n(Older/obsoleted spec: **RFC 7231, Section 7.1.3** defines the same syntax.) [2]\n\n**Sources**\n[1] RFC 9110, §10.2.3 “Retry-After” https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110  \n[2] RFC 7231, §7.1.3 “Retry-After” https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7231\n\n---\n\n🌐 Web query:\n\n`X-RateLimit-Reset header standard format Unix epoch vs RFC date`\n\n💡 Result:\n\n`X-RateLimit-Reset` is **not standardized**, so there is **no single “correct” format**. Different APIs use different semantics (some use a Unix epoch timestamp; others use a relative number of seconds). GitHub, for example, documents `x-ratelimit-reset` as **UTC epoch seconds** (Unix timestamp) in its REST API docs and examples. [1], [2]\n\nIf you want a **standard** HTTP way to communicate rate-limit reset timing:\n\n- **`RateLimit-Reset` (standardized RateLimit fields)**: defined to be an **integer number of seconds until the quota resets** (a *delay-seconds* / delta-seconds style value), **not** an absolute date/time. [3]\n- **`Retry-After` (widely deployed standard header for 429/503)**: explicitly supports **either** an **HTTP-date (IMF-fixdate/RFC 7231 style)** **or** **delay-seconds**. [4]\n\n**So:** `X-RateLimit-Reset` (nonstandard) is commonly **Unix epoch seconds** in the wild (e.g., GitHub), while the standardized direction is **relative seconds** (`RateLimit-Reset` or `Retry-After: <delay-seconds>`), with `Retry-After` also allowing an RFC-style HTTP date. [1], [3], [4]\n\n**Sources:** [1] [2] [3] [4]\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n# Check if the PRD document specifies header formats elsewhere\ncd modules/system/usage_collector/docs && cat -n PRD.md | grep -i -A 5 -B 5 \"retry-after\\|ratelimit\\|rate-limit\" | head -100\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 6978\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n**Specify exact format and semantics for rate-limit headers.**\n\nThe PRD mandates `Retry-After`, `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers but does not specify their formats:\n- `Retry-After`: RFC 9110 permits either HTTP-date (e.g., `Fri, 31 Dec 1999 23:59:59 GMT`) or delay-seconds (integer); clarify which.\n- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: No standard format exists; specify whether it uses Unix epoch seconds, relative seconds, or HTTP-date.\n- `X-RateLimit-Limit` and `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: Clarify if these are integer counts or other units.\n\nWithout format clarity, client developers will be ambiguous about parsing and implementing retry logic.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` around lines 409 - 410, Update\nthe PRD text to explicitly define the formats and semantics for the four\nheaders: state that Retry-After MUST be provided as delay-seconds (integer\nseconds to wait) and MAY include an HTTP-date for backward compatibility,\nX-RateLimit-Reset MUST be a Unix epoch timestamp in UTC (integer seconds\nindicating the reset time), and X-RateLimit-Limit and X-RateLimit-Remaining MUST\nbe integer counts (total allowance and remaining tokens); also add a short note\nthat clients should parse these as integers and treat missing/invalid values\nconservatively and clarify that all times are in UTC.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:eagle -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-10T08:10:56Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T08:10:57Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786422424","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786422424"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2786422424"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2786422424/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":409,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":410,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":410,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788107163","pull_request_review_id":3779121283,"id":2788107163,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mLyOb","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,791 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","original_commit_id":"7411662652a0028fdb2b1287927362b7e2d682e2","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_🛠️ Refactor suggestion_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Specify error handling for missing or invalid security context.**\n\nThe requirement states that tenant attribution is \"derived from security context\" and must be \"immutable,\" but does not define the system's behavior when the security context is unavailable, malformed, or missing the tenant identifier.\n\nRecommend adding explicit error handling semantics:\n- If security context is missing or does not contain a valid tenant ID, the system **MUST** reject the request with HTTP 401 (Unauthorized) or 400 (Bad Request) with a specific error code\n- Document whether missing context is treated as an authentication failure (401) or a malformed request (400)\n- Clarify whether this applies to all ingestion paths (SDK, collector agent, REST API) or only certain ones\n\nThis ensures consistent behavior across all implementations and prevents ambiguous tenant attribution.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📋 Proposed addition to requirement</summary>\n\nAdd after line 207:\n\n```diff\n The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+If the security context is missing, malformed, or does not contain a valid tenant identifier, the system **MUST** reject the request with HTTP 400 (Bad Request) and error code `MISSING_TENANT_CONTEXT`, preventing records from being ingested without proper attribution.\n```\n</details>\n\n<!-- suggestion_start -->\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Committable suggestion</summary>\n\n> ‼️ **IMPORTANT**\n> Carefully review the code before committing. Ensure that it accurately replaces the highlighted code, contains no missing lines, and has no issues with indentation. Thoroughly test & benchmark the code to ensure it meets the requirements.\n\n```suggestion\nThe system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n\nIf the security context is missing, malformed, or does not contain a valid tenant identifier, the system **MUST** reject the request with HTTP 400 (Bad Request) and error code `MISSING_TENANT_CONTEXT`, preventing records from being ingested without proper attribution.\n\n**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- suggestion_end -->\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` around lines 206 - 209, Update\nthe PRD requirement \"attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from\nsecurity context\" to include explicit error-handling semantics: specify that\nwhen the security context is missing, malformed, or lacks a tenant ID the system\nMUST reject the request with a defined HTTP status (401 for authentication\nfailure or 400 for malformed request) and a documented machine-readable error\ncode; state whether missing tenant context is treated as authentication (401) or\nbad request (400); and clarify that this behaviour applies uniformly to all\ningestion paths (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Reference the requirement text\nand actor identifier fdd-uc-actor-usage-source when adding these clauses.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-10T13:55:34Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T13:55:36Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2788107163","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788107163"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2788107163"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788107163/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":206,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":209,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":209,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788281325","pull_request_review_id":3779315004,"id":2788281325,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mMcvt","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,786 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"c471acf48bd6a8bc2c93081ce13dd24045d2446d","user":{"login":"rymenar","id":288567,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI4ODU2Nw==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/288567?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rymenar","html_url":"https://github.com/rymenar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rymenar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rymenar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rymenar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rymenar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rymenar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rymenar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rymenar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rymenar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rymenar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"What about usage aggregation from sub-tenants, when higher-level tenant can collect aggregated usage from their sub-tenants (e.g. vendor aggregates usage from customers)? Should it be in scope (did not find such scenario) or out of scope and implemented by some other service?","created_at":"2026-02-10T14:26:22Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T14:26:22Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2788281325","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788281325"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2788281325"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788281325/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":117,"original_line":116,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"NONE","original_position":116,"position":117,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788773169","pull_request_review_id":3779875751,"id":2788773169,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mOU0x","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,792 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"59c81847aced9792e7622eb10ad6eef3085041da","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n#!/bin/bash\n# Search for all throughput-related specifications\nrg -n \"events.{0,20}(per|second|minute)|throughput|records.{0,20}(per|minute)\" modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md -A 1 -B 1\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 4220\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n#!/bin/bash\n# Extract exact lines mentioned in the review\necho \"=== Line 29 ===\"\nsed -n '29p' modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md\n\necho -e \"\\n=== Line 155 ===\"\nsed -n '155p' modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md\n\necho -e \"\\n=== Line 465 ===\"\nsed -n '465p' modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md\n\necho -e \"\\n=== Line 747 (Acceptance Criteria) ===\"\nsed -n '747p' modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md\n\n# Check git log to see if this was addressed\necho -e \"\\n=== Recent commits mentioning throughput or PRD ===\"\ngit log --oneline -20 -- modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 711\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n**Resolve conflicting throughput targets across goals and requirements.**\n\nThe document specifies inconsistent throughput targets:\n- Line 29 (Goals): \"10,000+ events per second\"\n- Line 155 (Functional Requirement): \"10,000+ events per second\"  \n- Line 465 (Non-Functional Requirement): \"100,000 usage records per minute\"\n\n10,000 events/sec equals 600,000 events/min, which is 6× higher than the NFR target of 100,000 records/min. This discrepancy will drive different architecture decisions, capacity planning, and infrastructure sizing.\n\nAlign all three locations to a single target, or explicitly define separate \"sustained\" and \"burst\" rates with specific values for each.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` at line 29, Update the\ninconsistent throughput numbers in the PRD: locate the \"Goals\" statement (line\nwith \"High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second\"), the \"Functional\nRequirement\" entry (the line stating \"10,000+ events per second\"), and the\n\"Non-Functional Requirement\" entry (the line \"100,000 usage records per minute\")\nand make them consistent by either (A) unifying all three to a single agreed\nthroughput value, or (B) explicitly defining two clear metrics — e.g.,\n\"sustained rate: X events/sec (Y events/min)\" and \"burst rate: Z events/sec (W\nevents/min)\" — and update each of the three locations to reference these terms\nand values consistently so capacity/planning decisions use the same numbers.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-10T15:49:57Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T15:49:58Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2788773169","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788773169"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2788773169"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788773169/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":29,"original_line":29,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":29,"position":29,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788773187","pull_request_review_id":3779875751,"id":2788773187,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mOU1D","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,792 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range (scoped to a single tenant and usage type) and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Concurrent-write strategy — Reject and Retry**: During an active backfill, the system **MUST** use range-level locking to enforce exclusive write access to the affected `(tenant, usage_type, time_range)` partition. The backfill operation acquires a lock before beginning the archive-and-insert transaction and holds it until the transaction commits or rolls back. While the lock is held:\n+\n+1. **Real-time ingestion behavior**: Any real-time event (via SDK, collector agent, or REST API as defined in `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`) whose `(tenant_id, usage_type, timestamp)` falls within a currently locked backfill range **MUST** be rejected with HTTP status `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_IN_PROGRESS`. The response body **MUST** include the fields `retry_after_ms` (estimated remaining backfill duration, minimum 1000) and `locked_range` (the `[start, end)` interval that is locked).\n+2. **Lock scope and overlap**: The system **MUST** reject a backfill request with HTTP `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_RANGE_OVERLAP` if its requested range overlaps with any currently locked backfill range for the same tenant and usage type. Only one backfill operation per `(tenant, usage_type)` overlapping range is permitted at a time. Non-overlapping ranges for the same tenant and usage type, or any ranges for different tenants or usage types, **MAY** execute concurrently.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. The reject-and-retry strategy is chosen over queue-and-replay or snapshot isolation alternatives because it is the simplest model to implement correctly, avoids unbounded buffering on the server, keeps real-time ingestion latency deterministic (a fast 409 vs. an indeterminate queue wait), and pushes retry responsibility to the SDK where backpressure is already handled. Backfill operations are expected to be infrequent (operator-initiated corrections), so brief real-time rejection windows are an acceptable trade-off for strong atomicity guarantees. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Interaction with backfill**: If a backfill operation targets a time range that contains previously amended events, the backfill **MUST** archive the amended events along with all other events in that range. Amendment history is not preserved — the backfill's replacement events become the sole active record. This means backfill unconditionally supersedes any prior amendments within its range, keeping the correction model simple: amendments are for surgical fixes to individual events, while backfill is a wholesale replacement that starts from a clean slate.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Priority-Based Load Shedding\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-load-shedding`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support priority classification of usage event types (e.g., billing-critical counters vs. analytics metrics) and, when operating under sustained overload beyond rate limits, **MUST** preferentially accept higher-priority events while shedding lower-priority ones. The priority classification **MUST** be configurable per usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Under extreme load, indiscriminate rejection causes billing-critical data loss. Priority-based load shedding ensures that the most business-critical usage data (which affects revenue accuracy) is preserved even when the system cannot accept all traffic.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Observability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-observability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-tenant and per-source rate limit consumption as metrics (current usage vs. limit, rejection counts, throttle duration) for operator dashboards. The system **MUST** emit alerts when tenants approach configured warning thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90% of capacity).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operators need visibility into rate limit utilization to proactively adjust limits before tenants experience rejections. Approaching-limit alerts enable capacity planning.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### High Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-availability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** maintain 99.95% monthly availability for usage collection endpoints.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 99.95% uptime per calendar month\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection is on the critical path for all billable operations.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Throughput\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-throughput`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support ingestion of at least 100,000 usage records per minute under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: >= 100,000 records/minute sustained\n+**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-ingestion-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage record ingestion within 200ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 200ms\n+**Rationale**: Low ingestion latency prevents blocking in usage source services.\n+\n+#### Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage queries for 30-day ranges within 500ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 500ms for 30-day range queries\n+**Rationale**: Billing and quota enforcement systems require fast query responses.\n+\n+#### Exactly-Once Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-exactly-once`\n+\n+The system **MUST** guarantee exactly-once processing; zero usage records lost or duplicated under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss or duplication under normal operation\n+**Rationale**: Duplicate or missing records directly impact billing accuracy.\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** preserve immutable audit records for all usage data including source, timestamps, and any corrections.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of usage operations audited\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for billing disputes and compliance.\n+\n+#### Authentication Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authentication`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require authentication (OAuth 2.0, mTLS, or API key) for all API operations.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthenticated API access\n+**Rationale**: Usage data is sensitive and must be protected.\n+\n+#### Authorization Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce authorization for read/write operations based on tenant context and usage type permissions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized data access\n+**Rationale**: Authorization prevents unauthorized usage data manipulation.\n+\n+#### Horizontal Scalability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-scalability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** scale horizontally to handle increased load without architectural changes.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Linear throughput scaling with added instances\n+**Rationale**: Usage volume grows with platform adoption.\n+\n+#### Storage Fault Tolerance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-fault-tolerance`\n+\n+The system **MUST** buffer usage records during storage backend failures and recover without data loss.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss during storage backend failures\n+**Rationale**: Storage outages must not result in lost usage data.\n+\n+#### Configurable Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support retention periods from 7 days to 7 years depending on usage type and compliance requirements.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Configurable retention from 7 days to 7 years\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different compliance and operational retention needs.\n+\n+#### Graceful Degradation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-graceful-degradation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** continue accepting usage records even if downstream consumers (billing, monitoring) are unavailable.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero ingestion failures due to downstream consumer unavailability\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection must not be blocked by consumer outages.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Usage Ingestion SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-sdk`\n+\n+**Type**: Client library\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Client-side SDK with automatic batching for high-throughput usage emission. Primary ingestion path for platform services.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Usage REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: HTTP API for usage record ingestion (low-volume/external), querying, and administration (unit registration, retention configuration).\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Collector Agent\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-collector-agent`\n+\n+**Type**: Sidecar/agent process\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Sidecar deployment pattern for collecting usage from co-located services without SDK integration.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### Storage Adapter Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-storage-adapter`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Rust trait / plugin interface\n+**Compatibility**: Backward-compatible within major version; adapters implement a defined trait for read/write operations.\n+\n+#### Types Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-types-registry`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Internal API\n+**Compatibility**: Schema validation contract; UC depends on Types Registry for unit and type definitions.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC: High-Volume Usage Emission via SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-emission`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Usage type registered in Types Registry\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Service calls SDK to record usage event\n+2. SDK queues event in memory\n+3. SDK batches events (by count or time threshold)\n+4. SDK sends batch to UC\n+5. UC validates each record against type schema\n+6. UC persists to configured storage backend\n+7. SDK receives acknowledgment\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Usage records persisted with tenant/resource attribution; available for query and aggregation\n+\n+### UC: Configure Custom Measuring Unit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-unit`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Unit name is unique\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Operator defines unit schema (name, type: counter/gauge, base unit)\n+2. Operator submits via API\n+3. UC validates unit definition\n+4. UC registers unit with Types Registry\n+5. UC confirms registration\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- New unit immediately available for usage collection; sources can emit records with new unit type\n+\n+### UC: Query Tenant Usage for Billing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-billing-query`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Aggregation window closed\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Billing System queries usage API for billing period\n+2. UC retrieves aggregated usage for tenant and time window\n+3. UC returns usage grouped by usage type\n+4. Billing System processes for rating\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Billing System has accurate, deduplicated usage data for invoice generation\n+\n+### UC: Real-Time Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Quota Enforcement System queries UC API for current usage\n+2. UC returns aggregated usage for tenant and usage type\n+3. Quota Enforcement System compares against tenant limits\n+4. Quota Enforcement System triggers enforcement if threshold exceeded\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Tenant usage enforced before exceeding quota; no over-consumption\n+\n+### UC: Add New Storage Backend\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-add-storage`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Storage adapter plugin implemented\n+- Backend accessible\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Operator deploys adapter plugin\n+2. Operator configures adapter connection in UC\n+3. Operator defines routing rules (which usage types to which backend)\n+4. UC discovers and validates adapter\n+5. UC begins routing matching usage types to new backend\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- New storage backend active; usage routed per configuration; existing data unaffected\n+\n+### UC: Backfill Usage After Outage\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-after-outage`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Gap detected via reconciliation (missing heartbeats, stalled watermarks, or count mismatch)\n+- Replacement usage data available from secondary source (infrastructure metrics, API gateway logs, or source service replay)\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Reconciliation system detects gap and alerts operator with affected time range, tenant(s), and usage type(s)\n+2. Operator prepares replacement events from secondary source\n+3. Operator submits backfill request specifying time range, tenant, and replacement events\n+4. UC validates time range is within backfill window\n+5. UC archives existing events in the time range (if any)\n+6. UC validates and persists replacement events with backfill idempotency namespace\n+7. UC creates audit record for the backfill operation\n+8. UC re-aggregates affected time windows\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Gap filled with corrected data; archived events retained for audit; aggregations updated; downstream consumers can query corrected data\n+\n+### UC: View Tenant Usage Report\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-report`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Administrator requests usage for time period\n+2. UC queries aggregated usage for tenant only\n+3. UC returns usage breakdown by type and resource\n+4. Administrator reviews consumption\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Administrator sees only their tenant's usage; no cross-tenant data exposure\n+\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n+\n+- [ ] All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- [ ] Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- [ ] Custom unit registration completes in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- [ ] High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- [ ] 99.95%+ monthly availability maintained\n+- [ ] Backfill API can restore missing usage data for any time range within the backfill window\n+- [ ] Reconciliation detects usage data gaps within one heartbeat interval\n+- [ ] Zero data permanently deleted during backfill operations (archive-only)\n+- [ ] A single tenant exceeding its rate limit does not degrade ingestion latency for other tenants\n+- [ ] Rate limit configuration changes take effect without service restart\n+\n+## 10. Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency | Description | Criticality |\n+|------------|-------------|-------------|\n+| Storage Backend (PostgreSQL or ClickHouse) | At least one storage backend available in the platform | p1 |\n+| Types Registry | Schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units | p1 |\n+| Platform Auth Infrastructure | Authentication and authorization infrastructure (OAuth 2.0, mTLS) | p1 |\n+\n+## 11. Assumptions\n+\n+- At least one storage backend (PostgreSQL or ClickHouse) is available in the platform\n+- Types Registry service is available for schema validation\n+- Platform authentication/authorization infrastructure exists\n+- Consumers (Billing, Quota Enforcement, Monitoring) integrate via query API\n+\n+## 12. Risks\n+\n+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n+|------|--------|------------|\n+| Storage backend unavailability | Usage records lost during outage | Buffering with retry and backoff; multi-backend routing |\n+| High ingestion volume exceeds capacity | Usage sources rejected; delayed billing data | Horizontal scaling; SDK-side batching and buffering; per-tenant rate limiting |\n+| Schema evolution breaks existing sources | Usage sources fail validation after type changes | Backward-compatible schema evolution; versioned type schemas |\n+| Cross-tenant data leakage | Security and compliance violation | Fail-closed authorization; tenant isolation at all layers |\n+| Reconciliation false positives | Unnecessary backfill operations triggered by transient delays | Configurable thresholds and cooldown periods; require multiple consecutive missed heartbeats before alerting |\n+| Backfill data quality | Incorrect replacement data worsens the gap instead of fixing it | Full schema validation on backfill events; archive-not-delete allows rollback; audit trail enables investigation |\n+| Noisy-neighbor ingestion | A single tenant or source exhausts ingestion capacity, degrading service for all tenants | Hierarchical rate limiting (global, per-tenant, per-source); priority-based load shedding |\n+| Rate limit misconfiguration | Limits set too low cause legitimate data loss; too high provide no protection | System defaults with per-tenant hot-reloadable overrides; rate limit observability and approaching-limit alerts |\n+| Retry storms after rate limiting | Synchronized client retries after a rate limit event amplify load | SDK enforces exponential backoff with jitter; Retry-After headers provide explicit retry guidance |\n+\n+## 13. Open Questions\n+\n+- Specific CloudEvents format adoption for usage record schema\n+- Retention policy enforcement mechanism (TTL vs. scheduled cleanup)\n+- Exact SDK batching defaults (count threshold, time threshold)\n+- Secondary data sources for automated reconciliation (infrastructure metrics, API gateway logs)\n+- Default rate limit values for system-wide defaults and per-source defaults\n+- Rate limit algorithm distribution strategy for horizontally-scaled ingestion nodes (local per-node vs. centralized counter)","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"dc3a5bb686bc26e9e0919f2d68781928e5aa9530","original_commit_id":"59c81847aced9792e7622eb10ad6eef3085041da","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Resolve rate limit distribution strategy before P1 implementation.**\n\nLine 791 lists \"Rate limit algorithm distribution strategy for horizontally-scaled ingestion nodes (local per-node vs. centralized counter)\" as an open question. However, this is a critical architectural decision that directly affects the accuracy and behavior of P1 rate limiting requirements (lines 374-410).\n\n**Impact of unresolved strategy:**\n\n- **Local per-node limits**: A tenant distributing requests across N ingestion nodes could achieve N× the configured rate limit, violating isolation guarantees in `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit` and making rate limiting ineffective against noisy-neighbor issues.\n- **Centralized counter**: Provides accurate limits but introduces coordination overhead, potential bottlenecks, and single point of failure that could degrade `fdd-uc-req-scalability`.\n\nMove this decision out of Open Questions into the functional requirements. Specify one of:\n1. **Distributed rate limiting with shared state** (e.g., Redis-backed token bucket with TTL caching)\n2. **Sticky routing** (per-tenant node assignment with local limits)\n3. **Hybrid approach** (local fast path with periodic global synchronization)\n\nDocument the chosen strategy, trade-offs, and how it satisfies both accuracy and scalability requirements.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md` at line 791, Move the open\nquestion at line 791 into the functional requirements section and choose one\nconcrete distribution strategy (pick one: \"Distributed rate limiting with shared\nstate (Redis token-bucket + TTL caching)\", \"Sticky routing (per-tenant node\nassignment)\", or \"Hybrid: local fast-path + periodic global sync\"); update the\nPRD to state the chosen strategy, document trade-offs and failure modes, and\nexplicitly map how it satisfies or mitigates the requirements\nfdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit and fdd-uc-req-scalability (include how accuracy,\ncoordination overhead, fault tolerance, and scalability are handled).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-10T15:49:57Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T15:49:58Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2788773187","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788773187"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2788773187"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2788773187/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":791,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":791,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790463342","pull_request_review_id":3781825031,"id":2790463342,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mUxdu","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,786 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"c471acf48bd6a8bc2c93081ce13dd24045d2446d","user":{"login":"Artifizer","id":192502778,"node_id":"U_kgDOC3lb-g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/192502778?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer","html_url":"https://github.com/Artifizer","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"I believe the whole design here is about Metering (Ledger) system, so that aggregation, rules application, exceptions, reconciliation, report generation and other procedures supposed to be done by external systems","created_at":"2026-02-10T21:51:00Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T22:04:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790463342","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790463342"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790463342"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790463342/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":117,"original_line":116,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2788281325,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":116,"position":117,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790467195","pull_request_review_id":3781825031,"id":2790467195,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mUyZ7","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"Artifizer","id":192502778,"node_id":"U_kgDOC3lb-g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/192502778?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer","html_url":"https://github.com/Artifizer","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"I would emphasize below that we are talking here about Usage Ledger / Metering system, because otherwise there will be an intent to add more and more requirements here (e.g complex data aggregation)","created_at":"2026-02-10T21:52:12Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T22:04:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790467195","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790467195"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790467195"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790467195/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":1,"original_line":1,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":1,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790477082","pull_request_review_id":3781825031,"id":2790477082,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mU00a","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"Artifizer","id":192502778,"node_id":"U_kgDOC3lb-g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/192502778?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer","html_url":"https://github.com/Artifizer","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"please rename everywhere:\n\n`fdd-uc-` to `cpt-cf-uc-`\n\ncpt - Cyber Pilot ID\ncf - Cyber Fabric","created_at":"2026-02-10T21:55:00Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T22:04:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790477082","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790477082"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790477082"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790477082/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":153,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":153,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790498453","pull_request_review_id":3781825031,"id":2790498453,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mU6CV","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"Artifizer","id":192502778,"node_id":"U_kgDOC3lb-g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/192502778?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer","html_url":"https://github.com/Artifizer","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Requirements should mandate that permissions for reporting usage of a given resource type are validated through the GTS type system and the project’s access-control model. This prevents any module or integration from reporting usage for resources it does not own. For example, a File Parser module must not be able to report LLM token usage.","created_at":"2026-02-10T22:00:29Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T22:04:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790498453","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790498453"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790498453"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790498453/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":218,"original_line":215,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":215,"position":218,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790572028","pull_request_review_id":3781938918,"id":2790572028,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mVL_8","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"Artifizer","id":192502778,"node_id":"U_kgDOC3lb-g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/192502778?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer","html_url":"https://github.com/Artifizer","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"I'd suggest to have the following usage entries attributes (here or in DESIGN):\n```\n- tenant id (mandatory)\n- usage type (mandatory, as GTS type)\n- resource id (mandatory, for global per-tenant usage just use tenant ID)\n- value (mandatory)\n- event time (mandatory, when event happened on the source side)\n- ingest time (mandatory, when event was ingested into UC DB)\n- idempotency key (mandatory)\n- source (optional, this is required for distributed systems to distinguish origin of the usage record)\n- ...\n```\n\nso then the unique key would be:\n(tenant_id, source_id, usage_type, resource_id, idempotency_key)","created_at":"2026-02-10T22:20:47Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T22:27:55Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790572028","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790572028"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790572028"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790572028/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":251,"original_line":228,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":228,"position":251,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790603092","pull_request_review_id":3781971077,"id":2790603092,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mVTlU","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"Artifizer","id":192502778,"node_id":"U_kgDOC3lb-g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/192502778?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer","html_url":"https://github.com/Artifizer","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"We should require to have:\n1) stable order in APIs, e.g. have it sorted always like (event_ts, ingest_ts, idempotancy_key) in DESIGN (p1)\n2) cursor-based pagination (p1)\n3) snapshot reads (p3)","created_at":"2026-02-10T22:30:39Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T22:48:37Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790603092","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790603092"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790603092"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790603092/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":273,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":273,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790617890","pull_request_review_id":3781971077,"id":2790617890,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mVXMi","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range (scoped to a single tenant and usage type) and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Concurrent-write strategy — Reject and Retry**: During an active backfill, the system **MUST** use range-level locking to enforce exclusive write access to the affected `(tenant, usage_type, time_range)` partition. The backfill operation acquires a lock before beginning the archive-and-insert transaction and holds it until the transaction commits or rolls back. While the lock is held:\n+\n+1. **Real-time ingestion behavior**: Any real-time event (via SDK, collector agent, or REST API as defined in `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`) whose `(tenant_id, usage_type, timestamp)` falls within a currently locked backfill range **MUST** be rejected with HTTP status `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_IN_PROGRESS`. The response body **MUST** include the fields `retry_after_ms` (estimated remaining backfill duration, minimum 1000) and `locked_range` (the `[start, end)` interval that is locked).\n+2. **Lock scope and overlap**: The system **MUST** reject a backfill request with HTTP `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_RANGE_OVERLAP` if its requested range overlaps with any currently locked backfill range for the same tenant and usage type. Only one backfill operation per `(tenant, usage_type)` overlapping range is permitted at a time. Non-overlapping ranges for the same tenant and usage type, or any ranges for different tenants or usage types, **MAY** execute concurrently.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. The reject-and-retry strategy is chosen over queue-and-replay or snapshot isolation alternatives because it is the simplest model to implement correctly, avoids unbounded buffering on the server, keeps real-time ingestion latency deterministic (a fast 409 vs. an indeterminate queue wait), and pushes retry responsibility to the SDK where backpressure is already handled. Backfill operations are expected to be infrequent (operator-initiated corrections), so brief real-time rejection windows are an acceptable trade-off for strong atomicity guarantees. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Interaction with backfill**: If a backfill operation targets a time range that contains previously amended events, the backfill **MUST** archive the amended events along with all other events in that range. Amendment history is not preserved — the backfill's replacement events become the sole active record. This means backfill unconditionally supersedes any prior amendments within its range, keeping the correction model simple: amendments are for surgical fixes to individual events, while backfill is a wholesale replacement that starts from a clean slate.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption. Header formats are defined as follows:\n+\n+- **`Retry-After`**: **MUST** be provided as `delay-seconds` — an integer representing the number of seconds the client should wait before retrying. **MAY** additionally include an `HTTP-date` value for backward compatibility with older HTTP clients.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Limit`**: **MUST** be an integer representing the total request allowance for the current rate limit window.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Remaining`**: **MUST** be an integer representing the number of requests remaining in the current rate limit window.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Reset`**: **MUST** be an integer Unix epoch timestamp (seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z) in UTC indicating when the current rate limit window resets.\n+\n+All time-related header values are in UTC. Clients **SHOULD** parse these header values as integers and **SHOULD** treat missing or unparseable values conservatively (i.e., assume the limit is exhausted and apply a default backoff).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota. Explicit format definitions prevent ambiguity between delay-seconds and HTTP-date for `Retry-After`, and between epoch timestamps and other representations for `X-RateLimit-Reset`.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Priority-Based Load Shedding\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-load-shedding`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support priority classification of usage event types (e.g., billing-critical counters vs. analytics metrics) and, when operating under sustained overload beyond rate limits, **MUST** preferentially accept higher-priority events while shedding lower-priority ones. The priority classification **MUST** be configurable per usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Under extreme load, indiscriminate rejection causes billing-critical data loss. Priority-based load shedding ensures that the most business-critical usage data (which affects revenue accuracy) is preserved even when the system cannot accept all traffic.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Observability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-observability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-tenant and per-source rate limit consumption as metrics (current usage vs. limit, rejection counts, throttle duration) for operator dashboards. The system **MUST** emit alerts when tenants approach configured warning thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90% of capacity).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operators need visibility into rate limit utilization to proactively adjust limits before tenants experience rejections. Approaching-limit alerts enable capacity planning.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### High Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-availability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** maintain 99.95% monthly availability for usage collection endpoints.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 99.95% uptime per calendar month\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection is on the critical path for all billable operations.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Throughput\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-throughput`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support sustained ingestion of at least 600,000 usage records per minute (10,000 events per second) under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: >= 600,000 records/minute (10,000 events/sec) sustained\n+**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-ingestion-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage record ingestion within 200ms (p95) under normal load.","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"Artifizer","id":192502778,"node_id":"U_kgDOC3lb-g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/192502778?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer","html_url":"https://github.com/Artifizer","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Here we can add a requirement that Query operations or retention jobs must not impact Ingestion latency.\n\nIn the DESIGN, I'd consider design like:\n- usage is always inserted into operational database\n- usage history is stored and maintained separately in historical database (with different plugins, \"column\" store DB, etc)","created_at":"2026-02-10T22:35:22Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T22:48:37Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790617890","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790617890"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790617890"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790617890/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":524,"original_line":481,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":481,"position":524,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790640963","pull_request_review_id":3781971077,"id":2790640963,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mVc1D","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range (scoped to a single tenant and usage type) and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Concurrent-write strategy — Reject and Retry**: During an active backfill, the system **MUST** use range-level locking to enforce exclusive write access to the affected `(tenant, usage_type, time_range)` partition. The backfill operation acquires a lock before beginning the archive-and-insert transaction and holds it until the transaction commits or rolls back. While the lock is held:\n+\n+1. **Real-time ingestion behavior**: Any real-time event (via SDK, collector agent, or REST API as defined in `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`) whose `(tenant_id, usage_type, timestamp)` falls within a currently locked backfill range **MUST** be rejected with HTTP status `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_IN_PROGRESS`. The response body **MUST** include the fields `retry_after_ms` (estimated remaining backfill duration, minimum 1000) and `locked_range` (the `[start, end)` interval that is locked).","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"Artifizer","id":192502778,"node_id":"U_kgDOC3lb-g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/192502778?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer","html_url":"https://github.com/Artifizer","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Artifizer/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"CRITICAL: This is potentially problematic place that can lead to inability to collect critical usage, so I would suggest to consider some design when new usage events reporting is never blocked by the backfill.\n\nIdeally we can support both modes - blocking and non-blocking, because usage backfill procedures may have different severities in different scenarios","created_at":"2026-02-10T22:42:58Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T22:48:37Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790640963","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790640963"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2790640963"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2790640963/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":299,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":299,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2791913547","pull_request_review_id":3783359136,"id":2791913547,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6maThL","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"pr0tey","id":1847966,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NDc5NjY=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1847966?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey","html_url":"https://github.com/pr0tey","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"It will be nice to have a place to store some kind of metadata for every record. This metadata may later be processed by a custom reporting module to show a detailed report about LLM usage (as an example).\n\nYes, we can introduce different metrics for different models, but it will be more complicated to calculate them on the billing side.","created_at":"2026-02-11T08:03:47Z","updated_at":"2026-02-11T08:05:16Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2791913547","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2791913547"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2791913547"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2791913547/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":119,"original_line":118,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":118,"position":119,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2791915033","pull_request_review_id":3783359136,"id":2791915033,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6maT4Z","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"9d1e242148d7a633f35981c23db7b7a964cfed41","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"pr0tey","id":1847966,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NDc5NjY=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1847966?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey","html_url":"https://github.com/pr0tey","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pr0tey/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Please add per-user attribution as well. We would like to track usage on the user level.","created_at":"2026-02-11T08:04:18Z","updated_at":"2026-02-11T08:05:16Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2791915033","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2791915033"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2791915033"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2791915033/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":124,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":124,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2792403949","pull_request_review_id":3783873886,"id":2792403949,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mcLPt","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"In Constructor we planned more generic model, licenses and quotas can be assigned to different subjects (users, tenants), so, we need to collect usage for different subjects too (within a tenant). \r\nIn other words we have subject type + ID and tenant ID in our usage record model","created_at":"2026-02-11T10:05:49Z","updated_at":"2026-02-11T10:31:49Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2792403949","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2792403949"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2792403949"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2792403949/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":36,"original_line":36,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":36,"position":36,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2792508902","pull_request_review_id":3783873886,"id":2792508902,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6mck3m","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized platform service for collecting, storing, and distributing usage data from all sources, enabling accurate metering for billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, and analytics with tenant isolation and type-safe usage definitions.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture and storage; pricing, rating, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated data.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Zero discrepancies between billing and monitoring usage data\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Views usage reports and consumption data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to usage breakdowns by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes aggregated usage data for rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Multi-dimensional aggregation (tenant, resource, type, time window)\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Reconciliation and gap detection for proactive data integrity\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only\n+- **Usage Dashboards**: Handled by Monitoring/Analytics; UC provides data API\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying & Aggregation\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Aggregation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-aggregation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** aggregate usage data by tenant, resource, usage type, and configurable time windows (minute, hour, day).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Billing, quota enforcement, and monitoring require pre-aggregated data at different granularities.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying usage data with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to usage data for billing, monitoring, and reporting.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `fdd-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Reconciliation\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range (scoped to a single tenant and usage type) and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Concurrent-write strategy — Reject and Retry**: During an active backfill, the system **MUST** use range-level locking to enforce exclusive write access to the affected `(tenant, usage_type, time_range)` partition. The backfill operation acquires a lock before beginning the archive-and-insert transaction and holds it until the transaction commits or rolls back. While the lock is held:\n+\n+1. **Real-time ingestion behavior**: Any real-time event (via SDK, collector agent, or REST API as defined in `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion`) whose `(tenant_id, usage_type, timestamp)` falls within a currently locked backfill range **MUST** be rejected with HTTP status `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_IN_PROGRESS`. The response body **MUST** include the fields `retry_after_ms` (estimated remaining backfill duration, minimum 1000) and `locked_range` (the `[start, end)` interval that is locked).\n+2. **Lock scope and overlap**: The system **MUST** reject a backfill request with HTTP `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_RANGE_OVERLAP` if its requested range overlaps with any currently locked backfill range for the same tenant and usage type. Only one backfill operation per `(tenant, usage_type)` overlapping range is permitted at a time. Non-overlapping ranges for the same tenant and usage type, or any ranges for different tenants or usage types, **MAY** execute concurrently.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. The reject-and-retry strategy is chosen over queue-and-replay or snapshot isolation alternatives because it is the simplest model to implement correctly, avoids unbounded buffering on the server, keeps real-time ingestion latency deterministic (a fast 409 vs. an indeterminate queue wait), and pushes retry responsibility to the SDK where backpressure is already handled. Backfill operations are expected to be infrequent (operator-initiated corrections), so brief real-time rejection windows are an acceptable trade-off for strong atomicity guarantees. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as excluded from billing and aggregation while retaining them for audit).\n+\n+**Interaction with backfill**: If a backfill operation targets a time range that contains previously amended events, the backfill **MUST** archive the amended events along with all other events in that range. Amendment history is not preserved — the backfill's replacement events become the sole active record. This means backfill unconditionally supersedes any prior amendments within its range, keeping the correction model simple: amendments are for surgical fixes to individual events, while backfill is a wholesale replacement that starts from a clean slate.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be excluded from billing and aggregation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Reconciliation and Gap Detection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-reconciliation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gap detection through: (1) heartbeat monitoring — tracking periodic signals from known usage sources and alerting when expected heartbeats are missing; (2) watermark tracking — monitoring the latest event timestamp per source, tenant, and meter combination, and flagging sources whose watermark stops advancing; (3) scheduled count reconciliation — comparing event counts across pipeline stages (ingestion vs. storage) to detect data loss.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Proactive gap detection enables timely backfill before billing periods close, reducing the need for post-invoice corrections. Heartbeat monitoring detects source-level outages, watermark tracking detects partial failures, and count reconciliation detects pipeline-level data loss.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption. Header formats are defined as follows:\n+\n+- **`Retry-After`**: **MUST** be provided as `delay-seconds` — an integer representing the number of seconds the client should wait before retrying. **MAY** additionally include an `HTTP-date` value for backward compatibility with older HTTP clients.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Limit`**: **MUST** be an integer representing the total request allowance for the current rate limit window.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Remaining`**: **MUST** be an integer representing the number of requests remaining in the current rate limit window.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Reset`**: **MUST** be an integer Unix epoch timestamp (seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z) in UTC indicating when the current rate limit window resets.\n+\n+All time-related header values are in UTC. Clients **SHOULD** parse these header values as integers and **SHOULD** treat missing or unparseable values conservatively (i.e., assume the limit is exhausted and apply a default backoff).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota. Explicit format definitions prevent ambiguity between delay-seconds and HTTP-date for `Retry-After`, and between epoch timestamps and other representations for `X-RateLimit-Reset`.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Priority-Based Load Shedding\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-load-shedding`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support priority classification of usage event types (e.g., billing-critical counters vs. analytics metrics) and, when operating under sustained overload beyond rate limits, **MUST** preferentially accept higher-priority events while shedding lower-priority ones. The priority classification **MUST** be configurable per usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Under extreme load, indiscriminate rejection causes billing-critical data loss. Priority-based load shedding ensures that the most business-critical usage data (which affects revenue accuracy) is preserved even when the system cannot accept all traffic.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Observability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-rate-limit-observability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-tenant and per-source rate limit consumption as metrics (current usage vs. limit, rejection counts, throttle duration) for operator dashboards. The system **MUST** emit alerts when tenants approach configured warning thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90% of capacity).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operators need visibility into rate limit utilization to proactively adjust limits before tenants experience rejections. Approaching-limit alerts enable capacity planning.\n+**Actors**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `fdd-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### High Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-availability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** maintain 99.95% monthly availability for usage collection endpoints.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 99.95% uptime per calendar month\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection is on the critical path for all billable operations.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Throughput\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-throughput`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support sustained ingestion of at least 600,000 usage records per minute (10,000 events per second) under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: >= 600,000 records/minute (10,000 events/sec) sustained\n+**Rationale**: High-volume services (LLM Gateway, API Gateway) generate significant event throughput.\n+\n+#### Ingestion Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-ingestion-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage record ingestion within 200ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 200ms\n+**Rationale**: Low ingestion latency prevents blocking in usage source services.\n+\n+#### Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-query-latency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** complete usage queries for 30-day ranges within 500ms (p95) under normal load.\n+\n+**Threshold**: p95 <= 500ms for 30-day range queries\n+**Rationale**: Billing and quota enforcement systems require fast query responses.\n+\n+#### Exactly-Once Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-exactly-once`\n+\n+The system **MUST** guarantee exactly-once processing; zero usage records lost or duplicated under normal operation.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss or duplication under normal operation\n+**Rationale**: Duplicate or missing records directly impact billing accuracy.\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** preserve immutable audit records for all usage data including source, timestamps, and any corrections.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of usage operations audited\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for billing disputes and compliance.\n+\n+#### Authentication Required\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authentication`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require authentication (OAuth 2.0, mTLS, or API key) for all API operations.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthenticated API access\n+**Rationale**: Usage data is sensitive and must be protected.\n+\n+#### Authorization Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce authorization for read/write operations based on tenant context and usage type permissions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized data access\n+**Rationale**: Authorization prevents unauthorized usage data manipulation.\n+\n+#### Horizontal Scalability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-scalability`\n+\n+The system **MUST** scale horizontally to handle increased load without architectural changes.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Linear throughput scaling with added instances\n+**Rationale**: Usage volume grows with platform adoption.\n+\n+#### Storage Fault Tolerance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-fault-tolerance`\n+\n+The system **MUST** buffer usage records during storage backend failures and recover without data loss.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero data loss during storage backend failures\n+**Rationale**: Storage outages must not result in lost usage data.\n+\n+#### Configurable Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-retention`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support retention periods from 7 days to 7 years depending on usage type and compliance requirements.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Configurable retention from 7 days to 7 years\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different compliance and operational retention needs.\n+\n+#### Graceful Degradation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-graceful-degradation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** continue accepting usage records even if downstream consumers (billing, monitoring) are unavailable.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero ingestion failures due to downstream consumer unavailability\n+**Rationale**: Usage collection must not be blocked by consumer outages.\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Usage Ingestion SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-sdk`\n+\n+**Type**: Client library\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Client-side SDK with automatic batching for high-throughput usage emission. Primary ingestion path for platform services.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Usage REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: HTTP API for usage record ingestion (low-volume/external), querying, and administration (unit registration, retention configuration).\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+#### Collector Agent\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-interface-collector-agent`\n+\n+**Type**: Sidecar/agent process\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Sidecar deployment pattern for collecting usage from co-located services without SDK integration.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### Storage Adapter Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-storage-adapter`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Rust trait / plugin interface\n+**Compatibility**: Backward-compatible within major version; adapters implement a defined trait for read/write operations.\n+\n+#### Types Registry Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-contract-types-registry`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from client\n+**Protocol/Format**: Internal API\n+**Compatibility**: Schema validation contract; UC depends on Types Registry for unit and type definitions.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### UC: High-Volume Usage Emission via SDK\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-sdk-emission`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-usage-source`, `fdd-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Usage type registered in Types Registry\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Service calls SDK to record usage event\n+2. SDK queues event in memory\n+3. SDK batches events (by count or time threshold)\n+4. SDK sends batch to UC\n+5. UC validates each record against type schema\n+6. UC persists to configured storage backend\n+7. SDK receives acknowledgment\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Usage records persisted with tenant/resource attribution; available for query and aggregation\n+\n+### UC: Configure Custom Measuring Unit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-custom-unit`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Unit name is unique\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Operator defines unit schema (name, type: counter/gauge, base unit)\n+2. Operator submits via API\n+3. UC validates unit definition\n+4. UC registers unit with Types Registry\n+5. UC confirms registration\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- New unit immediately available for usage collection; sources can emit records with new unit type\n+\n+### UC: Query Tenant Usage for Billing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-billing-query`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+- Aggregation window closed\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Billing System queries usage API for billing period\n+2. UC retrieves aggregated usage for tenant and time window\n+3. UC returns usage grouped by usage type\n+4. Billing System processes for rating\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Billing System has accurate, deduplicated usage data for invoice generation\n+\n+### UC: Real-Time Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `fdd-uc-req-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Actor**: `fdd-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+1. Quota Enforcement System queries UC API for current usage\n+2. UC returns aggregated usage for tenant and usage type\n+3. Quota Enforcement System compares against tenant limits\n+4. Quota Enforcement System triggers enforcement if threshold exceeded\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+- Tenant usage enforced before exceeding quota; no over-consumption","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"78e5f864dfe8f6aeae2f93af9ef31275615effb2","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"In this scenario quota enforcement and usage reporting are separated and client should know what metric is quotable. In Constructor, we assumed that UC is the entry point for all usage operations, and if a metric has some attribute identifying that it's quotable metric, UC will perform a quota change operation (with acquisition if not previously acquired on client side) as a part of usage reporting. \nIn other words - usage of every metric should be reported to UC but not all trackable metric are quotable and if they're - it's a part of usage collection flow to decrease the possibility of error on the client side.\nFrom my perspective such flow can be supported too if we extend a bit model of \"storage\" to a plugin-based usage handler","created_at":"2026-02-11T10:29:25Z","updated_at":"2026-02-11T10:29:29Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2792508902","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2792508902"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2792508902"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2792508902/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":737,"original_line":689,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":689,"position":737,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2799343186","pull_request_review_id":3779106566,"id":2799343186,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6m2pZS","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,801 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized usage ledger (metering system) for reliably collecting, persisting, and exposing raw usage records from all platform sources, with exactly-once semantics, tenant isolation, and type-safe usage definitions. The Usage Collector acts as the authoritative record of resource consumption — it does not aggregate, interpret, or act on the data. Downstream systems (billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, analytics) consume raw usage records and apply their own aggregation rules, policies, and business logic.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture, storage, and raw data exposure; aggregation, pricing, rating, reconciliation, report generation, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection into a single metering ledger, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated raw data and apply their own domain-specific logic independently.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Single source of truth: all downstream consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) operate on the same raw usage records from UC\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies (performed by external systems; UC exposes metadata to support this) |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Queries raw usage data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to raw usage records filtered by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes raw usage records from UC for aggregation, rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Usage query API for raw record retrieval with filtering and pagination\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Data Aggregation**: Multi-dimensional aggregation (by time windows, rollups, grouping) is the responsibility of downstream consumers. UC exposes raw usage records; consumers apply their own aggregation logic.\n+- **Reconciliation & Gap Detection**: Monitoring for data gaps, heartbeat tracking, watermark analysis, and cross-stage count reconciliation are handled by external observability/reconciliation systems. UC exposes metadata (event counts, timestamps per source) that external systems can consume for this purpose.\n+- **Report Generation**: Usage reports, dashboards, and visualizations are handled by Monitoring/Analytics systems; UC provides raw data access.\n+- **Rules & Exceptions**: Business rules, usage policies, threshold-based actions, and exception handling are the responsibility of downstream consumers.\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System.\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System.\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only.\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase.\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-billing-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-types-registry`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Source Authorization\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-source-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** identify the source of each usage record through the platform's authentication infrastructure and **MUST** validate that the source is authorized to report usage for the specific usage type being submitted. Source-to-usage-type bindings **MUST** be defined through the GTS type system, with permitted usage types registered in the Types Registry as part of the source's type definition. The system **MUST** reject usage records from sources that are not authorized for the given usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without source-level authorization, any module or integration could report usage for resource types it does not own (e.g., a File Parser reporting LLM token usage), leading to inaccurate metering and potential billing manipulation.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-types-registry`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying raw usage records with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with pagination. The API returns raw records; aggregation is the responsibility of downstream consumers.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to raw usage data to apply their own aggregation, rating, and analysis logic.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-billing-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Amendment\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range (scoped to a single tenant and usage type) and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Concurrent-write strategy — Reject and Retry**: During an active backfill, the system **MUST** use range-level locking to enforce exclusive write access to the affected `(tenant, usage_type, time_range)` partition. The backfill operation acquires a lock before beginning the archive-and-insert transaction and holds it until the transaction commits or rolls back. While the lock is held:\n+\n+1. **Real-time ingestion behavior**: Any real-time event (via SDK, collector agent, or REST API as defined in `cpt-cf-uc-req-usage-ingestion`) whose `(tenant_id, usage_type, timestamp)` falls within a currently locked backfill range **MUST** be rejected with HTTP status `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_IN_PROGRESS`. The response body **MUST** include the fields `retry_after_ms` (estimated remaining backfill duration, minimum 1000) and `locked_range` (the `[start, end)` interval that is locked).\n+2. **Lock scope and overlap**: The system **MUST** reject a backfill request with HTTP `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_RANGE_OVERLAP` if its requested range overlaps with any currently locked backfill range for the same tenant and usage type. Only one backfill operation per `(tenant, usage_type)` overlapping range is permitted at a time. Non-overlapping ranges for the same tenant and usage type, or any ranges for different tenants or usage types, **MAY** execute concurrently.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. The reject-and-retry strategy is chosen over queue-and-replay or snapshot isolation alternatives because it is the simplest model to implement correctly, avoids unbounded buffering on the server, keeps real-time ingestion latency deterministic (a fast 409 vs. an indeterminate queue wait), and pushes retry responsibility to the SDK where backpressure is already handled. Backfill operations are expected to be infrequent (operator-initiated corrections), so brief real-time rejection windows are an acceptable trade-off for strong atomicity guarantees. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as inactive while retaining them for audit). Downstream consumers **MUST** be able to distinguish active from deprecated records when querying.\n+\n+**Interaction with backfill**: If a backfill operation targets a time range that contains previously amended events, the backfill **MUST** archive the amended events along with all other events in that range. Amendment history is not preserved — the backfill's replacement events become the sole active record. This means backfill unconditionally supersedes any prior amendments within its range, keeping the correction model simple: amendments are for surgical fixes to individual events, while backfill is a wholesale replacement that starts from a clean slate.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be clearly distinguishable from active records so that downstream consumers can exclude them from their processing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Ledger Metadata Exposure\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-ledger-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-source and per-tenant metadata — including event counts, latest event timestamps (watermarks), and ingestion statistics — via API, enabling external reconciliation and observability systems to detect gaps and perform integrity checks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: While reconciliation logic is out of scope for the usage ledger, exposing the raw metadata needed for gap detection enables external systems to build reconciliation workflows. This keeps the ledger focused while not blocking operational integrity monitoring.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+When a custom measuring unit is registered, the platform operator **MUST** also register the source-to-usage-type binding in the Types Registry, declaring which sources are permitted to emit records of this type. The system **MUST NOT** accept usage records for a type that has no registered source bindings.\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment. Source-to-usage-type bindings ensure that only authorized sources can emit records for each unit.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption. Header formats are defined as follows:\n+\n+- **`Retry-After`**: **MUST** be provided as `delay-seconds` — an integer representing the number of seconds the client should wait before retrying. **MAY** additionally include an `HTTP-date` value for backward compatibility with older HTTP clients.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Limit`**: **MUST** be an integer representing the total request allowance for the current rate limit window.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Remaining`**: **MUST** be an integer representing the number of requests remaining in the current rate limit window.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Reset`**: **MUST** be an integer Unix epoch timestamp (seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z) in UTC indicating when the current rate limit window resets.\n+\n+All time-related header values are in UTC. Clients **SHOULD** parse these header values as integers and **SHOULD** treat missing or unparseable values conservatively (i.e., assume the limit is exhausted and apply a default backoff).","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"c3a720802777e6ecc0472f1850e8d38e5be1857f","user":{"login":"graphite-app[bot]","id":96075541,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOBbn_FQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/158384?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/graphite-app","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**HTTP specification violation in Retry-After header format**\n\nThe requirement states `Retry-After` **MUST** be provided as `delay-seconds` and **MAY** additionally include an `HTTP-date` value. This violates RFC 7231: the `Retry-After` header accepts a single value that is EITHER delay-seconds OR HTTP-date, not both.\n\nClients parsing this header per HTTP spec will fail or behave unpredictably.\n\n**Fix**: Choose one format:\n\n```markdown\n- **`Retry-After`**: **MUST** be provided as `delay-seconds` — an integer representing the number of seconds the client should wait before retrying. The HTTP-date format **MUST NOT** be used to ensure consistent client parsing behavior.\n```\n\nOr if backward compatibility is truly needed:\n\n```markdown\n- **`Retry-After`**: **MUST** be provided as `delay-seconds` for new clients. For backward compatibility with legacy HTTP/1.0 clients, the system **MAY** also provide an `X-Retry-After-Date` extension header with HTTP-date format.\n```\n```suggestion\nWhen rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption. Header formats are defined as follows:\n\n- **`Retry-After`**: **MUST** be provided as `delay-seconds` — an integer representing the number of seconds the client should wait before retrying. The HTTP-date format **MUST NOT** be used to ensure consistent client parsing behavior.\n- **`X-RateLimit-Limit`**: **MUST** be an integer representing the total request allowance for the current rate limit window.\n- **`X-RateLimit-Remaining`**: **MUST** be an integer representing the number of requests remaining in the current rate limit window.\n- **`X-RateLimit-Reset`**: **MUST** be an integer Unix epoch timestamp (seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z) in UTC indicating when the current rate limit window resets.\n\nAll time-related header values are in UTC. Clients **SHOULD** parse these header values as integers and **SHOULD** treat missing or unparseable values conservatively (i.e., assume the limit is exhausted and apply a default backoff).\n```\n  \n\n*Spotted by [Graphite Agent](https://app.graphite.com/diamond/?org=cyberfabric&ref=ai-review-comment)*<i class='graphite__hidden'><br /><br /><a href=\"https://app.graphite.com/github/pr/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/536?chatWithGeneratedComment=791b3a8f-82e8-4a34-86f5-83ba9f436ac9\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Fix in Graphite\" src=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-dark.svg\"></picture></a></i><i class='graphite__hidden'><br /><br />Is this helpful? React 👍 or 👎 to let us know.</i>","created_at":"2026-02-12T14:53:12Z","updated_at":"2026-02-12T14:53:13Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2799343186","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2799343186"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2799343186"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2799343186/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":459,"original_start_line":420,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":466,"original_line":427,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":427,"position":466,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2799471261","pull_request_review_id":3791649599,"id":2799471261,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6m3Iqd","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,842 @@\n+# PRD — Usage Collector\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+A centralized usage ledger (metering system) for reliably collecting, persisting, and exposing raw usage records from all platform sources, with exactly-once semantics, tenant isolation, and type-safe usage definitions. The Usage Collector acts as the authoritative record of resource consumption — it does not aggregate, interpret, or act on the data. Downstream systems (billing, quota enforcement, monitoring, analytics) consume raw usage records and apply their own aggregation rules, policies, and business logic.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The Usage Collector (UC) serves as the single source of truth for all platform usage data. UC focuses purely on usage capture, storage, and raw data exposure; aggregation, pricing, rating, reconciliation, report generation, and invoicing are handled by downstream consumers. It supports diverse collection patterns optimized for different throughput needs, multiple storage backends, and provides type-safe usage definitions through a schema-based type system.\n+\n+The service addresses the fragmentation problem where different consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) implement their own collection logic, leading to inconsistent data and duplicated effort. By centralizing usage collection into a single metering ledger, the platform ensures that all consumers operate on the same accurate, deduplicated raw data and apply their own domain-specific logic independently.\n+\n+Key problems:\n+\n+- **Fragmented tracking**: Each consumer implements own collection leading to inconsistent data\n+- **High-volume ingestion**: Per-event synchronous REST calls are inefficient at high throughput due to HTTP overhead and blocking behavior\n+- **No custom units**: Cannot meter new resource types (AI tokens) without code changes\n+- **Storage lock-in**: No flexibility for different retention and performance needs\n+\n+Notable systems that influenced this design: Amberflo (client-side SDK batching pattern, accuracy guarantees), OpenMeter (stream processing architecture, CloudEvents format, ClickHouse for time-series storage), Stripe Meters (meter configuration pattern, aggregation over billing periods), OpenTelemetry Collector (sidecar/agent deployment pattern for high-throughput telemetry).\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- All billable platform services emit usage through UC\n+- Single source of truth: all downstream consumers (billing, monitoring, quota enforcement) operate on the same raw usage records from UC\n+- Custom unit registration in less than 5 minutes without code changes\n+- High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per second without blocking\n+- 99.95%+ monthly availability\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Usage Record | A single data point representing resource consumption by a tenant |\n+| Counter | A monotonically increasing metric (e.g., total API calls) |\n+| Gauge | A point-in-time metric that can go up or down (e.g., current memory usage) |\n+| Measuring Unit | A registered schema defining how a usage type is measured (e.g., \"ai-credits\", \"vCPU-hours\") |\n+| Storage Adapter | A plugin that connects UC to a specific storage backend |\n+| Idempotency Key | A client-provided identifier ensuring exactly-once processing of a usage record |\n+| Backfill | The process of retroactively submitting historical usage data to fill gaps caused by outages, pipeline failures, or corrections |\n+| Grace Period | A configurable time window during which late-arriving events are accepted via normal ingestion without requiring explicit backfill |\n+| Reconciliation | The process of comparing usage data across pipeline stages or external sources to detect gaps and inconsistencies (performed by external systems; UC exposes metadata to support this) |\n+| Amendment | A correction to previously recorded usage data, either by replacing events in a time range or deprecating individual events |\n+| Rate Limit | A constraint on the volume of requests or data a tenant or source can submit within a time window |\n+| Load Shedding | The deliberate dropping or deferral of low-priority work to preserve system stability under overload |\n+| Snapshot Read | A query that sees data as it existed at a specific point in time, providing consistency across paginated requests despite concurrent data modifications |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Platform Operator\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+**Role**: Configures storage adapters, retention policies, custom measuring units, and monitors system health.\n+**Needs**: Ability to manage storage backends, define retention policies, register custom units, and monitor system health without code changes.\n+\n+#### Tenant Administrator\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+**Role**: Queries raw usage data for their tenant.\n+**Needs**: Access to raw usage records filtered by type and resource for their tenant only, with time-range filtering.\n+\n+#### Platform Developer\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+**Role**: Integrates services with UC using SDKs or APIs to emit usage data.\n+**Needs**: Well-documented SDKs and APIs for emitting usage data with minimal integration effort.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### Usage Source\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+**Role**: Any platform service, infrastructure adapter, or gateway that emits usage records (e.g., LLM Gateway, Compute Service, API Gateway).\n+\n+#### Billing System\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-billing-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes raw usage records from UC for aggregation, rating, pricing, and invoice generation.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement System\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes real-time usage data to enforce tenant resource limits and quotas.\n+\n+#### Monitoring System\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+**Role**: Consumes usage metrics for dashboards, alerting, and operational visibility.\n+\n+#### Types Registry\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+**Role**: Provides schema validation for usage types and custom measuring units.\n+\n+#### Storage Backend\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+**Role**: Persists usage records (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, or external system via adapter).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond project defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Client-side SDK with batching (primary ingestion path)\n+- Collector/agent pattern for sidecar deployment\n+- REST API for simple/low-volume cases and external integrations\n+- Counter and gauge metric semantics\n+- Per-tenant and per-resource usage attribution\n+- Pluggable storage adapter framework (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom)\n+- Custom measuring unit registration via API\n+- Usage query API for raw record retrieval with filtering and pagination\n+- Configurable retention policies\n+- Idempotency and deduplication for exactly-once semantics\n+- Backfill API for retroactive submission of historical usage data\n+- Late-arriving event handling with configurable grace period\n+- Per-tenant and per-source ingestion rate limiting with configurable overrides\n+- Priority-based load shedding under sustained overload\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- **Data Aggregation**: Multi-dimensional aggregation (by time windows, rollups, grouping) is the responsibility of downstream consumers. UC exposes raw usage records; consumers apply their own aggregation logic.\n+- **Reconciliation & Gap Detection**: Monitoring for data gaps, heartbeat tracking, watermark analysis, and cross-stage count reconciliation are handled by external observability/reconciliation systems. UC exposes metadata (event counts, timestamps per source) that external systems can consume for this purpose.\n+- **Report Generation**: Usage reports, dashboards, and visualizations are handled by Monitoring/Analytics systems; UC provides raw data access.\n+- **Rules & Exceptions**: Business rules, usage policies, threshold-based actions, and exception handling are the responsibility of downstream consumers.\n+- **Billing/Rating Logic**: Pricing calculation handled by downstream Billing System.\n+- **Invoice Generation**: Handled by Billing System.\n+- **Quota Enforcement Decisions**: Handled by Quota Enforcement System; UC provides data only.\n+- **Usage Prediction/Forecasting**: Deferred to future phase.\n+- **Multi-Region Replication**: Deferred to future phase.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code coverage unless otherwise specified. Document verification method only for non-test approaches (analysis, inspection, demonstration).\n+\n+### 5.1 Usage Ingestion\n+\n+#### Usage Record Ingestion\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-usage-ingestion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage records via SDK (with batching), collector agent, and REST API, supporting high-throughput scenarios (10,000+ events per second).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage sources have different throughput needs; providing multiple ingestion paths ensures all sources can efficiently emit data.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Pull-Based Collection\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-pull-collection`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support polling usage data from sources that cannot push, with configurable intervals and transformation to standard format. Pull adapters will be implemented as needed when specific integration requirements arise for systems that cannot emit usage data via push patterns.\n+\n+Initial implementation focuses on push patterns (SDK, collector agent, REST API). Pull-based collection will be added when concrete use cases are identified for sources that cannot integrate via push.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Some usage sources cannot integrate via push patterns and require polling.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Idempotency and Deduplication\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotency keys to ensure exactly-once processing, preventing duplicate records and incorrect aggregations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Network retries and batching can produce duplicate submissions; deduplication ensures billing accuracy.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.2 Metric Semantics\n+\n+#### Counter Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-counter-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce counter semantics (monotonically increasing values), validate increments, compute deltas, and detect/reject counter violations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Counters represent cumulative totals (e.g., total API calls); violations indicate data corruption or misconfigured sources.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Gauge Metric Semantics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-gauge-semantics`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support gauge metrics (point-in-time values) without monotonicity validation, storing values as-is.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Gauges represent instantaneous measurements (e.g., current memory usage) that naturally fluctuate.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+### 5.3 Attribution & Isolation\n+\n+#### Tenant Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-tenant-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** attribute all usage records to a tenant derived from security context, ensuring attribution is immutable and used for isolation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Accurate tenant attribution is the foundation for billing, quota enforcement, and data isolation.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Resource Attribution\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-resource-attribution`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support attributing usage to specific resource instances within a tenant, including resource ID, type, and lineage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Granular resource attribution enables per-resource billing and usage analysis.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce strict tenant isolation ensuring usage data is never accessible across tenants, failing closed on authorization failures.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant data isolation is a security and compliance requirement.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-developer`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-billing-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-quota-enforcement`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-types-registry`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Source Authorization\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-source-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** identify the source of each usage record through the platform's authentication infrastructure and **MUST** validate that the source is authorized to report usage for the specific usage type being submitted. Source-to-usage-type bindings **MUST** be defined through the GTS type system, with permitted usage types registered in the Types Registry as part of the source's type definition. The system **MUST** reject usage records from sources that are not authorized for the given usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without source-level authorization, any module or integration could report usage for resource types it does not own (e.g., a File Parser reporting LLM token usage), leading to inaccurate metering and potential billing manipulation.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-types-registry`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+### 5.4 Storage & Retention\n+\n+#### Pluggable Storage Framework\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-pluggable-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multiple storage backends (ClickHouse, PostgreSQL, custom adapters) with configurable routing by usage type.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different usage types have different retention and performance needs; pluggable storage avoids lock-in.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+#### Retention Policy Management\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable retention policies (global, per-tenant, per-usage-type) with automated enforcement.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Retention policies balance storage costs with compliance and operational needs.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Storage Health Monitoring\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-storage-health`\n+\n+The system **MUST** monitor storage adapter health, buffer records during failures, retry with backoff, and alert on persistent issues.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage failures must not result in data loss; buffering and retry ensure durability.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-storage-backend`\n+\n+### 5.5 Querying\n+\n+#### Usage Query API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-query-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide an API for querying raw usage records with filtering by time range, tenant, resource, and usage type with cursor-based pagination. The API returns raw records; aggregation is the responsibility of downstream consumers.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Downstream consumers need flexible access to raw usage data to apply their own aggregation, rating, and analysis logic.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-billing-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Stable Query Result Ordering\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-stable-ordering`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return query results in a stable, deterministic order across all API endpoints. The ordering **MUST** be consistent across pagination requests to prevent records from being missed or duplicated when combined with cursor-based pagination.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Stable ordering is essential for cursor-based pagination to work correctly. Without deterministic ordering, cursors cannot reliably mark positions in the result set, leading to missing or duplicate records across pages. This is critical for billing accuracy where downstream consumers must process complete, non-duplicated datasets.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-billing-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+#### Cursor-Based Pagination\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-cursor-pagination`\n+\n+The system **MUST** implement cursor-based pagination for all query APIs. Each page response **MUST** include an opaque cursor token that marks the position after the last record in the current page. Clients **MUST** pass this cursor in subsequent requests to retrieve the next page. Cursors **MUST** remain valid for at least 24 hours after issuance.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Offset-based pagination (`LIMIT/OFFSET`) is unreliable when data is being inserted concurrently — new insertions shift offsets, causing records to be skipped or duplicated across pages. Cursor-based pagination provides stable position markers that are unaffected by concurrent writes. This is essential for billing systems that must process complete usage datasets without gaps or duplicates.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-billing-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-tenant-admin`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### Snapshot Read Consistency\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-snapshot-reads`\n+\n+The system **SHOULD** support snapshot reads, allowing clients to query data with a consistent point-in-time view. When a query is initiated with snapshot isolation, all subsequent pagination requests in that query session **MUST** see the dataset as it existed at the snapshot timestamp, regardless of concurrent insertions, updates, or backfill operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Even with cursor-based pagination, concurrent data modifications (late-arriving events, backfill operations) can cause inconsistencies across paginated queries. Snapshot reads provide the strongest consistency guarantee: a billing system paginating through a month of data sees the exact same records on every page, as they existed when the query started. This is marked p3 because cursor-based pagination with stable ordering provides sufficient consistency for most use cases, but snapshot isolation is valuable when absolute consistency is required for auditing or financial reconciliation.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-billing-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-tenant-admin`\n+\n+### 5.6 Backfill & Amendment\n+\n+#### Late-Arriving Event Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-late-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept usage events with timestamps within a configurable grace period (default 24 hours, configurable per tenant and per usage type) via the standard ingestion path, applying normal deduplication and schema validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In distributed systems, clock skew, batch processing delays, and asynchronous architectures cause events to routinely arrive after their actual timestamp. A grace period allows these events to be processed without requiring explicit backfill operations.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-backfill-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** provide a dedicated backfill API that accepts a time range (scoped to a single tenant and usage type) and a set of replacement events, atomically archiving existing events in that range and inserting the new events.\n+\n+The backfill API **MUST** be separate from the real-time ingestion path with independent rate limits and lower processing priority to prevent backfill operations from starving real-time ingestion.\n+\n+**Concurrent-write strategy — Reject and Retry**: During an active backfill, the system **MUST** use range-level locking to enforce exclusive write access to the affected `(tenant, usage_type, time_range)` partition. The backfill operation acquires a lock before beginning the archive-and-insert transaction and holds it until the transaction commits or rolls back. While the lock is held:\n+\n+1. **Real-time ingestion behavior**: Any real-time event (via SDK, collector agent, or REST API as defined in `cpt-cf-uc-req-usage-ingestion`) whose `(tenant_id, usage_type, timestamp)` falls within a currently locked backfill range **MUST** be rejected with HTTP status `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_IN_PROGRESS`. The response body **MUST** include the fields `retry_after_ms` (estimated remaining backfill duration, minimum 1000) and `locked_range` (the `[start, end)` interval that is locked).\n+2. **Lock scope and overlap**: The system **MUST** reject a backfill request with HTTP `409 Conflict` and error code `BACKFILL_RANGE_OVERLAP` if its requested range overlaps with any currently locked backfill range for the same tenant and usage type. Only one backfill operation per `(tenant, usage_type)` overlapping range is permitted at a time. Non-overlapping ranges for the same tenant and usage type, or any ranges for different tenants or usage types, **MAY** execute concurrently.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When usage data is lost due to outages, pipeline failures, or misconfigured sources, operators need a mechanism to retroactively submit corrected data for an entire time range. The reject-and-retry strategy is chosen over queue-and-replay or snapshot isolation alternatives because it is the simplest model to implement correctly, avoids unbounded buffering on the server, keeps real-time ingestion latency deterministic (a fast 409 vs. an indeterminate queue wait), and pushes retry responsibility to the SDK where backpressure is already handled. Backfill operations are expected to be infrequent (operator-initiated corrections), so brief real-time rejection windows are an acceptable trade-off for strong atomicity guarantees. Timeframe-based replacement (as opposed to individual event insertion) ensures atomicity and prevents partial amendment states.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`\n+\n+#### Individual Event Amendment\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-event-amendment`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support amending individual usage events (updating properties except tenant ID and timestamp) and deprecating individual events (marking them as inactive while retaining them for audit). Downstream consumers **MUST** be able to distinguish active from deprecated records when querying.\n+\n+**Interaction with backfill**: If a backfill operation targets a time range that contains previously amended events, the backfill **MUST** archive the amended events along with all other events in that range. Amendment history is not preserved — the backfill's replacement events become the sole active record. This means backfill unconditionally supersedes any prior amendments within its range, keeping the correction model simple: amendments are for surgical fixes to individual events, while backfill is a wholesale replacement that starts from a clean slate.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Not all corrections require full timeframe backfill. Individual event amendments handle cases like incorrect resource attribution or value errors on specific events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Time Boundaries\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-backfill-boundaries`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable time boundaries for backfill operations: a maximum backfill window (default 90 days) beyond which backfill requests are rejected, and a future timestamp tolerance (default 5 minutes) to account for clock drift. Backfill requests exceeding the maximum window **MUST** require elevated authorization.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded backfill creates risks for data integrity and billing accuracy. Time boundaries constrain the blast radius of backfill operations while allowing legitimate corrections. Different limits for automated retry (grace period) vs. operator-initiated backfill (90 days) match different use cases. The 5-minute future tolerance follows Stripe's pattern for handling clock drift in distributed systems.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Event Archival\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-backfill-archival`\n+\n+When a backfill operation replaces events in a time range, the system **MUST** archive (not delete) the replaced events. Archived events **MUST** remain queryable for audit purposes but **MUST** be clearly distinguishable from active records so that downstream consumers can exclude them from their processing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Permanent deletion of replaced events destroys audit trail and makes it impossible to investigate billing disputes or reconstruct historical state.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Backfill Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-backfill-audit`\n+\n+Every backfill operation **MUST** produce an immutable audit record containing: operator identity, initiation timestamp, affected time range, affected tenant(s), number of events added/replaced/deprecated, reason or justification, and whether the operation affected an already-invoiced period.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backfill operations are high-risk changes to billing-critical data. Comprehensive audit records are essential for dispute resolution, compliance, and operational visibility.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Ledger Metadata Exposure\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-ledger-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose per-source and per-tenant metadata — including event counts, latest event timestamps (watermarks), and ingestion statistics — via API, enabling external reconciliation and observability systems to detect gaps and perform integrity checks.\n+\n+**Rationale**: While reconciliation logic is out of scope for the usage ledger, exposing the raw metadata needed for gap detection enables external systems to build reconciliation workflows. This keeps the ledger focused while not blocking operational integrity monitoring.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-monitoring-system`\n+\n+### 5.7 Type System\n+\n+#### Usage Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate all usage records against registered type schemas, rejecting invalid records with actionable error messages.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Schema validation prevents corrupt or malformed data from entering the system.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+#### Custom Unit Registration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-custom-units`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow registration of custom measuring units via API without code changes.\n+\n+Primary use cases: AI/LLM token metering (input/output tokens, custom credit units), compute metering (vCPU-hours, memory-GB-hours, GPU-hours), API request metering (calls by tenant and endpoint), storage metering (GB-hours across tiers), network transfer (bytes ingress/egress).\n+\n+When a custom measuring unit is registered, the platform operator **MUST** also register the source-to-usage-type binding in the Types Registry, declaring which sources are permitted to emit records of this type. The system **MUST NOT** accept usage records for a type that has no registered source bindings.\n+\n+**Rationale**: New resource types (AI tokens, GPU-hours) must be meterable without service redeployment. Source-to-usage-type bindings ensure that only authorized sources can emit records for each unit.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-types-registry`\n+\n+### 5.8 Rate Limiting\n+\n+#### Per-Tenant Ingestion Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-tenant ingestion rate limits with independently configurable sustained rate (events per second) and burst size parameters. Requests exceeding the rate limit **MUST** be rejected with HTTP 429 status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without per-tenant rate limiting, a single misbehaving or high-volume tenant can exhaust ingestion capacity and degrade service for all other tenants. Burst tolerance is required because usage event emission is inherently bursty (e.g., a batch job completing and emitting thousands of records at once).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Per-Source Rate Limiting\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-source-rate-limit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce per-source rate limits within each tenant, preventing a single usage source (e.g., a misconfigured LLM Gateway) from consuming the tenant's entire ingestion quota. Per-source limits **MUST** be configurable independently of the tenant-level limit.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenant-level rate limits alone do not prevent a single noisy source from starving other sources within the same tenant. Per-source limits provide fault isolation within a tenant's service portfolio.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Multi-Dimensional Rate Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-multi-dimensional-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce rate limits across multiple dimensions simultaneously: events per second, bytes per second, maximum batch size (events per request), and maximum record size (bytes per event). All dimensions **MUST** pass for a request to be accepted.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-dimension rate limits are insufficient; a tenant could comply with events/sec limits while submitting oversized payloads that exhaust network or storage bandwidth. Multi-dimensional limits protect all resource types (CPU for event processing, network for payload transfer, storage for persistence).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Configuration and Overrides\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-rate-limit-config`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support rate limit configuration with system-wide defaults and per-tenant overrides. Per-tenant overrides **MUST** be hot-reloadable without service restart. Unspecified fields in overrides **MUST** inherit from the system defaults.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different tenants have different throughput needs based on their workload profile. Hot-reloadable overrides enable operators to respond to capacity issues or tenant requests without service disruption.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-operator`\n+\n+#### Rate Limit Response Format\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-rate-limit-response`\n+\n+When rate limits are exceeded, the system **MUST** respond with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) and include a `Retry-After` header indicating when the client can retry. The system **MUST** include `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers on all API responses to enable clients to monitor their quota consumption. Header formats are defined as follows:\n+\n+- **`Retry-After`**: **MUST** be provided as `delay-seconds` — an integer representing the number of seconds the client should wait before retrying. **MAY** additionally include an `HTTP-date` value for backward compatibility with older HTTP clients.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Limit`**: **MUST** be an integer representing the total request allowance for the current rate limit window.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Remaining`**: **MUST** be an integer representing the number of requests remaining in the current rate limit window.\n+- **`X-RateLimit-Reset`**: **MUST** be an integer Unix epoch timestamp (seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z) in UTC indicating when the current rate limit window resets.\n+\n+All time-related header values are in UTC. Clients **SHOULD** parse these header values as integers and **SHOULD** treat missing or unparseable values conservatively (i.e., assume the limit is exhausted and apply a default backoff).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Standard rate limit headers (used by Stripe, GitHub, Datadog) enable clients to track quota consumption and schedule retries, reducing wasted requests against an already-exhausted quota. Explicit format definitions prevent ambiguity between delay-seconds and HTTP-date for `Retry-After`, and between epoch timestamps and other representations for `X-RateLimit-Reset`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n+\n+#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n+\n+The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429), honoring the `Retry-After` header when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-developer`","path":"modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ef00a2602100a538c41c3f48f5684cec8f9d28cd","original_commit_id":"9d1e242148d7a633f35981c23db7b7a964cfed41","user":{"login":"graphite-app[bot]","id":96075541,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOBbn_FQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/158384?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/graphite-app","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/graphite-app%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"SDK retry behavior is specified only for HTTP 429 (rate limiting) but the backfill concurrent-write strategy (lines 329-333) returns HTTP 409 with `retry_after_ms` when events fall within a locked backfill range. The SDK requirement does not specify handling for 409 responses, creating a gap where events during backfill operations may be permanently lost instead of retried.\n\n```markdown\nFix: Update the SDK retry requirement to handle both 429 and 409 responses:\n\"The SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429) and backfill conflict responses (HTTP 409), honoring the `Retry-After` header or `retry_after_ms` field when present.\"\n```\n```suggestion\n#### SDK Retry and Buffering on Rate Limit\n\n- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-uc-req-sdk-retry`\n\nThe SDK **MUST** buffer usage events in a bounded in-memory queue and retry with exponential backoff and jitter on rate limit responses (HTTP 429) and backfill conflict responses (HTTP 409), honoring the `Retry-After` header or `retry_after_ms` field when present. When the buffer is full, the SDK **MUST** drop oldest events and report the loss via metrics. The SDK **MUST NOT** block the calling service due to rate limiting.\n\n**Rationale**: Usage sources generate events regardless of collector availability. The SDK must absorb temporary rate limiting transparently, retrying without burdening the caller. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry bursts across sources. Non-blocking behavior is critical because usage emission must not degrade the source service's primary function.\n**Actors**: `cpt-cf-uc-actor-usage-source`, `cpt-cf-uc-actor-platform-developer`\n```\n  \n\n*Spotted by [Graphite Agent](https://app.graphite.com/diamond/?org=cyberfabric&ref=ai-review-comment)*<i class='graphite__hidden'><br /><br /><a href=\"https://app.graphite.com/github/pr/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/536?chatWithGeneratedComment=a73e0162-10c8-4e8e-96a2-7c4f498a1977\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Fix in Graphite\" src=\"https://static.graphite.dev/github-diamond-fix-in-graphite-dark.svg\"></picture></a></i><i class='graphite__hidden'><br /><br />Is this helpful? React 👍 or 👎 to let us know.</i>","created_at":"2026-02-12T15:15:33Z","updated_at":"2026-02-12T15:15:34Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2799471261","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2799471261"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#discussion_r2799471261"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2799471261/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":471,"original_start_line":460,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":478,"original_line":467,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":467,"position":478,"subject_type":"line"}],"source_reviews":[{"id":3777110342,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7hIiFG","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 8**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md`:\n- Line 614: Update the Markdown heading levels for the use case entries so they\nincrement correctly under the \"## 8. Use Cases\" section: replace the h4 headings\n(####) for each use case title such as \"UC: High-Volume Usage Emission via SDK\"\nand the other listed use case headings with h3 headings (###) so they sit\ndirectly beneath the h2 section; ensure all occurrences mentioned (the headings\nat lines referenced in the review) are changed consistently from #### to ###.\n- Around line 302-309: Update the PRD to explicitly define how individual event\namendments (fdd-uc-req-event-amendment) interact with backfill archival: state\nthat an amended event may later be archived during a backfill, that archival\ndoes not delete amendment history but instead marks the original event as\n\"archived/replaced\" while retaining its amendment records and timestamps, and\nthat amendment and archival are not mutually exclusive states but sequential\nflags that can coexist (e.g., amended → archived); ensure the audit model\nrecords the full sequence (original event, amendment entries, archival entry\nwith backfill id and replacement mapping) and reference the actor\nfdd-uc-actor-platform-operator for permitted operations.\n- Line 285: The PRD currently contradicts itself by stating concrete defaults\n(\"default 24 hours\" and \"default 90 days\") in the functional requirements\nsentence containing \"The system MUST accept usage events...\" while the Open\nQuestions section still lists those same values as \"proposed\"; update the\ndocument to be consistent by either (A) removing the \"proposed\" entries from the\nOpen Questions (lines referencing \"Default grace period duration (proposed 24\nhours...)\" and \"Maximum backfill window (proposed 90 days)\") if the defaults are\nconfirmed, or (B) changing the requirement text that contains \"default 24 hours,\nconfigurable per tenant and per usage type\" and the \"default 90 days\" occurrence\nto prefix them with \"proposed default\" or \"to be determined\" so the requirements\nmatch the unresolved status in the Open Questions; ensure you modify the exact\nstrings \"default 24 hours\" and \"default 90 days\" and the Open Questions entries\nso both places convey the same confirmed or proposed status.\n- Line 144: The document is inconsistent about \"Pull-based collection\": it\nappears under \"Out of Scope\" and also as a P2 functional requirement; reconcile\nby choosing one of three fixes and applying it to both places—either remove the\n\"Pull-based collection\" entry from the Out of Scope section if you intend it as\na P2 for initial release, or remove the detailed P2 functional requirement (the\n\"Pull-based collection\" block) if it is truly deferred, or explicitly state in\nboth the Out of Scope section and the P2 functional requirement block that P2\nitems (including \"Pull-based collection\") are planned for post-launch\nprioritization; update the text for the phrase \"Pull-based collection\" and both\nthe Out of Scope and P2 functional requirement sections so they convey the same\nscope/priority.\n- Around line 416-423: Update requirement fdd-uc-req-sdk-retry to avoid silent\nloss of billing-critical events by replacing the blanket \"drop oldest events\"\nbehavior with a defined overflow strategy: preserve billing-critical events\n(exactly-once semantics) via one or more of these options—priority-aware\ndropping (evict only low-priority events), bounded disk spillover, or explicit\nbackpressure/fail-fast for billing events—and ensure the SDK exposes a\nbackpressure/circuit-breaker callback and metric/error signals when high-water\nmarks are reached; document which approach applies to billing vs analytics\nevents and how Retry-After, exponential backoff with jitter, and metric\nreporting interact with the chosen overflow policy.\n- Around line 707-730: Add a clear functional requirement and integration\ncontract for tracking invoiced/closed billing periods so the Usage Collector can\ndetect overlap with invoiced windows: add a new requirement \"Billing Period\nClosure Tracking\" with ID fdd-uc-req-billing-period-tracking that mandates the\nsystem MUST track closed/invoiced billing periods and prohibit unacknowledged\nbackfills on them; describe supported mechanisms (Billing System push\nnotifications, UC pull API, or operator/admin API/Registry) and specify the\ncontract details (API endpoints/events, auth, idempotency, SLA/latency, and\nreconciliation semantics) and whether notifications are push or pull; update\nsection 7.2 External Integration Contracts to include the Billing System\ncontract and update Dependencies (section 10) to list the Billing System or\nBilling Period Registry, and reference this requirement from the \"Backfill Usage\nAfter Outage\" flow where \"UC checks if time range overlaps with an\nalready-invoiced period.\"\n- Around line 737-741: In the PRD.md \"Main Flow\" section rename the bullet\nnumbers so they are sequential (no skip): make the list read 1. Administrator\nrequests usage for time period, 2. UC queries aggregated usage for tenant only,\n3. UC returns usage breakdown by type and resource, 4. Administrator reviews\nconsumption (i.e., decrement the current \"4\" to \"3\" and \"5\" to \"4\"). Update the\nnumbered items under the \"Main Flow\" header accordingly so there is no missing\nstep number.\n- Around line 291-300: Update the fdd-uc-req-backfill-api requirement to\nexplicitly state concurrent-write semantics: pick one strategy (e.g., \"Reject\nreal-time events\" with a defined error code and retry guidance, or \"Queue and\nreplay\" with buffering guarantees, or a specific transaction isolation level\nlike \"snapshot isolation/serializable\", or \"Backfill-first deterministic\nordering\") and describe exactly how real-time ingestion (the real-time path)\nmust behave during an active backfill, including API/SDK behavior, error codes,\nretry/backoff expectations, and how atomicity is achieved across the archive +\ninsert phases referenced in \"atomically archive and insert\". Ensure the text\nreferences fdd-uc-req-backfill-api and clarifies interaction with the real-time\ningestion path so implementers know the required isolation/ordering model.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`424-432`: **Document priority classification scheme before implementation.**\n\nThe priority-based load shedding requirement (lines 424-432) depends on classifying usage event types by priority (e.g., billing-critical vs. analytics), but line 799 in Open Questions indicates this classification hasn't been defined yet.\n\nSince this is a P2 requirement, the priority scheme can be designed during implementation. However, consider documenting the classification criteria in the PRD to guide implementation:\n- What makes an event type \"billing-critical\" vs. \"analytics\"?\n- How many priority levels are needed? (P0, P1, P2 or High/Medium/Low?)\n- Are priorities mutable per tenant, or global per usage type?\n- Default priority for newly registered custom units?\n\n\n\n\nAdd a subsection under 5.8 Rate Limiting defining the priority classification scheme, or create a separate section if this applies beyond rate limiting.\n\n---\n\n`369-441`: **Consider specifying rate limit distribution strategy.**\n\nThe rate limiting requirements (lines 369-441) are comprehensive but don't specify how rate limits are enforced in a horizontally scaled deployment. Line 797 in Open Questions acknowledges this: \"Rate limit algorithm distribution strategy for horizontally-scaled ingestion nodes (local per-node vs. centralized counter).\"\n\nWhile this can be resolved during implementation, consider documenting the trade-offs in the PRD to guide architectural decisions:\n\n**Local per-node limiting**:\n- ✅ Lower latency, no external coordination\n- ❌ Less accurate (N instances each allow full limit, multiplying effective capacity by N)\n- ❌ Uneven distribution if load balancer doesn't distribute evenly\n\n**Centralized counter (e.g., Redis)**:\n- ✅ Accurate global limits regardless of instance count\n- ✅ Even enforcement across instances\n- ❌ Added latency for counter coordination\n- ❌ External dependency (Redis/similar) required\n- ❌ Becomes a single point of failure without HA\n\n**Hybrid approach**:\n- Local rate limiting for fast rejection of obvious violations\n- Periodic synchronization with central authority for accurate enforcement\n\n\n\n\nGiven the billing-critical nature of usage data and the NFR for exactly-once semantics, centralized rate limiting with a resilient backing store (Redis Cluster or similar) is recommended to prevent the \"multiplication effect\" where per-node limits effectively multiply by instance count.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#pullrequestreview-3777110342","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#pullrequestreview-3777110342"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-10T07:10:20Z","commit_id":"b39c610c07ae8f9637dd37c10f81a41360e7ea53"},{"id":3777353097,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7hJdWJ","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 2**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md`:\n- Around line 153-156: The PRD currently has conflicting throughput targets for\nthe usage ingestion requirement `fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion` (one place says\n10,000+ events/sec, another says 100,000 records/min); update the document to\npick a single clear throughput spec or explicitly define both “sustained” and\n“burst” rates (e.g., sustained X events/sec and burst Y events/sec with duration\nand frequency), and apply the chosen numbers consistently wherever\n`fdd-uc-req-usage-ingestion` or the usage ingestion throughput is referenced\n(including the later section that currently states 100,000 records/min) so\narchitecture and capacity planning use the same target.\n- Around line 409-410: Update the PRD text to explicitly define the formats and\nsemantics for the four headers: state that Retry-After MUST be provided as\ndelay-seconds (integer seconds to wait) and MAY include an HTTP-date for\nbackward compatibility, X-RateLimit-Reset MUST be a Unix epoch timestamp in UTC\n(integer seconds indicating the reset time), and X-RateLimit-Limit and\nX-RateLimit-Remaining MUST be integer counts (total allowance and remaining\ntokens); also add a short note that clients should parse these as integers and\ntreat missing/invalid values conservatively and clarify that all times are in\nUTC.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#pullrequestreview-3777353097","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#pullrequestreview-3777353097"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-10T08:10:57Z","commit_id":"c471acf48bd6a8bc2c93081ce13dd24045d2446d"},{"id":3779121283,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7hQNCD","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 2**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md`:\n- Line 790: Replace the open-question bullet \"Rate limit algorithm distribution\nstrategy for horizontally-scaled ingestion nodes (local per-node vs. centralized\ncounter)\" with a definitive choice moved into the functional requirements\nsection: pick one of the recommended strategies (Distributed rate limiting with\nshared state e.g., Redis token-bucket, Sticky routing with per-tenant node\nassignment, or a Hybrid local fast-path + periodic global sync), state which\noption you chose, and add a short justification and trade-offs showing how it\nsatisfies fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit and fdd-uc-req-scalability (include\nimplementation notes like use of TTL caching, coordination points, or sticky\nrouting mechanism and failure modes). Ensure the new text references the\noriginal phrasing \"Rate limit algorithm distribution strategy for\nhorizontally-scaled ingestion nodes\" and documents operational impacts,\nconsistency guarantees, and fallback behavior.\n- Around line 206-209: Update the PRD requirement \"attribute all usage records\nto a tenant derived from security context\" to include explicit error-handling\nsemantics: specify that when the security context is missing, malformed, or\nlacks a tenant ID the system MUST reject the request with a defined HTTP status\n(401 for authentication failure or 400 for malformed request) and a documented\nmachine-readable error code; state whether missing tenant context is treated as\nauthentication (401) or bad request (400); and clarify that this behaviour\napplies uniformly to all ingestion paths (SDK, collector agent, REST API).\nReference the requirement text and actor identifier fdd-uc-actor-usage-source\nwhen adding these clauses.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`291-303`: **Clarify backfill idempotency semantics.**\n\nLine 293 mentions \"backfill idempotency namespace\" but does not define the idempotency behavior for repeated backfill requests. If an operator submits the same backfill request twice (same time range, same tenant, same replacement events), the current specification suggests:\n\n1. First request: archives existing events (if any), inserts new events\n2. Second request: archives the events inserted by the first request, inserts the same events again, creating duplicate backfill audit records\n\n**Recommendation**: Explicitly define backfill idempotency by adding one of:\n\n**Option 1 (Idempotent)**: Require clients to provide a backfill-specific idempotency key. If the same key is resubmitted, return the original backfill operation result without re-executing the archive-and-insert.\n\n**Option 2 (Non-idempotent with prevention)**: Each backfill request is distinct. To prevent accidental re-backfilling, require the client to specify the expected number of events to be archived; reject with `409 Conflict` if the current event count doesn't match, forcing explicit operator review.\n\n**Option 3 (Content-addressed idempotency)**: Hash the combination of `(tenant, usage_type, time_range, replacement_events)` to create a deterministic backfill ID. Identical requests are deduplicated automatically.\n\nDocument the chosen approach in the requirement and update line 719 in the use case to reflect the idempotency behavior.\n\n---\n\n`162-170`: **Consider adding concrete details to the pull-based collection P2 requirement.**\n\nThe pull-based collection requirement is notably less detailed than other P2 requirements in the document. While line 166 acknowledges that \"Initial implementation focuses on push patterns\" and pull-based collection \"will be added when concrete use cases are identified,\" the requirement lacks:\n\n- API design for configuring and managing pull sources\n- Definition of \"configurable intervals\" (cron-like schedules? fixed intervals? adaptive polling?)\n- Specification of \"transformation to standard format\" (which formats are supported? is transformation pluggable?)\n- Acceptance criteria for what constitutes successful pull-based collection\n- A corresponding use case in Section 8\n\n**Recommendation**: If pull-based collection is deferred until concrete integration requirements arise, consider either:\n1. Downgrading it from a formal P2 requirement to a \"Future Consideration\" section\n2. Adding placeholder API contracts and acceptance criteria that can be refined when specific pull sources are identified (e.g., \"Pull adapter interface must support: schedule configuration, state persistence for cursor/offset, error handling, backpressure\")\n\nThis will help future implementers understand the expected scope and design constraints.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#pullrequestreview-3779121283","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#pullrequestreview-3779121283"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-10T13:55:36Z","commit_id":"7411662652a0028fdb2b1287927362b7e2d682e2"},{"id":3779875751,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7hTFOn","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 2**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/system/usage_collector/docs/PRD.md`:\n- Line 791: Move the open question at line 791 into the functional requirements\nsection and choose one concrete distribution strategy (pick one: \"Distributed\nrate limiting with shared state (Redis token-bucket + TTL caching)\", \"Sticky\nrouting (per-tenant node assignment)\", or \"Hybrid: local fast-path + periodic\nglobal sync\"); update the PRD to state the chosen strategy, document trade-offs\nand failure modes, and explicitly map how it satisfies or mitigates the\nrequirements fdd-uc-req-tenant-rate-limit and fdd-uc-req-scalability (include\nhow accuracy, coordination overhead, fault tolerance, and scalability are\nhandled).\n- Line 29: Update the inconsistent throughput numbers in the PRD: locate the\n\"Goals\" statement (line with \"High-volume services can emit 10,000+ events per\nsecond\"), the \"Functional Requirement\" entry (the line stating \"10,000+ events\nper second\"), and the \"Non-Functional Requirement\" entry (the line \"100,000\nusage records per minute\") and make them consistent by either (A) unifying all\nthree to a single agreed throughput value, or (B) explicitly defining two clear\nmetrics — e.g., \"sustained rate: X events/sec (Y events/min)\" and \"burst rate: Z\nevents/sec (W events/min)\" — and update each of the three locations to reference\nthese terms and values consistently so capacity/planning decisions use the same\nnumbers.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#pullrequestreview-3779875751","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/536#pullrequestreview-3779875751"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/536"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-10T15:49:58Z","commit_id":"59c81847aced9792e7622eb10ad6eef3085041da"}]},{"source_pr_number":535,"source_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/535","source_state":"closed","source_author":"MikeFalcon77","target_issue_number":null,"title":"[PR #535] feat(modkit-auth): add outbound OAuth2 client-credentials token + bearer auth layer","status":"exported","mirrored_at":null,"comments_status":"none","comments_mirrored":0,"pr_head_ref":"feature/263-modkit-auth-phase0-1","pr_head_sha":"4498f35da206c75bcd6f33785fa0327d9533d6bb","pr_base_ref":"main","pr_head_owner":"MikeFalcon77","pr_from_fork":1,"source_data":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pu