\n>Test common suffix alias with required header\n>\n><a href='https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-4c2eeebfac7042273dc07316c604f97b7aae30bd5b1a347d89d114aecbaf4d98'> modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/positive-3.1-multi-endpoint-common-suffix-with-header.md\n </a>\n<hr/>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>13. modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/positive-3.2-case-insensitive-matching.md\n <code>🧪 Tests</code> <code> +31/-0 </code>  </summary>  \n\n<br/>\n\n>Test case-insensitive header matching\n>\n><a href='https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-3c7a29173be2d2a000d880c6c66ad94450ea0b6d382bcf87fb13f1931c27bc11'> modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/positive-3.2-case-insensitive-matching.md\n </a>\n<hr/>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>14. modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/positive-3.3-load-balancing-bypass.md\n <code>🧪 Tests</code> <code> +45/-0 </code>  </summary>  \n\n<br/>\n\n>Test header bypasses load balancing\n>\n><a href='https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-5357bef0320fef719ee580132044bff7136acb67e9bfcd08a8c33c7acf533af7'> modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/positive-3.3-load-balancing-bypass.md\n </a>\n<hr/>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>15. modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-1.1-missing-header-common-suffix.md\n <code>🧪 Tests</code> <code> +46/-0 </code>  </summary>  \n\n<br/>\n\n>Test missing header for common suffix alias\n>\n><a href='https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-c9ba5e633b16a49f561545ee93d746097a5b7549c296b5145505cd2df106fdb3'> modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-1.1-missing-header-common-suffix.md\n </a>\n<hr/>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>16. modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-1.2-invalid-format-with-port.md\n <code>🧪 Tests</code> <code> +46/-0 </code>  </summary>  \n\n<br/>\n\n>Test invalid header format with port number\n>\n><a href='https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-100225a641656222f9a3b9f73b3de37b39d236b2f1ad78f5d3122121781ccfef'> modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-1.2-invalid-format-with-port.md\n </a>\n<hr/>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>17. modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-1.3-invalid-format-with-path.md\n <code>🧪 Tests</code> <code> +46/-0 </code>  </summary>  \n\n<br/>\n\n>Test invalid header format with path component\n>\n><a href='https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-b818d376090e42b7b9e6d6590f65c569f9b325b34477081f067167770a212690'> modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-1.3-invalid-format-with-path.md\n </a>\n<hr/>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>18. modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-1.4-invalid-format-special-chars.md\n <code>🧪 Tests</code> <code> +46/-0 </code>  </summary>  \n\n<br/>\n\n>Test invalid header format with special characters\n>\n><a href='https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-627dbad61fcd66ae7765ea4e6c2634956eefa1c04642e3d8e37d611ea53bd7a3'> modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-1.4-invalid-format-special-chars.md\n </a>\n<hr/>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>19. modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-2.1-unknown-host.md\n <code>🧪 Tests</code> <code> +47/-0 </code>  </summary>  \n\n<br/>\n\n>Test unknown host not in endpoint list\n>\n><a href='https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-792c3f656574f1b67799d5f1cac6ce8214864118b861136dc910a46bd4965839'> modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-2.1-unknown-host.md\n </a>\n<hr/>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>20. modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-2.2-ip-address-when-hostname-expected.md\n <code>🧪 Tests</code> <code> +47/-0 </code>  </summary>  \n\n<br/>\n\n>Test IP address mismatch with hostname endpoints\n>\n><a href='https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-83715735b5e05f8007964878960be053d23ec80bbc3af12cf71c7f9d5fb47f84'> modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-2.2-ip-address-when-hostname-expected.md\n </a>\n<hr/>\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n<img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/light-grey-line.svg\" height=\"10%\" alt=\"Grey Divider\">\n\n\n<a href=\"https://www.qodo.ai\"><img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/qodo-logo.svg\" width=\"80\" alt=\"Qodo Logo\"></a>","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/comments/3870818933/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":{"id":484649,"client_id":"Iv1.8b06d3cf5deea057","slug":"qodo-code-review","node_id":"A_kwHOCuzIt84AB2Up","owner":{"login":"qodo-ai","id":183290039,"node_id":"O_kgDOCuzItw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/183290039?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai","html_url":"https://github.com/qodo-ai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/received_events","type":"Organization","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"name":"Qodo Code Review","description":"### The AI Code Review Platform\r\n\r\n## Get Started\r\nSimply sign into Github and click the \"Configure\" button. Check out our documentation for more info: https://docs.qodo.ai/get-started.\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\nQodo automatically reviews every GitHub pull request, identifies logic bugs, edge cases, security issues, and code quality problems, and provides actionable suggestions your team can apply immediately.\r\n\r\nInstall in minutes. Integrates seamlessly into your existing workflows.\r\n\r\n## Qodo is designed for developers who:\r\n- Handle frequent pull requests and need reliable review coverage\r\n- Balance code quality with fast delivery\r\n- Work in complex, multi-repo systems\r\n- Use AI-generated code and need stronger guardrails\r\n- Build production software where reliability matters\r\n\r\n## Qodo Capabilities\r\n- ## Automated PR Analysis\r\n  - Reviews every pull request automatically\r\n  - No manual trigger required\r\n\r\n- ## Context-Aware Code Review\r\n  - Understands cross-file changes\r\n  - Detects architectural inconsistencies\r\n\r\n- ## Actionable Suggestions\r\n  - Concrete code fixes\r\n  - Clear explanations\r\n  - Prioritized feedback\r\n\r\n- ## Security & Reliability Checks\r\n  - Identifies risky patterns\r\n  - Reduces attack surface area","external_url":"https://www.qodo.ai/","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/qodo-code-review","created_at":"2023-11-16T16:18:38Z","updated_at":"2026-05-06T15:53:40Z","permissions":{"actions":"read","checks":"read","contents":"write","discussions":"write","issues":"write","metadata":"read","pull_requests":"write"},"events":["check_run","check_suite","discussion_comment","issue_comment","pull_request","pull_request_review_comment","push"]}},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/comments/3870865899","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/527#issuecomment-3870865899","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/527","id":3870865899,"node_id":"IC_kwDOPaLyBs7muLnr","user":{"login":"qodo-code-review[bot]","id":151058649,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCQD42Q","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/484649?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/qodo-code-review","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-code-review%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-02-09T10:34:11Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T12:30:19Z","body":"<h3>Code Review by Qodo</h3>\n\n<code>🐞 Bugs (5)</code>  <code>📘 Rule violations (1)</code>  <code>📎 Requirement gaps (0)</code>\n\n<img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/light-grey-line.svg\" height=\"10%\" alt=\"Grey Divider\">\n\n<br/>\n\n<img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/action-required.png\" height=\"20\" alt=\"Action required\">\n\n<details>\n<summary>  1.  Routing pseudocode incomplete <code>🐞 Bug</code> <code>✓ Correctness</code></summary>\n\n<br/>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Description</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>• Alias-resolution pseudocode validates <b><i>X-OAGW-Target-Host</i></b> only inside the\n><b><i>len(selected.endpoints) &gt; 1</i></b> block, so single-endpoint upstreams never get validated in this\n>algorithm. • Even for multi-endpoint upstreams, <b><i>matched_endpoint</i></b> is computed but never applied to\n>routing/selection, conflicting with scenarios requiring explicit endpoint selection and\n>load-balancing bypass. • This is likely to mislead implementers and cause scenario/test failures.\n></pre>\n></details>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Code</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[R465-502]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-f4fdd5834a964c7cd99fdd61c8ca9d8366cf65565894c59f592ecc606d139760R465-R502)</code>\n>\n>```diff\n>+    # Multiple endpoints with common suffix alias require X-OAGW-Target-Host header\n>    if len(selected.endpoints) > 1:\n>        has_common_suffix = any(\n>            ep.host != alias and ep.host.endswith(\".\" + alias)\n>            for ep in selected.endpoints\n>        )\n>-        if has_common_suffix and \"Host\" not in req.headers:\n>-            return Response(status=400)  # Missing Host header\n>+        if has_common_suffix and \"X-OAGW-Target-Host\" not in req.headers:\n>+            return ErrorResponse(\n>+                status=400,\n>+                type=\"gts.x.core.errors.err.v1~x.oagw.routing.missing_target_host.v1\",\n>+                detail=f\"X-OAGW-Target-Host header required. Valid hosts: {[ep.host for ep in selected.endpoints]}\"\n>+            )\n>+\n>+        # Validate X-OAGW-Target-Host header if present\n>+        if \"X-OAGW-Target-Host\" in req.headers:\n>+            target_host = req.headers[\"X-OAGW-Target-Host\"]\n>+\n>+            # Format validation: must be valid hostname or IP (no port, path, or special chars)\n>+            if not is_valid_hostname_or_ip(target_host):\n>+                return ErrorResponse(\n>+                    status=400,\n>+                    type=\"gts.x.core.errors.err.v1~x.oagw.routing.invalid_target_host.v1\",\n>+                    detail=\"X-OAGW-Target-Host must be a valid hostname or IP address\"\n>+                )\n>+\n>+            # Allowlist validation: must match one of the endpoint hosts (case-insensitive)\n>+            matched_endpoint = None\n>+            for ep in selected.endpoints:\n>+                if ep.host.lower() == target_host.lower():\n>+                    matched_endpoint = ep\n>+                    break\n>+\n>+            if matched_endpoint is None:\n>+                return ErrorResponse(\n>+                    status=400,\n>+                    type=\"gts.x.core.errors.err.v1~x.oagw.routing.unknown_target_host.v1\",\n>+                    detail=f\"X-OAGW-Target-Host '{target_host}' does not match any endpoint. Valid hosts: {[ep.host for ep in selected.endpoints]}\"\n>+                )\n>```\n></details>\n\n> <details >\n><summary>Evidence</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>The pseudocode nests all header validation inside the multi-endpoint branch, and never uses\n><b><i>matched_endpoint</i></b> to affect routing, while scenarios require explicit selection via the header\n>(including for single-endpoint validation).\n></pre>\n>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[465-503]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md/#L465-L503)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/positive-2.2-multi-endpoint-explicit-alias-with-header.md[20-29]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/positive-2.2-multi-endpoint-explicit-alias-with-header.md/#L20-L29)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/positive-1.2-single-endpoint-with-header.md[23-32]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/positive-1.2-single-endpoint-with-header.md/#L23-L32)</code>\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Agent prompt</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n>```\n>The issue below was found during a code review. Follow the provided context and guidance below and implement a solution\n>\n>## Issue description\n>The Alias Resolution pseudocode currently (a) scopes header validation under `len(endpoints) &amp;gt; 1`, skipping single-endpoint validation, and (b) computes `matched_endpoint` without applying it, despite scenarios requiring explicit endpoint selection.\n>## Issue Context\n>Scenarios under `scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/` expect the header to be validated for single-endpoint upstreams and to select a specific endpoint for multi-endpoint upstreams.\n>## Fix Focus Areas\n>- modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[458-513]\n>```\n> <code>ⓘ Copy this prompt and use it to remediate the issue with your preferred AI generation tools</code>\n></details>\n\n<hr/>\n</details>\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>  2.  Disabled upstream mismatch <code>🐞 Bug</code> <code>✓ Correctness</code></summary>\n\n<br/>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Description</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>• PRD/scenarios define “disabled upstream” as a gateway rejection (scenario expects 503), but the\n>DESIGN “Find Upstream by Alias” query filters disabled upstreams out, which implies alias-not-found\n>behavior. • Without a single defined HTTP status + Problem <b><i>type</i></b> for disabled upstreams, clients\n>will interpret the same state differently (404 vs 503). • Recommend defining a stable error mapping\n>(status + type) and aligning PRD, DESIGN queries, and scenarios.\n></pre>\n></details>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Code</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><code>[modules/system/oagw/PRD.md[R104-115]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-bb1245bb0699cb8248aaefaebecad30b8dfa41c3c230af0a71e82f5129035ffdR104-R115)</code>\n>\n>```diff\n>+### Enable/Disable Semantics\n>+\n>+Upstreams and routes support an `enabled` boolean field (default: `true`).\n>+\n>+**Behavior**:\n>+- **Disabled upstream**: All proxy requests to this upstream rejected with gateway error\n>+- **Disabled route**: Route excluded from matching; request falls through to next match or returns 404\n>+\n>+**Hierarchical Inheritance**:\n>+- If ancestor tenant disables an upstream, it is disabled for all descendants\n>+- Descendant cannot re-enable an ancestor-disabled resource\n>+\n>```\n></details>\n\n> <details >\n><summary>Evidence</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>The PRD introduces enabled/disabled behavior, scenarios assert 503 when disabled, while the DESIGN\n>query excludes disabled rows entirely (which would typically surface as not-found).\n></pre>\n>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/PRD.md[104-110]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/PRD.md/#L104-L110)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-api/upstreams/positive-2.11-re-enable-upstream-restores-proxy-traffic.md[65-74]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-api/upstreams/positive-2.11-re-enable-upstream-restores-proxy-traffic.md/#L65-L74)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[2527-2545]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md/#L2527-L2545)</code>\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Agent prompt</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n>```\n>The issue below was found during a code review. Follow the provided context and guidance below and implement a solution\n>\n>## Issue description\n>The spec currently conflicts on how a disabled upstream is surfaced at proxy time (explicit gateway error like 503 vs effectively not-found due to `enabled=TRUE` filtering).\n>## Issue Context\n>Scenarios and PRD imply operators can temporarily disable an upstream and receive a distinct gateway rejection. DESIGN query examples currently filter disabled upstreams out.\n>## Fix Focus Areas\n>- modules/system/oagw/PRD.md[104-115]\n>- modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[2524-2546]\n>- modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-api/upstreams/positive-2.11-re-enable-upstream-restores-proxy-traffic.md[65-74]\n>```\n> <code>ⓘ Copy this prompt and use it to remediate the issue with your preferred AI generation tools</code>\n></details>\n\n<hr/>\n</details>\n\n\n<br/>\n\n<img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/review-recommended.png\" height=\"20\" alt=\"Remediation recommended\">\n\n<details>\n<summary>  3.  <b><i>valid_hosts</i></b> leaked in errors <code>📘 Rule violation</code> <code>⛨ Security</code></summary>\n\n<br/>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Description</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>• The updated OAGW error design includes user-facing Problem Details fields and <b><i>detail</i></b> strings\n>that enumerate valid upstream endpoint hosts and include internal identifiers (e.g., <b><i>upstream_id</i></b>).\n>• Exposing endpoint allowlists/topology and internal IDs to external callers can leak sensitive\n>implementation details and expand an attacker’s reconnaissance surface. • This information should be\n>confined to secure internal logs/telemetry (e.g., keyed by <b><i>trace_id</i></b>) while keeping client-facing\n>errors generic.\n></pre>\n></details>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Code</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[R2095-2104]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-f4fdd5834a964c7cd99fdd61c8ca9d8366cf65565894c59f592ecc606d139760R2095-R2104)</code>\n>\n>```diff\n>+{\n>+  \"type\": \"gts.x.core.errors.err.v1~x.oagw.routing.missing_target_host.v1\",\n>+  \"title\": \"Missing Target Host Header\",\n>+  \"status\": 400,\n>+  \"detail\": \"X-OAGW-Target-Host header required for multi-endpoint upstream with common suffix alias. Valid hosts: [us.vendor.com, eu.vendor.com]\",\n>+  \"instance\": \"/api/oagw/v1/proxy/vendor.com/v1/api/resource\",\n>+  \"upstream_id\": \"gts.x.core.oagw.upstream.v1~7c9e6679-7425-40de-944b-e07fc1f90ae7\",\n>+  \"alias\": \"vendor.com\",\n>+  \"valid_hosts\": [\"us.vendor.com\", \"eu.vendor.com\"],\n>+  \"trace_id\": \"01J...\"\n>```\n></details>\n\n> <details >\n><summary>Evidence</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>PR Compliance ID 4 requires user-facing errors to avoid exposing internal system details, and PR\n>Compliance ID 6 requires sensitive/internal data to be handled without exposure. In <b><i>DESIGN.md</i></b>, the\n>proposed Problem Details payloads and pseudocode explicitly include lists of valid endpoint hosts\n>and internal IDs in the response body/<b><i>detail</i></b>, which would be presented to end users/clients if\n>implemented as specified.\n></pre>\n>\n> <code>Rule 4: Generic: Secure Error Handling</code>\n> <code>Rule 6: Generic: Security-First Input Validation and Data Handling</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[2095-2104]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md/#L2095-L2104)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[471-476]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md/#L471-L476)</code>\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Agent prompt</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n>```\n>The issue below was found during a code review. Follow the provided context and guidance below and implement a solution\n>\n>## Issue description\n>The design specifies user-facing error responses that disclose internal routing topology (`valid_hosts` / endpoint allowlists) and internal identifiers (e.g., `upstream_id`) in Problem Details responses and in `detail` messages.\n>## Issue Context\n>Compliance requires that user-facing errors not expose internal implementation details and that sensitive/internal data not be exposed externally. The current design examples/pseudocode appear to encourage returning internal details directly to clients.\n>## Fix Focus Areas\n>- modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[471-502]\n>- modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[2086-2145]\n>```\n> <code>ⓘ Copy this prompt and use it to remediate the issue with your preferred AI generation tools</code>\n></details>\n\n<hr/>\n</details>\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>  4.  Scenario conflict remains <code>🐞 Bug</code> <code>⛯ Reliability</code></summary>\n\n<br/>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Description</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>• scenarios/INDEX.md now describes common-suffix alias routing as requiring <b><i>X-OAGW-Target-Host</i></b>,\n>but it still links to an older scenario file whose content requires the <b><i>Host</i></b> header. • Even with\n>the “superseded” note, this creates confusing, conflicting acceptance criteria and can break\n>automated scenario-to-test mapping. • Recommend updating or removing the Host-based scenario\n>reference (or clearly marking it deprecated and moving it out of the main index).\n></pre>\n></details>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Code</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><code>[modules/system/oagw/scenarios/INDEX.md[R337-345]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-44307139cdaa663206189dca1b50a80f65f4d3007ee635772c217b12ea22a83bR337-R345)</code>\n>\n>```diff\n>+### [x] 6.3 Justification: Multi-endpoint common-suffix alias requires `X-OAGW-Target-Host` header\n>+- Scenario: [`negative-6.3-multi-endpoint-common-suffix-alias-requires-host-header.md`](proxy-api/alias-resolution/negative-6.3-multi-endpoint-common-suffix-alias-requires-host-header.md) *(superseded by section 6.5)*\n>- Why it matters:\n>  - Avoid ambiguous endpoint selection and SSRF-by-host-header tricks.\n>- What to check:\n>  - For alias `vendor.com` backed by endpoints `us.vendor.com`, `eu.vendor.com`:\n>-    - Missing inbound `Host` yields `400 HOST_HEADER_REQUIRED` (or defined error), `ESrc=gateway`.\n>-    - With `Host: us.vendor.com` routes to that endpoint.\n>-    - With `Host` not in pool rejects.\n>+    - Missing routing header yields `400` error, `ESrc=gateway`.\n>+    - With valid routing header routes to that endpoint.\n>+    - With routing header not in pool rejects.\n>```\n></details>\n\n> <details >\n><summary>Evidence</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>The index claims X-OAGW-Target-Host semantics but points to a scenario whose title/content is\n>explicitly Host-header-based.\n></pre>\n>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/scenarios/INDEX.md[337-345]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/scenarios/INDEX.md/#L337-L345)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/alias-resolution/negative-6.3-multi-endpoint-common-suffix-alias-requires-host-header.md[1-23]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/alias-resolution/negative-6.3-multi-endpoint-common-suffix-alias-requires-host-header.md/#L1-L23)</code>\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Agent prompt</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n>```\n>The issue below was found during a code review. Follow the provided context and guidance below and implement a solution\n>\n>## Issue description\n>The scenario index and a linked scenario file describe different steering mechanisms (X-OAGW-Target-Host vs Host header). This creates conflicting acceptance criteria.\n>## Issue Context\n>A &amp;quot;superseded&amp;quot; note helps, but the index entry still actively links to the Host-header scenario and describes it as if it matched the new behavior.\n>## Fix Focus Areas\n>- modules/system/oagw/scenarios/INDEX.md[337-345]\n>- modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/alias-resolution/negative-6.3-multi-endpoint-common-suffix-alias-requires-host-header.md[1-34]\n>```\n> <code>ⓘ Copy this prompt and use it to remediate the issue with your preferred AI generation tools</code>\n></details>\n\n<hr/>\n</details>\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>  5.  Metrics labels conflict <code>🐞 Bug</code> <code>✓ Correctness</code></summary>\n\n<br/>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Description</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>• Newly added routing metrics use <b><i>{upstream_id, endpoint_host}</i></b> labels, but the same document’s\n>cardinality guidance says to use hostname instead of UUID for readability. • This inconsistency will\n>complicate dashboarding/alerting and risks high-cardinality metrics if upstream IDs are numerous. •\n>Recommend standardizing routing metrics on the same label conventions as other metrics (e.g.,\n><b><i>{host, endpoint_host, selection_method}</i></b>) or updating the cardinality rules explicitly.\n></pre>\n></details>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Code</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[R2725-2734]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-f4fdd5834a964c7cd99fdd61c8ca9d8366cf65565894c59f592ecc606d139760R2725-R2734)</code>\n>\n>```diff\n>+**Routing Metrics**:\n>+\n>+```promql\n>+# X-OAGW-Target-Host header usage for explicit endpoint selection\n>+oagw_routing_target_host_used{upstream_id, endpoint_host} counter\n>+# Tracks when X-OAGW-Target-Host header is used to bypass load balancing\n>+\n>+# Multi-endpoint routing decisions\n>+oagw_routing_endpoint_selected{upstream_id, endpoint_host, selection_method} counter\n>+# selection_method: \"explicit_header\", \"round_robin\", \"default\"\n>```\n></details>\n\n> <details >\n><summary>Evidence</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>The document introduces routing metrics labeled by UUID-ish upstream_id, but later mandates hostname\n>labels instead of UUIDs.\n></pre>\n>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[2725-2734]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md/#L2725-L2734)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[2750-2752]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md/#L2750-L2752)</code>\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Agent prompt</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n>```\n>The issue below was found during a code review. Follow the provided context and guidance below and implement a solution\n>\n>## Issue description\n>Routing metrics currently use `{upstream_id, endpoint_host}` which conflicts with the later stated rule to use hostname instead of UUID.\n>## Issue Context\n>Consistency in label schema is important for operational usability and to prevent unintended cardinality growth.\n>## Fix Focus Areas\n>- modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[2725-2735]\n>- modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[2748-2753]\n>```\n> <code>ⓘ Copy this prompt and use it to remediate the issue with your preferred AI generation tools</code>\n></details>\n\n<hr/>\n</details>\n\n\n<details><summary><ins><strong>View more (1)</strong></ins></summary><br/>\n<details>\n<summary>  6.  Broken example links <code>🐞 Bug</code> <code>⛯ Reliability</code></summary>\n\n<br/>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Description</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>• DESIGN.md adds an “Additional Protocol Examples” section, but the linked <b><i>./examples/*.md</i></b> files\n>are not present in the repo. • The actual protocol examples live under\n><b><i>scenarios/protocols/*/examples/</i></b> with different filenames, so readers will hit broken links. •\n>Recommend updating the links to the real example paths (or adding/renaming files to match the linked\n>paths).\n></pre>\n></details>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Code</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[R2315-2319]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/pull/527/files#diff-f4fdd5834a964c7cd99fdd61c8ca9d8366cf65565894c59f592ecc606d139760R2315-R2319)</code>\n>\n>```diff\n>+**Additional Protocol Examples:**\n>+\n>- [Plain HTTP Request/Response](./examples/1.http.positive.md)\n>- [Server-Sent Events (SSE)](./examples/2.sse.positive.md)\n>- [Streaming WebSockets](./examples/3.websocket.positive.md)\n>```\n></details>\n\n> <details >\n><summary>Evidence</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>DESIGN.md links to <b><i>./examples/...</i></b> files, while existing example documents have different names and\n>locations under <b><i>scenarios/protocols/*/examples/</i></b>.\n></pre>\n>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[2315-2320]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md/#L2315-L2320)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/scenarios/protocols/http/examples/positive-example-01-http-request-response.md[1-2]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/scenarios/protocols/http/examples/positive-example-01-http-request-response.md/#L1-L2)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/scenarios/protocols/sse/examples/positive-example-02-sse-streaming.md[1-2]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/scenarios/protocols/sse/examples/positive-example-02-sse-streaming.md/#L1-L2)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/scenarios/protocols/websocket/examples/positive-example-03-websocket-upgrade.md[1-2]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/scenarios/protocols/websocket/examples/positive-example-03-websocket-upgrade.md/#L1-L2)</code>\n> <code>[modules/system/oagw/scenarios/protocols/grpc/examples/positive-example-04-grpc-unary-proxy.md[1-2]](https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core/blob/c9c711923d4db2ea8af2f40aeccc2fc2bb664b5a/modules/system/oagw/scenarios/protocols/grpc/examples/positive-example-04-grpc-unary-proxy.md/#L1-L2)</code>\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Agent prompt</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n>```\n>The issue below was found during a code review. Follow the provided context and guidance below and implement a solution\n>\n>## Issue description\n>DESIGN.md points to non-existent `./examples/*.md` files. The actual examples exist elsewhere under `scenarios/protocols/*/examples`.\n>## Issue Context\n>This breaks navigability of the spec and undermines the newly added examples section.\n>## Fix Focus Areas\n>- modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md[2315-2320]\n>```\n> <code>ⓘ Copy this prompt and use it to remediate the issue with your preferred AI generation tools</code>\n></details>\n\n<hr/>\n</details>\n\n\n</details>\n<img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/light-grey-line.svg\" height=\"10%\" alt=\"Grey Divider\">\n<pre>ⓘ The new review experience is currently in Beta. <a href=\"https://docs.qodo.ai/qodo-documentation/code-review\">Learn more</a></pre>\n<img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/light-grey-line.svg\" height=\"10%\" alt=\"Grey Divider\">\n\n<a href=\"https://www.qodo.ai\"><img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/qodo-logo.svg\" width=\"80\" alt=\"Qodo Logo\"></a>","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/comments/3870865899/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":{"id":484649,"client_id":"Iv1.8b06d3cf5deea057","slug":"qodo-code-review","node_id":"A_kwHOCuzIt84AB2Up","owner":{"login":"qodo-ai","id":183290039,"node_id":"O_kgDOCuzItw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/183290039?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai","html_url":"https://github.com/qodo-ai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/qodo-ai/received_events","type":"Organization","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"name":"Qodo Code Review","description":"### The AI Code Review Platform\r\n\r\n## Get Started\r\nSimply sign into Github and click the \"Configure\" button. Check out our documentation for more info: https://docs.qodo.ai/get-started.\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\nQodo automatically reviews every GitHub pull request, identifies logic bugs, edge cases, security issues, and code quality problems, and provides actionable suggestions your team can apply immediately.\r\n\r\nInstall in minutes. Integrates seamlessly into your existing workflows.\r\n\r\n## Qodo is designed for developers who:\r\n- Handle frequent pull requests and need reliable review coverage\r\n- Balance code quality with fast delivery\r\n- Work in complex, multi-repo systems\r\n- Use AI-generated code and need stronger guardrails\r\n- Build production software where reliability matters\r\n\r\n## Qodo Capabilities\r\n- ## Automated PR Analysis\r\n  - Reviews every pull request automatically\r\n  - No manual trigger required\r\n\r\n- ## Context-Aware Code Review\r\n  - Understands cross-file changes\r\n  - Detects architectural inconsistencies\r\n\r\n- ## Actionable Suggestions\r\n  - Concrete code fixes\r\n  - Clear explanations\r\n  - Prioritized feedback\r\n\r\n- ## Security & Reliability Checks\r\n  - Identifies risky patterns\r\n  - Reduces attack surface area","external_url":"https://www.qodo.ai/","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/qodo-code-review","created_at":"2023-11-16T16:18:38Z","updated_at":"2026-05-06T15:53:40Z","permissions":{"actions":"read","checks":"read","contents":"write","discussions":"write","issues":"write","metadata":"read","pull_requests":"write"},"events":["check_run","check_suite","discussion_comment","issue_comment","pull_request","pull_request_review_comment","push"]}},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/comments/3874595578","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/527#issuecomment-3874595578","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/527","id":3874595578,"node_id":"IC_kwDOPaLyBs7m8aL6","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-02-10T00:17:16Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T01:01:22Z","body":"I've got security report for this design and dound it interesting:\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### RISK-01: SSRF — No validation of resolved IP (P0)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: OAGW resolves a hostname from the upstream config and sends a request. If the hostname resolves to a private IP (127.0.0.1, 169.254.169.254, 10.x.x.x, fc00::/7, ::1), OAGW becomes an SSRF proxy to internal services.\r\n* **Impact**: Critical — access to cloud metadata, internal APIs, credential theft.\r\n* **Likelihood**: High — a standard attack against egress proxies.\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Security Considerations mentions “IP pinning rules” but marks them as “out of scope for this document”. Implementation is deferred to F-P1-008 (Phase 1).\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * **Move to P0**: DNS resolve → IP check MUST be in the MVP. This is not hardening, this is baseline security.\r\n  * Implement `validate_resolved_ip(ip)` → block RFC 1918, RFC 6598, RFC 3927, loopback, link-local, multicast, IPv6 ULA and link-local.\r\n  * Validate **after** DNS resolve, before connect. Re-validate on every new connection (DNS rebinding).\r\n  * Optional: resolve DNS in OAGW and pass the IP into the HTTP client via a `resolve` API (reqwest supports this).\r\n\r\n### RISK-02: DNS Rebinding (P0)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: An attacker configures upstream `evil.com` → first resolve = `1.2.3.4` (public). OAGW caches it as “allowed”. DNS then changes to `169.254.169.254`. Subsequent requests go to cloud metadata.\r\n* **Impact**: Critical — SSRF to internal services.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Medium — requires DNS control.\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md does not mention DNS rebinding at all. The HTTP/2 capability cache (§HTTP Version Negotiation) caches by host/IP, but re-resolve behavior is not specified.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * Validate IP on **every** connection, not only on the first one.\r\n  * Do not cache “allowed” status based on hostname, only on IP.\r\n  * Use a connection pool keyed by IP, not by hostname.\r\n\r\n### RISK-03: Missing redirect policy (P0)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: The design **nowhere** describes how HTTP redirects (301, 302, 307, 308) are handled. If reqwest follows redirects by default, then:\r\n\r\n  1. Redirect to an internal IP → SSRF\r\n  2. Redirect → Auth headers (Authorization: Bearer) leak to another domain\r\n  3. Redirect loop → DoS\r\n* **Impact**: Critical (SSRF + credential leak).\r\n* **Likelihood**: High — redirects are common.\r\n* **Where**: **Missing** from the design. No section, no ADR, not mentioned in Security Considerations.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * **Disable redirect following** by default. Return the redirect response to the client.\r\n  * If following redirects is required, apply the same SSRF checks to the redirect target.\r\n  * **Never** forward Authorization headers when redirecting to a different domain.\r\n  * Limit max redirects (3–5).\r\n  * Add a dedicated section to DESIGN.\r\n\r\n### RISK-04: Header Smuggling via X-OAGW-Target-Host (P0)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: A new PR introduces `X-OAGW-Target-Host` to select an endpoint. The caller supplies the value, and OAGW compares it case-insensitively with endpoint hosts. However:\r\n\r\n  1. Unicode, IDN, or punycode may bypass string comparison\r\n  2. If OAGW uses this value for the downstream `Host` header, host header injection is possible\r\n  3. The design says “stripped before forwarding”, but this is an **invariant**, not an enforced point\r\n* **Impact**: High — routing to unintended endpoints, potential SSRF.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Medium.\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Alias Resolution, §Headers Transformation, PR diff with new scenarios.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n* **Note**: I see basic things but we need to implement ASCII-only, punycode/IDN normalizaiton for this header\r\n\r\n  * Validate `X-OAGW-Target-Host`: ASCII-only, no `@`, no `/`, no `:`, no null bytes, no newlines.\r\n  * Compare normalized values (lowercase, punycode-decoded).\r\n  * **Explicitly document** that `Host`/`:authority` is ALWAYS set to `upstream.endpoint.host`, never from `X-OAGW-Target-Host`.\r\n  * Add negative tests: X-OAGW-Target-Host with unicode, punycode, port, userinfo, path.\r\n\r\n### RISK-05: Tenant Isolation — Cache Poisoning (P1)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: Cache keys in ADR Data Plane Caching:\r\n\r\n  * `upstream:{tenant_id}:{alias}` — OK, includes tenant\r\n  * `route:{upstream_id}:{method}:{path}` — **does NOT include tenant_id**\r\n  * `plugin:{plugin_id}` — **does NOT include tenant_id**\r\n  * CP L1 cache does not describe keys at all\r\n    If `upstream_id` accidentally collides or an upstream is shared between tenants, the route cache may return another tenant’s route.\r\n* **Impact**: High — cross-tenant data leak, routing to wrong config.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Medium — depends on shared upstream implementation.\r\n* **Where**: adr-data-plane-caching.md §Cache Keys.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * **All** cache keys MUST include `tenant_id` (or scope chain).\r\n  * `route:{tenant_id}:{upstream_id}:{method}:{path}`\r\n  * `plugin:{tenant_id}:{plugin_id}`\r\n  * CP L1 cache must be keyed by `(tenant_id, upstream_id)`.\r\n\r\n### RISK-06: Confused Deputy — cred_store Access (P1)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: Auth config contains `secret_ref: \"cred://partner-openai-key\"`. OAGW calls cred_store on behalf of whom? The design says “cred_store checks if the secret is accessible to the current tenant”, but:\r\n\r\n  1. Who authenticates to cred_store? If OAGW uses a service account, cred_store may not enforce tenant scope.\r\n  2. If tenant A sets `secret_ref: \"cred://tenant-b-secret\"` and cred_store does not check properly, secrets leak.\r\n* **Impact**: Critical — cross-tenant secret theft.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Medium — depends on cred_store implementation.\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Secret Access Control — describes desired behavior, but not the enforcement mechanism.\r\n* **Notes**: Mostly covered\r\n\r\n  * OAGW MUST pass `tenant_id` and `principal_id` to cred_store on every request.\r\n  * cred_store MUST enforce access control, but OAGW MUST add defense in depth: validate that `secret_ref` belongs to the tenant BEFORE calling cred_store.\r\n  * Add audit logging for every cred_store access.\r\n  * Add an integration test: tenant A cannot read tenant B’s secret.\r\n\r\n### RISK-07: Secret leakage in error responses (P1)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: If cred_store returns an error containing secret material, or if an upstream returns a response that includes sent credentials in the body, OAGW may pass this to the client.\r\n* **Impact**: High — credential exposure.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Medium.\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Error Handling — passthrough of upstream errors “status + headers + body without modification”. §Audit Logging — “No secrets in logs”. No mechanism for response body scanning.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * cred_store errors MUST be replaced with a generic `500 SecretNotFound`, without details.\r\n  * Upstream error passthrough is OK, but redact any OAGW-injected headers (Authorization, API keys) from error details.\r\n  * On auth plugin failure, do not include secret material in Problem details.\r\n\r\n### RISK-08: Request Body DoS — Decompression Bombs (P1)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: There is a 100MB hard body limit. But with `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` and `Content-Encoding: gzip`, a compressed 1KB payload can decompress into gigabytes.\r\n* **Impact**: High — OOM, crash.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Medium.\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Body Validation Rules — “Hard limit 100MB; reject before buffering”. No mention of Content-Encoding.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * Enforce limits on **decompressed** size, not just wire size.\r\n  * Or do not decompress at all (proxy as-is) and enforce limits on wire size.\r\n  * Reject unsupported Content-Encoding values.\r\n  * Add this to Body Validation Rules.\r\n\r\n### RISK-09: Starlark Plugin Escape — Credential Access (P1)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: Starlark plugins have access to `ctx.request.headers` (read/write). After auth plugin injection, headers contain `Authorization: Bearer <secret>`. A transform plugin (on_request phase) runs AFTER auth and can read and exfiltrate it via `ctx.log.info(\"got token\", ctx.request.headers.get(\"Authorization\"))`.\r\n* **Impact**: High — secret leak via plugin logs.\r\n* **Likelihood**: High — per design plugin chain: Auth → Guard → Transform(request).\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Plugin Execution Order, §Starlark Context API.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * `ctx.request.headers` exposed to Starlark MUST **hide** security-sensitive headers (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie).\r\n  * Or inject auth AFTER transform plugins (architectural change).\r\n  * Or add a redaction layer to `ctx.log.*` to strip known secret patterns.\r\n  * Enforce log size limits per plugin call (already mentioned in F-P1-005, but must be enforced).\r\n\r\n### RISK-10: IDOR via Management API (P1)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: The Management API accepts GTS IDs as UUIDs. If tenant A guesses the UUID of tenant B’s upstream and Secure ORM does not enforce scope on every query, this is IDOR.\r\n* **Impact**: High — read or modify чужая configuration.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Low to Medium (UUIDs are hard to guess, but UUIDv7 is predictable).\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Management API, F-P1-003.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * SeaORM Secure ORM (`SecureConn`) with tenant scope on EVERY query, already planned (F-P1-002, F-P1-003).\r\n  * Move enforcement from P1 to P0: deny-by-default scope.\r\n  * Add a test: request for another tenant’s upstream UUID → 404 (not 403, to avoid leaking existence).\r\n\r\n### RISK-11: Rate Limit Bypass via Multiple Tenants (P2)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: Default scope = “tenant”. If an attacker creates N child tenants, each gets its own rate limit bucket. Effective limit = N * configured_rate.\r\n* **Impact**: High — billing abuse, upstream overload.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Medium — depends on tenant creation policy.\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Rate Limiting, ADR Rate Limiting.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * Enforce aggregate rate limits per upstream (across all tenants), a “global” scope.\r\n  * Ancestor `enforce` rate limits must be per upstream instance, not per tenant.\r\n  * Clarify scope hierarchy: `min(global, ancestor.enforce, tenant)`.\r\n\r\n### RISK-12: SQL Injection via JSONB Queries (P1)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: Common Queries use JSONB path expressions with string interpolation:\r\n\r\n  ```sql\r\n  AND $3 LIKE (match ->'http'->>'path' || '%')\r\n  ```\r\n\r\n  If `path` in JSONB contains SQL wildcards (`%`, `_`), pattern matching can be corrupted. Not classic SQL injection, but logic bypass.\r\n* **Impact**: Medium — route matching corruption.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Low.\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Common Queries — “SQL in DESIGN is illustrative”, but it defines semantics.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * Escape `%` and `_` in values coming from JSONB before LIKE.\r\n  * Or use prefix comparison (`starts_with`) instead of LIKE.\r\n  * F-P1-003 already addresses “no raw SQL”, ensure this also covers LIKE injection.\r\n\r\n### RISK-13: Cache Invalidation Race — Stale Config Serving (P2)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: Config cache (L1/L2) has no TTL in L1. If an admin disables an upstream (enabled=false) for emergency shutdown, L1 cache may continue serving requests until eviction.\r\n* **Impact**: Medium — bypass of emergency shutoff.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Medium.\r\n* **Where**: adr-data-plane-caching.md — “L1: No TTL (evicted by LRU)”.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * L1 cache MUST have a bounded TTL (30–60s) in addition to LRU.\r\n  * Or implement synchronous invalidation from DP to CP on security-critical changes (enabled or disabled toggle).\r\n  * CP L1 cache notification mechanism is not described (“notified by DP or periodic sync”).\r\n\r\n### RISK-14: URL Parsing — `@` in userinfo, scheme confusion (P1)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: Upstream endpoint `host` field allows `format: hostname`. But:\r\n\r\n  * `evil@internal.host` if the URL parser treats it as userinfo\r\n  * IDN homograph: `аpi.openai.com` (Cyrillic ‘а’) vs `api.openai.com`\r\n  * Non-standard ports in alias: `api.openai.com:8443`, alias comparison may be inconsistent\r\n* **Impact**: High — SSRF, credential routing to the wrong host.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Low to Medium.\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Upstream Schema — `host` is `format: hostname` but no explicit normalization.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * Strict ASCII-only validation for `host`. Reject non-ASCII, `@`, `#`, `?`, `/`.\r\n  * Normalize host: lowercase, punycode → reject or convert.\r\n  * Validate at the management API (write time), not at proxy time.\r\n\r\n### RISK-15: Proxy Headers Not Stripped (P1)\r\n\r\n* **Risk**: A caller can send `X-Forwarded-For`, `X-Real-IP`, `X-Forwarded-Host`. If the upstream trusts these headers, an attacker can spoof the source IP.\r\n* **Impact**: Medium — bypass of IP-based access control at the upstream.\r\n* **Likelihood**: Medium.\r\n* **Where**: DESIGN.md §Headers Transformation — lists hop-by-hop headers to strip but does NOT include `X-Forwarded-*`, `X-Real-IP`, `Forwarded`.\r\n* **Fix**:\r\n\r\n  * Add `X-Forwarded-For`, `X-Forwarded-Host`, `X-Forwarded-Proto`, `X-Real-IP`, `Forwarded` to the strip list.\r\n  * Or only pass through explicitly allowlisted headers (default `passthrough: none` is good, but verify enforcement).\r\n","author_association":"MEMBER","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/comments/3874595578/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/comments/3874811499","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/527#issuecomment-3874811499","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/527","id":3874811499,"node_id":"IC_kwDOPaLyBs7m9O5r","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-02-10T01:30:03Z","updated_at":"2026-02-10T01:30:03Z","body":"About RISK report I have a new data. It's comparision with Kong and Tyk. Looks like we are ok in most ticks:\r\n\r\n# OAGW Security Risks: Comparison with Kong and Tyk\r\n\r\n## RISK-01: SSRF via IP (P0) — Upstream URL resolves to private IP (10.x, 169.254.x, etc.)\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **None built-in.** `ip-restriction` plugin filters **client** IPs, not upstream targets. Admin enters upstream hostname/IP via Admin API. No validation that target doesn't resolve to private IP. | **None built-in.** Upstream target_url is set by administrator. JSON schema validation is for request payload, not upstream IP. | **Real risk, but industry norm is not to protect.** Kong and Tyk trust the administrator. OAGW can add optional `deny_private_ip` flag as defence-in-depth, but this is **not P0 but P2** — downgrade. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-02: DNS Rebinding (P2, downgraded) — Upstream hostname initially resolves safely, later resolves to internal IP\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Not implemented.** No DNS pinning or re-validation. Nginx resolves DNS according to TTL. | **Not implemented.** Go net/http standard DNS resolution. There was a PR on DNS caching (#2040) — but for performance, not security. | **Confirmed: P2.** No gateway implements this. OAGW is egress-only, upstream targets are admin-configured. Keep P2. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-03: Redirect Following (P1) — CP's HTTP client follows 3xx redirect to internal service\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Does not follow redirects.** Kong is a transparent proxy (nginx-based). 3xx from upstream is transparently returned to client. Kong does not follow redirects on behalf of the client. | **Does not follow redirects.** Tyk is a transparent proxy. 3xx from upstream is proxied back to client. \"Looping\" is internal URL rewrite, not following upstream redirects. | **Confirmed: OAGW must NOT follow redirects** (redirect_policy: `none` by default, like Kong/Tyk). If follow is enabled — validate destination IP. Matches industry. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-04: Host Header / Hop-by-Hop (P1) — Client-supplied headers (Host, Connection, Transfer-Encoding) forwarded to upstream\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Yes.** `preserve_host=false` (default) — Kong sets Host = upstream hostname. `preserve_host=true` — forwards original Host. `host_header` on upstream entity — override. Hop-by-hop: nginx removes Connection, Transfer-Encoding when proxying. [Issue #2202](https://github.com/Kong/kong/issues/2202) — hop-by-hop handling improvement discussed. | **Partial.** Tyk sets Host to target_url hostname by default. preserve_host_header in API definition. Go net/http handles hop-by-hop headers by default. | **Standard practice, OAGW must implement.** Strip hop-by-hop headers (Connection, Transfer-Encoding, Upgrade). Set Host = upstream hostname. Matches Kong/Tyk. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-05: Cache Poisoning via Missing Tenant (P0) — Cache keys like `route:{upstream_id}:{method}:{path}` without tenant_id\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Implicit isolation.** Kong Enterprise Workspaces — each workspace has its own entities (Services, Routes). Entity IDs are globally unique (UUID). Cache by entity ID is automatically isolated. `db_cache_ttl`, `db_cache_neg_ttl`, `db_cache_stale_ttl` — configurable. | **Implicit isolation.** Tyk — APIs bound to org_id. Redis cache per-API (API ID = key). API IDs are unique within organization. Cache flush — per API ID. | **Confirmed P0 for OAGW.** Kong/Tyk solve this through UUID-based entity isolation. OAGW cache keys use `upstream_id` (UUID) — this already contains implicit tenant isolation. **BUT**: key `route:{upstream_id}:{method}:{path}` — if upstream_id is scoped to tenant, then OK. Need to verify that upstream_id is always scoped to tenant. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-06: Credential Store Leak in Logs (P0) — Secrets from cred_store appear in error messages or logs\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Partial.** OSS — credentials in plain text in DB. Enterprise — Vault integration (HashiCorp, AWS SM, GCP SM, env vars). Secrets referenced via `{vault://...}` URI — config stores reference, not value. But: key-auth plugin doesn't encrypt even in Enterprise. Admin API can return credentials in plain text. | **Yes.** `enable_key_logging: false` (default) — masks API keys in logs. Secrets masked as `****`. Tyk Dashboard RBAC restricts access to keys. | **Confirmed P0.** Both platforms had issues. Tyk is better (default masking). OAGW must: 1) never log secrets, 2) vault references instead of values, 3) redact in error responses. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-07: Secret Leak in Error Responses (P1) — Upstream URL or auth tokens appear in error messages returned to caller\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Partial.** Kong returns standard errors (`502 Bad Gateway`, `503 Service Unavailable`). Upstream URL **not** included in response body by default. But error logs may contain upstream details. Admin API can expose configuration. | **Partial.** Tyk returns standard error codes. `MaxResponseBodySize` = 0 (no limit). Error body from upstream is proxied as-is. Custom error templates supported. | **Standard practice.** OAGW must: generic error messages for client, upstream details only in internal logs. Kong/Tyk both follow this pattern (with caveats). |\r\n\r\n## RISK-08: Decompression Bomb / Body Limit (P1) — No limit on upstream response body causes OOM\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Yes (request).** `Request Size Limiting` plugin. `nginx_http_client_max_body_size` (default: 0 = disabled). **Response:** `Response Size Limiting` plugin (community, by Content-Length header). Nginx buffer settings. Decompression bomb — no special protection. | **Yes (both directions).** `MaxRequestBodySize` — 413 on exceed. `MaxResponseBodySize` — 500 on exceed (only works with Response Body Transform middleware). Decompression bomb — no special protection. | **Standard practice.** Both platforms limit by Content-Length, **but do NOT protect against decompression bomb** (gzip bomb). OAGW must: max body size on request and response, + streaming limit on decompressed size during decompression. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-09: Starlark Plugin Credential Access (P1) — Custom plugins can extract and exfiltrate secrets from the ctx object\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **No sandbox.** Lua plugins have full access to Kong PDK (kong.ctx, kong.service, kong.request). Lua sandbox exists (`untrusted_lua`), but by default plugins run with full access. Enterprise: can restrict which plugins are allowed. OSS: credentials store (key-auth) accessible from plugin code. | **No sandbox.** Go plugins — native code, full access. JS plugins (JSVM) — receive `request` and `session` objects. gRPC plugins — full API via Tyk protocol. No isolation of secrets from plugin code. | **OAGW is in a better position.** Starlark sandbox is already more secure than Lua/Go/JS in competitors. But ctx API must provide **only** needed data. Don't pass raw credentials to ctx. Only `Authorization` header injection after plugin chain. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-10: IDOR — Tenant A accesses Tenant B's upstream/route/plugin by guessing UUID\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Enterprise: Workspaces + RBAC.** Each workspace — isolated entities. Admin API scoped to workspace. RBAC roles: Super Admin, Admin, Read-Only. Cross-workspace access requires explicit permission. OSS: no multi-tenancy. | **Dashboard: Organizations + RBAC.** Each org — isolated unit. API keys bound to org_id. Dashboard RBAC: Read/Write/Deny per section. org_id in API definition ensures isolation. | **Standard practice.** Both platforms solve through workspace/org scoping + RBAC. OAGW: every CRUD request MUST check `tenant_id` ownership. `WHERE tenant_id = ?` in every SQL query. UUID is not enough — tenant filter required. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-11: Rate Limit Bypass via Multiple Tenants (P1) — Attacker creates N tenants to get N x rate_limit\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **No aggregate limit.** Rate Limiting plugin — per consumer/route/service. No cross-workspace aggregate. Kong Plus/Enterprise: no global aggregate across tenants. | **Yes, org-level.** Tyk supports Organization rate limits (aggregate across all APIs in org). MDCB distributes org limits to slave gateways. API-level rate limit — aggregate across all consumers. DRL (Distributed Rate Limiter) — across gateway instances. | **Tyk is better.** OAGW must: 1) per-tenant rate limit, 2) **global aggregate** rate limit per upstream (all tenants combined). Tyk's org-level limits are a good pattern. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-12: SQL Injection via Config Values (P2) — Plugin config or upstream URL containing SQL injection payload\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Implicit (ORM).** Kong uses PostgreSQL/Cassandra via its own DAO layer. Entity schemas are validated. **Explicit:** Injection Protection plugin (Enterprise 3.9+) — SQL injection detection in request headers/body/path. But this is for client requests, not admin config values. | **Implicit (ORM).** Tyk uses MongoDB/SQL via standard Go database drivers with parameterized queries. Request Validation middleware — JSON schema validation. | **Standard practice, P2 confirmed.** Both platforms rely on ORM/parameterized queries. OAGW uses modkit-db — must use parameterized queries. Config values must pass schema validation. Not critical if ORM is used correctly. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-13: L1 Cache Without TTL (P1) — Stale config served indefinitely from LRU cache\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Has TTL.** `db_cache_ttl` (default: 0 = never expire). `db_cache_neg_ttl` — negative cache TTL. `db_cache_stale_ttl` — stale resurrection TTL. Event-based invalidation: Admin API write -> invalidation event -> all nodes flush. Cluster invalidation events via DB polling. | **Has TTL.** `cache_timeout` per API. Redis-based cache with TTL. Cache flush API endpoint per API. Upstream-controlled TTL via `x-tyk-cache-action-set-ttl` header. | **OAGW ADR states \"L1: No TTL, evicted by LRU\" — this deviates from industry.** Kong also defaults to TTL=0 (no expire), BUT has event-based invalidation. OAGW also has invalidation on write — this compensates for lack of TTL. Recommendation: add optional safety-net TTL (e.g. 1 hour) as fallback. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-14: URL/Hostname Parsing Differences (P2) — Different URL parsers between validation and actual HTTP call lead to bypass\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Implicit.** Kong — nginx URL parsing (single parser). Target = `host:port`, validated on creation via Admin API. No dual-parser problem — nginx parses, validates, and proxies. | **Implicit.** Tyk — Go `net/url` parsing (single parser). `target_url` validated on API creation. Go standard library URL parsing. | **P2 confirmed.** Both platforms use a single parser. OAGW: if using `hyper` for HTTP calls — use `hyper::Uri` for both validation and request. One parser = no divergence. |\r\n\r\n## RISK-15: X-Forwarded / Proxy Header Injection (P1) — Client injects X-Forwarded-For, X-Real-IP to appear as internal\r\n\r\n| | Kong | Tyk | Verdict for OAGW |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **Protection** | **Yes.** `trusted_ips` — CIDR blocks of trusted IPs. Trusted IP -> forward X-Forwarded-*. Untrusted -> Kong **overwrites** with own values. `real_ip_header`, `real_ip_recursive`. Can disable injection: header config = `\"off\"`. [Issue #11752](https://github.com/Kong/kong/issues/11752) — selective forwarding. | **Partial.** Tyk adds `X-Forwarded-For` header. No explicit trusted_ips mechanism like Kong. Client-supplied headers can be overwritten by middleware. | **Standard practice.** Kong is better (trusted_ips). OAGW is an egress gateway, so X-Forwarded is less critical (no external clients directly). But for internal callers: strip untrusted X-Forwarded-* headers. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Summary Table\r\n\r\n| Risk | P | Kong | Tyk | Verdict |\r\n|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n| RISK-01 SSRF/IP | P0->**P2** | None | None | **Downgrade.** Industry doesn't protect. Optional deny_private_ip. |\r\n| RISK-02 DNS Rebind | P2 | None | None | **Confirmed P2.** Nobody does it. |\r\n| RISK-03 Redirects | P1 | Doesn't follow | Doesn't follow | **Confirmed.** OAGW must not follow (default). |\r\n| RISK-04 Host/Hop | P1 | Yes | Partial | **Confirmed.** Standard proxy responsibility. |\r\n| RISK-05 Cache tenant | P0->**P1** | Implicit (UUID) | Implicit (org_id) | **Downgrade if upstream_id is scoped.** Verify. |\r\n| RISK-06 Cred logs | P0 | Partial | Yes (default mask) | **Confirmed P0.** OAGW must mask. |\r\n| RISK-07 Error secrets | P1 | Partial | Partial | **Confirmed.** Generic errors to client. |\r\n| RISK-08 Body bomb | P1 | Yes (partial) | Yes (partial) | **Confirmed.** + decompression limit needed. |\r\n| RISK-09 Plugin creds | P1 | No sandbox | No sandbox | **OAGW is better** (Starlark). But restrict ctx. |\r\n| RISK-10 IDOR | P1 | Enterprise RBAC | Org RBAC | **Confirmed.** tenant_id in every query. |\r\n| RISK-11 Rate bypass | P1 | No aggregate | Yes org-level | **Confirmed.** OAGW needs per-upstream aggregate. |\r\n| RISK-12 SQLi | P2 | ORM | ORM | **Confirmed P2.** Parameterized queries. |\r\n| RISK-13 L1 no TTL | P1 | TTL=0 + events | TTL + flush API | **Confirmed.** Add safety-net TTL. |\r\n| RISK-14 URL parse | P2 | Single parser | Single parser | **Confirmed P2.** Single parser = OK. |\r\n| RISK-15 X-Forwarded | P1 | Yes (trusted_ips) | Partial | **Confirmed.** Strip for egress. |\r\n\r\n## Key Takeaways\r\n\r\n1. **RISK-01 and RISK-02 — downgrade.** Neither Kong nor Tyk validate upstream IP. This is the administrator's / network layer's responsibility.\r\n\r\n2. **RISK-05 — requires verification.** If `upstream_id` (UUID) is already bound to tenant — cache poisoning is impossible. Need to verify in code.\r\n\r\n3. **RISK-09 — OAGW is in a better position** than competitors. Starlark sandbox > Lua/Go/JS without sandbox. But need to restrict what ctx exposes.\r\n\r\n4. **RISK-11 — Tyk is better** with org-level aggregate rate limits. OAGW needs an analogous mechanism.\r\n\r\n5. **RISK-13 — add safety-net TTL.** Kong also defaults to TTL=0, but has event-based invalidation. OAGW also has this — but safety-net TTL protects against missed invalidation events.\r\n\r\n6. **Originally there were 3 P0 risks. After comparison, 1 real P0 remains — RISK-06 (credential masking in logs).** The rest are P1/P2.\r\n","author_association":"MEMBER","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/comments/3874811499/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/comments/3876279375"