 2>/dev/null | head -10\n\n# Check if there's a PR description or objectives file\necho -e \"\\n=== Checking root directory for PR/review docs ===\"\nfd -e md . -maxdepth 2 2>/dev/null | grep -i -E 'risk|security|objective|review' | head -10\n\n# Search for X-OAGW-Target-Host bypass mentions\necho -e \"\\n=== Searching for Target-Host bypass/security mentions ===\"\nrg -i 'target-host.*bypass|bypass.*target-host|unicode.*target|idn.*target' . 2>/dev/null | head -20\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 50386\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n# Search more broadly for risk/security references\nrg -i 'risk|security.*review|threat.*model|attack.*vector' . --type md --max-count 50 2>/dev/null | head -30\n\n# Check for PR descriptions or objectives\nfind . -name \"*.md\" -type f | xargs grep -l \"P0\\|P1\\|priority\" 2>/dev/null | head -10\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 3879\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\ncat modules/system/oagw/REVIEW_FEEDBACK.md | head -100\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 7712\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n# Search for X-OAGW-Target-Host specific security concerns in review feedback\ngrep -n -i \"target-host\\|header.*bypass\\|unicode\\|idn\\|punycode\\|control.*char\\|userinfo\\|@\" modules/system/oagw/REVIEW_FEEDBACK.md\n\n# Check if there are any RISK numbered items in the feedback\ngrep -E \"RISK-[0-9]+\" modules/system/oagw/REVIEW_FEEDBACK.md\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 1039\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n**Expand X-OAGW-Target-Host validation tests to cover additional bypass vectors.**\n\nCurrent negative test scenarios cover port syntax, paths, query parameters, and IP addresses. Additional test cases would strengthen validation coverage for header parsing edge cases:\n\n- **IDN/Punycode**: Test punycode (`xn--` prefix) vs Unicode equivalents to ensure consistent normalization\n- **Case variations**: Verify handling of mixed-case hostnames (`US.VENDOR.COM` vs `us.vendor.com`)\n- **Control characters & whitespace**: Reject headers with embedded CRLF, null bytes, or leading/trailing spaces\n- **Userinfo syntax**: Reject headers containing `@` to prevent SSRF via userinfo parsing (`attacker.com@us.vendor.com`)\n- **Homograph attacks**: Test visually similar characters (e.g., Cyrillic lookalikes) to ensure they're treated as distinct hosts\n- **Trailing dots**: Clarify FQDN normalization behavior (`us.vendor.com.` vs `us.vendor.com`)\n\nThe existing test `negative-2.2-ip-address-when-hostname-expected.md` already validates that IP addresses are rejected when hostname endpoints are configured, and `positive-3.2-case-insensitive-matching.md` confirms case-insensitive matching. The gaps above represent parsing robustness rather than a missing critical test, but covering them would reduce the surface area for unexpected input handling.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn\n`@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-2.1-unknown-host.md`\naround lines 1 - 47, Update the negative-2.1-unknown-host.md test suite to add\nadditional header-parsing edge cases: add scenarios for IDN/punycode vs Unicode\nequivalents, control characters and leading/trailing whitespace (CRLF, null\nbytes, spaces), userinfo syntax containing '@', homograph/lookalike characters,\nand trailing-dot FQDNs; ensure each case asserts a 400 with the same error type\nand includes the configured valid_hosts list as in the existing expected\nresponse. Reference the existing\nnegative-2.2-ip-address-when-hostname-expected.md and\npositive-3.2-case-insensitive-matching.md tests for structure and reuse their\nvalidation/assertion patterns (status 400, X-OAGW-Error-Source: gateway,\nproblem+json body) so the new cases follow the same request/response format and\nerror field checks.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting: