usage on TLS port 443.**\n> \n> `h2c` is cleartext HTTP/2 and typically isn’t served on a TLS port. Either remove `h2c` from this TLS example or add a note that `h2c` applies only to a separate cleartext listener.\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-cors.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `55-73`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Add language identifiers to fenced code blocks.**  \n> markdownlint flags MD040 here; please add a language (e.g., `json`) to keep docs lint-clean.\n> \n> ---\n> \n> `153-205`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Add language identifiers to fenced code blocks.**  \n> markdownlint flags MD040 here; please add a language (e.g., `json`) to keep docs lint-clean.\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n````\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md`:\n- Around line 655-666: The current detection uses has_common_suffix = any(...)\nwhich triggers when only a single endpoint ends with the alias; change the logic\nto require that every endpoint in selected.endpoints shares the common-suffix\n(use all(...) semantics) so the X-OAGW-Target-Host header is only required when\nall endpoints are alias-derived; update the condition that computes\nhas_common_suffix (referencing has_common_suffix, selected.endpoints, alias) and\nleave the subsequent header check and ErrorResponse usage intact.\n- Around line 383-391: The fenced code block showing the request flow in\nDESIGN.md lacks a language label and triggers MD040; edit the block containing\nthe \"Incoming Request -> Auth Plugin ...\" flow and add a language identifier\n(e.g., use ```text) to the opening fence so the block becomes a labelled fenced\ncode block, preserving the exact content and indentation of the \"Incoming\nRequest\" sequence.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-cors.md`:\n- Around line 94-110: The fenced code block showing the JSON example that begins\nwith { \"server\": { \"endpoints\": ... } } should include a language identifier;\nupdate the opening triple-backtick for that block to ```json so the block is\nmarked as JSON (this will satisfy markdownlint MD040). Locate the fenced block\nin adr-cors.md containing the CORS example and add the `json` label immediately\nafter the backticks.\n- Around line 111-133: The MD040 lint error means there are fenced code blocks\nwithout a language tag around the content near the \"## Comparison Matrix\"\nsection (the table is likely wrapped in triple backticks or other fenced\nblocks); fix by either removing the code fence so the table is plain Markdown,\nor add an explicit language identifier (e.g., ```markdown) to the opening fence\nfor any fenced block in adr-cors.md (look for the table under \"## Comparison\nMatrix\" and any other fenced snippets) so markdownlint MD040 is satisfied.\n- Around line 353-356: Fix the typo and incorrect contains usage in the Rust\nexample: replace the stray \"& &\" with the logical operator \"&&\", and change the\nwildcard check from config.allowed_origins.contains(\"*\") to a valid Vec<String>\ncheck such as config.allowed_origins.contains(&\"*\".to_string()) or\nconfig.allowed_origins.iter().any(|o| o == \"*\"); keep the rest of the\nconditional using config.allow_credentials and return Err(...) as shown.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-rust-abi-client-library.md`:\n- Around line 432-449: The requests_in_flight counter is incremented before\nfallible calls (build_reqwest_request, self.inner.execute, and\nresp.bytes().await) but only decremented on the successful path; ensure it is\ndecremented on every early return by adding a scope guard or explicit decrements\nin each map_err/err branch. Specifically, ensure requests_in_flight.dec() runs\nif build_reqwest_request returns Err, if self.inner.execute(...) fails\n(map_reqwest_error closure), and if resp.bytes().await fails (the\nClientError::Io branch), so the counter is always decremented before returning\nan Err; you can implement this by using a RAII guard that calls\nrequests_in_flight.dec() on Drop or by calling\nself.metrics.requests_in_flight.dec() inside each error-mapping closure and\nbefore constructing the ClientError::Io in the bytes() error path.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-state-management.md`:\n- Around line 87-90: The doc is inconsistent: the bullets state \"No TTL\" but\nelsewhere mention \"short‑lived cache entries\"; update the ADR to be consistent\nby either (A) removing any \"short‑lived\" wording and clarifying that cache\nrelies solely on LRU + explicit invalidation (keep \"No TTL\"), or (B) introduce a\nTTL policy (e.g., default TTL, configurable via env var) and update bullets to\nreflect \"Optional TTL for short‑lived entries\" plus how it interacts with LRU\nand invalidation; edit the phrases \"No TTL\" and any occurrences of \"short‑lived\ncache entries\" to match the chosen option and include the config symbol name\nused for TTL if you add it.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/PRD.md`:\n- Around line 48-52: Clarify the rate‑limiting ownership by stating that the\n\"API Handler\" is responsible for ingress-level concerns\n(authentication/authorization throttling and global per-client ingress request\nlimits), while the \"Control Plane (CP)\" owns proxy/outbound rate limiting\n(per-upstream, per-route, and per-plugin limits) and is responsible for\nconfiguring and distributing those rate-limiting policies to the Data Plane\n(DP); update the OAGW component list and any ADR sections that mention rate\nlimiting (including the CP proxy flow descriptions) to explicitly map ingress vs\noutbound responsibilities and note that DP enforces CP-distributed limits at\nruntime.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/INDEX.md`:\n- Around line 337-345: Rename the 6.3 scenario title/filename and any internal\nreferences from “host header” to “X-OAGW-Target-Host” (e.g., update the scenario\ntitle and the filename\nnegative-6.3-multi-endpoint-common-suffix-alias-requires-host-header.md and its\ntitle/header text) or add a clear deprecation note at the top stating that “host\nheader” is superseded by X-OAGW-Target-Host; ensure all mentions in INDEX.md and\nthe scenario markdown refer to X-OAGW-Target-Host consistently so implementers\naren’t confused.\n\nIn\n`@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-2.1-unknown-host.md`:\n- Around line 35-40: The error payload currently returns a \"valid_hosts\" array\n(and \"invalid_value\"/\"detail\") which can leak upstream topology; change the\nerror-generation logic that emits this unknown-host response to make inclusion\nof the \"valid_hosts\" field configurable (e.g., via a boolean flag like\nshow_valid_hosts_in_errors or expose_valid_hosts) and default it to false for\nproduction, only enabling it for debug/non‑prod environments; update the\nunknown-host error builder to omit \"valid_hosts\" unless the flag is true (apply\nthe same change for the other referenced unknown-host responses that include\nlines similar to \"valid_hosts\" / \"invalid_value\" / \"detail\").\n````\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (7)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-rust-abi-client-library.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`892-945`: **Avoid external-network “unit” tests; some endpoints are flaky/defunct.**  \nThe examples hit `https://httpbin.org/*` and `wss://echo.websocket.org`, which makes tests non-deterministic and the WebSocket endpoint is retired. Recommend replacing with a local test server/mocked transport and reclassifying as integration tests if network is required.  \n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Possible doc adjustment</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        let request = Request::builder()\n-            .method(Method::GET)\n-            .uri(\"https://httpbin.org/get\")\n+        // Use a local test server or mock transport for deterministic tests.\n+        let request = Request::builder()\n+            .method(Method::GET)\n+            .uri(\"http://127.0.0.1:PORT/get\")\n@@\n-        let request = Request::builder()\n-            .uri(\"wss://echo.websocket.org\")\n+        // Prefer a local echo server for websocket tests.\n+        let request = Request::builder()\n+            .uri(\"ws://127.0.0.1:PORT/echo\")\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-api/routes/positive-3.10-list-routes-includes-disabled-resources.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`75-75`: **Disambiguate duplicate \"Expected response\" headings.**\n\nBoth Step 4 and Step 5 use \"## Expected response\" as their heading. Consider making these headings more specific to improve navigability.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix to disambiguate headings</summary>\n\n```diff\n-## Expected response\n+## Expected response (all routes)\n```\n\nAnd for the second occurrence:\n\n```diff\n-## Expected response\n+## Expected response (enabled only)\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 91-91\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-request-routing.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`33-38`: **Add language specifier to fenced code blocks.**\n\nThe fenced code blocks showing request flow diagrams should specify a language identifier for proper rendering.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n Client\n → API Handler (auth, rate limit)\n → Data Plane (validate, write DB, invalidate cache)\n → Response\n ```\n```\n\nApply the same change to the second code block at line 42.\n\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 42-51\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-component-architecture.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`70-79`: **Add language specifier to directory structure block.**\n\nThe fenced code block showing the module structure should specify a language identifier for proper rendering.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n modules/system/oagw/\n ├── oagw-sdk/          # Public API traits, models, errors\n │                      # OAGWClientV1, AuthPlugin, GuardPlugin, TransformPlugin\n ├── oagw-core/         # Shared internals, service traits\n │                      # ControlPlaneService, DataPlaneService\n ├── oagw-api/          # API Handler implementation\n ├── oagw-cp/           # Control Plane implementation\n ├── oagw-dp/           # Data Plane implementation\n ```\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-api/upstreams/positive-2.12-list-upstreams-includes-disabled-resources.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`54-54`: **Disambiguate duplicate \"Expected response\" headings.**\n\nBoth Step 3 and Step 4 use \"## Expected response\" as their heading. Consider making these headings more specific to improve navigability and consistency with the scenario pattern.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix to disambiguate headings</summary>\n\n```diff\n-## Expected response\n+## Expected response (all upstreams)\n```\n\nAnd for the second occurrence:\n\n```diff\n-## Expected response\n+## Expected response (enabled only)\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 70-70\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-plugin-system.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`129-151`: **Clarify external plugin loading for guards/transforms.**\n\nThe `ControlPlane::new` snippet only registers external `AuthPlugin`s. Consider adding guard/transform registration (or a note that the snippet is auth-only for brevity) so readers don’t assume only auth is extensible.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-concurrency-control.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`127-134`: **Minor race condition acceptable for approximate limiting.**\n\nThe `try_acquire` implementation has a brief race window where `in_flight` can momentarily exceed `max_concurrent` before being decremented. This is acceptable for local-only limiting (Phase 1) where approximate enforcement is sufficient, but worth noting:\n\n- Thread A: `fetch_add(1)` → current=99, writes 100\n- Thread B: `fetch_add(1)` → current=100, writes 101 (exceeds limit)\n- Thread A: Check passes (99 < 100)\n- Thread B: Check fails (100 >= 100), decrements back to 100\n\nThe implementation correctly handles this by decrementing on failure. For strict enforcement, consider using compare-and-swap (CAS) instead.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/527#pullrequestreview-3773384752","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/527","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/527#pullrequestreview-3773384752"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/527"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-09T13:44:02Z","commit_id":"484dbf65efad76bd5da70776fa1647ae1f526174"},{"id":3773555657,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7g6-PJ","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.