 comments posted: 13**\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> Some comments are outside the diff and can’t be posted inline due to platform limitations.\n> \n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>⚠️ Outside diff range comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `780-785`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Fix contradictory comment in invalid example.**\n> \n> Line 784 shows an invalid duplicate alias scenario but comments `No CONFLICT`, which contradicts the section header \"Invalid - duplicate alias within same tenant\". Should be `← CONFLICT` or `← ERROR: duplicate alias`.\n> \n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n>  *Invalid - duplicate alias within same tenant*:\n>  \n>  ```\n>  Tenant A: alias=\"my-service\" → server: 10.0.1.1:443\n> -Tenant A: alias=\"my-service\" → server: 10.0.1.2:443  No CONFLICT\n> +Tenant A: alias=\"my-service\" → server: 10.0.1.2:443  ← CONFLICT: duplicate alias\n>  ```\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n````\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-backpressure-queueing.md`:\n- Around line 54-62: Edit the disadvantage bullet list in the ADR text and\nremove the accidental \"No \" prefix from the two items that currently read \"No\nPoor UX during spikes\" and \"No Clients must implement retry logic\" so they read\n\"Poor UX during spikes\" and \"Clients must implement retry logic\"; update the\nmarkdown lines containing those bullets (the two disadvantage list entries) to\ndelete the leading \"No \" so the list is grammatically correct.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-cors.md`:\n- Around line 431-433: Fix the three malformed bullets by removing the leading\n\"No \" prefix and correcting the double-negative: change \"No Adds built-in logic\nto OAGW core\" to \"Adds built-in logic to OAGW core\", change \"No Requires CORS\nconfiguration for each upstream/route\" to \"Requires CORS configuration for each\nupstream/route\", and change \"No Not extensible via plugins (but can be disabled\nif needed)\" to \"Not extensible via plugins (but can be disabled if needed)\" so\nthe phrasing reads cleanly and consistently.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-rust-abi-client-library.md`:\n- Around line 2022-2025: The test is accessing the private field directly on the\nresponse; replace the direct field access with the public accessor method by\ncalling response.error_source() in the assertion so it compares the returned\nErrorSource against ErrorSource::Gateway (keep the assert_eq! for\nStatusCode::NOT_FOUND as-is). Ensure you reference the response variable and the\nerror_source() method rather than response.error_source.\n- Around line 1110-1114: Update the RequestBuilder::path implementation to\nnormalize the provided path string so it always begins with a leading '/' before\nstoring it (handle the impl Into<String> input by converting to String, and if\nthe first char is not '/', prepend '/'); ensure this normalization happens\ninside the path method used by RequestBuilder so callers passing \"v1/foo\" become\n\"/v1/foo\" when building the final URL.\n- Around line 1716-1724: The WebSocket Request builder currently only sets a few\nheaders (Authorization, Connection, Upgrade, Sec-WebSocket-*) and thus ignores\nand drops incoming request.headers(); fix by iterating over the original\nrequest.headers() and forwarding them into the websocket Request builder (the\nsame builder that produces req) while explicitly skipping hop-by-hop headers\n(e.g., \"connection\", \"upgrade\", \"sec-websocket-key\", \"sec-websocket-version\",\n\"sec-websocket-accept\", \"keep-alive\", \"proxy-authenticate\",\n\"proxy-authorization\", \"te\", \"trailers\") and avoiding duplicate headers you\nalready set (like Authorization/Upgrade); use the same conversion used elsewhere\nfor header name/value (to_str or proper HeaderValue creation) and preserve\nsubprotocols so Sec-WebSocket-Protocol is forwarded when present, and keep\nmapping build errors to ClientError::BuildError as before.\n- Around line 1503-1505: The ADR references a non-existent\nget_control_plane_from_di() function and should instead follow ModKit's\ndocumented DI pattern; either implement get_control_plane_from_di() to return\nthe required control-plane type or refactor the constructor to accept/inject\nModKit's Extension<Arc<Service>> (or the concrete Extension wrapper used in the\nrepo) and pull the control plane from that Extension in the same place where\nREST handlers use Extension<Arc<Service>> (see the ModKit unified system docs).\nUpdate the ADR example to use the concrete symbol Extension<Arc<Service>> (or\nyour project's Extension wrapper) and the appropriate service type so the\ncontrol-plane injection is consistent with existing DI usage.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk/Cargo.toml`:\n- Around line 1-10: The new crate \"cf-oagw-sdk\" (lib name oagw_sdk) in\nmodules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk is not listed in the root workspace members, so it\nwon't build as part of the workspace; update the root Cargo.toml's members array\nto include \"modules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk\" (following the pattern used for\nmodules/system/nodes_registry/nodes_registry-sdk) so the cf-oagw-sdk crate is\npicked up by the workspace build.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/cache-invalidation.md`:\n- Around line 327-356: The route and plugin cache key patterns are missing\ntenant scoping which can cause cross-tenant cache bleed; update the route key\npattern \"route:{upstream_id}:{method}:{path}\" to include tenant context (e.g.,\n\"route:{tenant_id}:{upstream_id}:{method}:{path}\") and update the plugin key\n\"plugin:{plugin_id}\" to include tenant_id (e.g.,\n\"plugin:{tenant_id}:{plugin_id}\") or explicitly state that plugin_id/upstream_id\nare globally unique; change any invalidation rules that reference these keys\n(e.g., invalidations on PUT/DELETE for routes/plugins and cascade on DELETE\n/upstreams/{id}) to use the new tenant-scoped key format so cache invalidation\nremains correct per tenant.\n- Around line 29-45: Add explicit language tags to all fenced code blocks in\nmodules/system/oagw/scenarios/cache-invalidation.md to satisfy markdownlint\nMD040; for the three caches shown (the CP L1, DP L1 and DP L2 fenced blocks that\ncurrently start with ```), update each fence to include a language like \"text\"\n(e.g., change ``` to ```text) so they become ```text ... ``` consistently across\nthe file.\n\nIn\n`@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-api/upstreams/positive-2.12-list-upstreams-includes-disabled-resources.md`:\n- Around line 70-74: The file contains a duplicate \"Expected response\" heading\n(MD024); rename the second heading (the one before the bullet list that\ndescribes `200 OK` and included/excluded hosts) to a unique, descriptive title\nsuch as \"Expected filtered response\" or \"Filtered list\" and update any internal\nreferences to that section accordingly so the duplicate-heading rule is\nsatisfied; look for the second \"Expected response\" heading in the markdown\naround the example response and replace it with the new heading.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-operations.md`:\n- Around line 215-217: The plugin cache key \"plugin:{plugin_id}\" omits tenant_id\nand can cause cross-tenant cache leakage; update all places that set or read the\nplugin cache (the DP L1/L2 and CP L1 flows that use the key\n\"plugin:{plugin_id}\") to include tenant_id (e.g.,\n\"plugin:{tenant_id}:{plugin_id}\") so the cache is namespaced per tenant, and\nadjust any cache get/set/delete calls or helpers that build the key to\naccept/derive tenant_id accordingly.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-operations.md`:\n- Around line 22-25: Update the \"API Handler\" bullet that currently reads\n\"Applies inbound rate limiting\" to use the hyphenated compound adjective\n\"Applies inbound rate-limiting\" in the\nmodules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-operations.md documentation; locate the \"API\nHandler\" section and edit the second bullet so the compound modifier is\nhyphenated.\n- Around line 1-6: The markdown contains many unlabeled code fences in the\n\"Proxy Operations Flow\" document; update each unlabeled triple-backtick block to\ninclude the appropriate language tag (use text for ASCII flow diagrams, json for\nJSON payloads, http for HTTP request/response examples, and bash for\nshell/command snippets) so markdownlint rule MD040 is satisfied; search for\nunlabeled fences under headings like \"Proxy Operations Flow\", \"Overview\", and\nthe subsequent example sections and add the matching language identifier to each\ncode fence.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (9)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **Scope `allow(dead_code)` more narrowly.**\n\nCrate-wide suppression can hide legitimate unused items. Prefer `#[allow(dead_code)]` on specific modules/items or guard it behind `cfg(test)` while stubs mature.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-operations.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`9-18`: **Add language identifiers to fenced code blocks (MD040).**\n\nmarkdownlint flags unlabeled fences; use `text` (or a more specific language) to keep lint clean.  \n\n\n<details>\n<summary>✅ Example fix pattern</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n Client Request\n   → API Handler (auth, rate limit)\n     → Control Plane (orchestrate)\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 65-72, 76-86, 108-112, 133-150, 154-163, 167-172, 176-181, 211-214, 217-220, 223-227\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-rust-abi-client-library.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1240-1266`: **Handle CRLF in SSE parsing.**\n\nSSE allows `\\r\\n`; current parsing only detects `\\n\\n`. Normalize CRLF or handle both delimiters to avoid missing events.  \n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Suggested fix (CRLF tolerant)</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        if let Some(pos) = self.buffer.windows(2).position(|w| w == b\"\\n\\n\") {\n-            let event_bytes = self.buffer.drain(..pos + 2).collect::<Vec<u8>>();\n+        if let Some(pos) = self.buffer.windows(4).position(|w| w == b\"\\r\\n\\r\\n\") {\n+            let event_bytes = self.buffer.drain(..pos + 4).collect::<Vec<u8>>();\n+            return Ok(Some(Self::parse_sse_event(&event_bytes)?));\n+        }\n+        if let Some(pos) = self.buffer.windows(2).position(|w| w == b\"\\n\\n\") {\n+            let event_bytes = self.buffer.drain(..pos + 2).collect::<Vec<u8>>();\n             return Ok(Some(Self::parse_sse_event(&event_bytes)?));\n         }\n```\n\n```diff\n-        for line in data.split(|&b| b == b'\\n') {\n+        for line in data.split(|&b| b == b'\\n') {\n+            let line = line.strip_suffix(b\"\\r\").unwrap_or(line);\n             if line.is_empty() {\n                 continue;\n             }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-api/routes/positive-3.10-list-routes-includes-disabled-resources.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`91-95`: **Fix duplicate heading to improve document navigation.**\n\nThe static analysis tool flagged duplicate `## Expected response` headings (lines 75 and 91). Consider renaming this to distinguish it, e.g., `## Expected response (filtered)`.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-## Expected response\n+## Expected response (filtered)\n \n - `200 OK`\n - Response contains only `/get` route\n - `/post` route is excluded\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-rate-limiting.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`334-338`: **MVP scope is appropriate; consider documenting RISK-11 mitigation for Phase 2.**\n\nThe MVP per-instance rate limiting is reasonable. However, the security review (RISK-11) flagged that multiple child tenants can multiply effective rate limits against a shared upstream. The current scope options include `global`, but the ADR doesn't explicitly address how to aggregate limits *per upstream* across tenants (as recommended in RISK-11).\n\nConsider adding a note or future consideration for Phase 2 that addresses upstream-scoped aggregate limits to prevent tenant multiplication bypass.\n\n---\n\n`354-356`: **Document Redis failure mode impact on accuracy bounds.**\n\nThe fallback behavior (`local_only`) is documented, but consider quantifying the accuracy degradation. During Redis outages, effective limits could be `configured_limit * node_count` in worst case. This helps operators understand the risk profile.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-operations.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`9-14`: **Add language specifier to fenced code blocks.**\n\nThe flow diagram code blocks at lines 9-14 and 131-137 lack language specifiers, triggering markdownlint MD040 warnings. Consider using `text` or `plaintext` for ASCII diagrams.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n Client Request\n   → API Handler (auth, rate limit)\n     → Data Plane (validate, write DB, invalidate cache)\n       → Response\n ```\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/PRD.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`444-454`: **Add language labels to fenced code blocks.**\n\nMarkdownlint flags the code blocks showing API endpoints (lines 444-449 and 452-454) for missing language identifiers. Adding `http` or `text` labels improves rendering and silences linter warnings.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```http\n POST/GET/PUT/DELETE /api/oagw/v1/upstreams[/{id}]\n POST/GET/PUT/DELETE /api/oagw/v1/routes[/{id}]\n POST/GET/DELETE /api/oagw/v1/plugins[/{id}]\n GET /api/oagw/v1/plugins/{id}/source\n ```\n\n-```\n+```http\n {METHOD} /api/oagw/v1/proxy/{alias}[/{path}][?{query}]\n ```\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`223-236`: **Add language labels to fenced code blocks.**\n\nMultiple flow diagrams and code blocks lack language identifiers, triggering markdownlint MD040 warnings. Adding `text` labels improves rendering consistency.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fixes</summary>\n\n```diff\n #### Management Operations Flow\n \n-```\n+```text\n Client Request: POST /api/oagw/v1/upstreams\n │\n └─→ API Handler\n```\n\n```diff\n #### Proxy Operations Flow\n \n-```\n+```text\n Client Request: GET /api/oagw/v1/proxy/openai/v1/chat/completions\n │\n └─→ API Handler\n```\n\n```diff\n ### Module Structure\n \n-```\n+```text\n modules/system/oagw/\n ├── oagw-sdk/          # Public API traits, models, errors\n```\n\n```diff\n **Plugin Execution Order**:\n \n-```\n+```text\n Incoming Request\n   -> Auth Plugin (credential injection)\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 242-269, 281-303, 390-398\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/527#pullrequestreview-3777599335","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/527","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/527#pullrequestreview-3777599335"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/527"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-10T08:53:48Z","commit_id":"c4001447ea4b5a7a1747d7b00479084589d01bd9"},{"id":3778753151,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7hOzJ_","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 15**\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> Some comments are outside the diff and can’t be posted inline due to platform limitations.\n> \n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>⚠️ Outside diff range comments (2)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-rust-abi-client-library.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `2228-2236`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n> \n> **Security considerations should address redirect handling and proxy headers (RISK-03, RISK-15).**\n> \n> The security considerations section is missing guidance on:\n> \n> 1. **Redirect handling (RISK-03)**: The ADR doesn't specify whether the client follows HTTP redirects. Following redirects can lead to SSRF if redirect targets aren't validated. Consider documenting:\n>    - Whether redirects are followed (recommend: disabled by default)\n>    - If enabled, apply SSRF checks to redirect targets\n>    - Strip `Authorization` when redirecting across domains\n> \n> 2. **Proxy header spoofing (RISK-15)**: When forwarding headers (line 1655-1657), `X-Forwarded-*`, `X-Real-IP`, and `Forwarded` headers from the original request should be stripped or overwritten to prevent spoofing.\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `797-802`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Fix the “duplicate alias” example text.**\n> \n> This example is labeled invalid, but it says “No CONFLICT,” which contradicts the heading.  \n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>✍️ Suggested fix</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n> -Tenant A: alias=\"my-service\" → server: 10.0.1.2:443  No CONFLICT\n> +Tenant A: alias=\"my-service\" → server: 10.0.1.2:443  CONFLICT\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n````\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/Cargo.toml`:\n- Around line 1-13: The root Cargo.toml's workspace members list is missing the\nsub-workspace \"modules/system/oagw\"; update the root workspace members array to\ninclude \"modules/system/oagw\" so the sub-workspace declared by the [workspace]\nand [workspace.package] blocks in Cargo.toml is part of the repository\nworkspace; ensure the entry matches the relative path exactly and that the root\n[workspace] resolver and members syntax remains valid.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-circuit-breaker.md`:\n- Around line 371-375: Remove the stray \"No \" prefixes from the four negative\nbullets so they read as intended; change \"- No Adds complexity to request\nhandling path\", \"- No Dependency on Redis for distributed state\", \"- No False\npositives possible during upstream maintenance\", and \"- No Additional\nmonitoring/alerting needed\" to \"Adds complexity to request handling path\",\n\"Dependency on Redis for distributed state\", \"False positives possible during\nupstream maintenance\", and \"Additional monitoring/alerting needed\" respectively\nso the list items no longer incorrectly include \"No\".\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-component-architecture.md`:\n- Around line 44-78: The repo uses reversed crate names: oagw-cp actually\nimplements the Data Plane and oagw-dp implements the Control Plane; either\nrename crates to clear names (preferred) or add a prominent warning in ADR and\nall docs. To fix, pick one: (A) Rename crates from oagw-cp -> oagw-data-plane\nand oagw-dp -> oagw-control-plane (update folder names, Cargo.toml package\nnames, any references in code/tests/CI, and adjust module exports and imports\nsuch as OAGWClientV1, ControlPlaneService, DataPlaneService), and update docs\n(adr-component-architecture.md, management-operations.md, README) to use the new\nnames; or (B) add a bold warning at the top of adr-component-architecture.md and\npropagate the same note to management-operations.md and other docs clarifying\nthat oagw-cp = Data Plane and oagw-dp = Control Plane and recommend using full\nnames in prose; ensure all references to oagw-cp/oagw-dp in code comments and\ndocs are updated to match the chosen approach.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-concurrency-control.md`:\n- Around line 421-424: The three negative bullet points currently start with a\nstray \"No\" prefix which inverts their meaning; remove the leading \"No \" from the\nbullets that read \"No Additional configuration complexity\", \"No Potential false\nrejections during traffic spikes (mitigated by backpressure/queueing)\", and \"No\nLocal-only limiting means limit is approximation across nodes\" so they read as\nintended (\"Additional configuration complexity\", \"Potential false rejections\nduring traffic spikes (mitigated by backpressure/queueing)\", and \"Local-only\nlimiting means limit is approximation across nodes\"). Ensure the punctuation and\nsurrounding list formatting in adr-concurrency-control.md remains consistent\nafter the edits.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-rate-limiting.md`:\n- Around line 240-241: The paragraph uses ambiguous labels \"Data Plane (CP)\" and\n\"Control Plane (DP)\"; update it to remove ambiguity by either dropping the\nparentheticals or by adding a one-line mapping that restates the crate-name\ninversion (e.g., \"Note: our crate names are inverted—'CP' refers to the Data\nPlane crate and 'DP' refers to the Control Plane crate\") right after the\nsentence mentioning \"Data Plane (CP)\" so ownership reads consistently; adjust\nthe sentence referencing \"ADR: State Management\" only if needed to keep the\nmapping clear.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-request-routing.md`:\n- Around line 22-27: The routing table entries `/api/oagw/v1/upstreams/*`,\n`/api/oagw/v1/routes/*`, and `/api/oagw/v1/plugins/*` currently state they route\nto \"Control Plane\" but the rationale section titled \"Why DP handles management\noperations\" claims the Data Plane owns configuration—make them consistent by\neither: (A) changing the routing table entries to route those paths to \"Data\nPlane\" (if DP should own management operations), or (B) updating the rationale\nheading and prose from \"Why DP handles management operations\" / \"DP owns\nconfiguration data and database access\" to instead state that the Control Plane\n(CP) handles management operations and owns configuration, ensuring the terms\n\"Control Plane\" or \"Data Plane\" are used consistently across the routing table\nand the rationale section.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw-dp/Cargo.toml`:\n- Line 5: The Cargo.toml description currently says \"Control Plane\nimplementation - configuration management and caching\" but this crate is the\nData Plane; update the description field in Cargo.toml (the description = \"...\")\nto accurately describe the Data Plane crate (e.g., mention Data Plane\nresponsibilities such as request handling, traffic forwarding, or data-plane\ncaching) so it no longer references \"Control Plane\".\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/PRD.md`:\n- Around line 450-460: The fenced code blocks listing the API endpoints (blocks\ncontaining \"POST/GET/PUT/DELETE /api/oagw/v1/upstreams[/{id}]\" and \"{METHOD}\n/api/oagw/v1/proxy/{alias}[/{path}][?{query}]\") lack language identifiers and\ntrigger MD040; update both opening fences from ``` to ```text so the blocks\nbecome ```text ... ``` to satisfy the lint rule and preserve formatting.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-operations.md`:\n- Around line 21-24: The routing table incorrectly labels management paths as\ngoing to the Data Plane; update the three entries for\n`/api/oagw/v1/upstreams/*`, `/api/oagw/v1/routes/*`, and\n`/api/oagw/v1/plugins/*` so they route to the Control Plane (i.e., replace \"DP\"\nwith \"Control Plane\" or \"oagw-dp\") to reflect that CRUD management APIs are\nowned by the Control Plane.\n- Around line 211-213: The route cache key \"route:{upstream_id}:{method}:{path}\"\nlacks tenant scoping and can cause cross-tenant collisions; update all usages\nand documentation to include tenant_id (e.g.,\n\"route:{tenant_id}:{upstream_id}:{method}:{path}\"), and ensure the same change\nis applied for DP L1/L2 and CP L1 (notified by the API Handler) so cache\nreads/writes use the tenant-scoped key consistently.\n- Around line 110-137: The document inconsistently uses \"DP\" and \"Control\nPlane/CP\" in the update and cache-invalidation flows; standardize to \"Control\nPlane\" (or its abbreviation \"CP\") everywhere by replacing instances like \"routes\nto DP\", \"DP writes to DB\", \"DP flushes L1/L2 cache\", and \"DP returns success to\nAPI Handler\" with \"routes to Control Plane/CP\", \"CP writes to DB\", \"CP flushes\nL1/L2 cache\", and \"CP returns success to API Handler\", and ensure the Cache\nInvalidation Flow block and the step list consistently use the chosen term and\nthe same cache key names (e.g., `upstream:{tenant_id}:openai`) and \"API Handler\"\nreferences remain unchanged.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/plugin-execution.md`:\n- Around line 108-117: The example API key \"sk-proj-abc123...\" in the\nplugin-execution scenario should be redacted to avoid secret-scanner hits;\nupdate the example in the Actions/Output blocks (the Authorization header\nexample and any other occurrences around the noted range) to use a non-secret\nplaceholder such as \"sk-REDACTED\" or \"sk-test-XXXX\" (e.g., replace\n`sk-proj-abc123...` with `sk-REDACTED`) so the request context/Headers examples\nremain illustrative but contain no real-looking secrets.\n\nIn\n`@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/custom-header-routing/negative-2.1-unknown-host.md`:\n- Around line 45-47: Update the \"exact match\" validation wording in the\ncustom-header routing spec (negative-2.1-unknown-host.md) to prescribe\nnormalization and edge-case handling: state that header hostnames must be\nnormalized to ASCII/Punycode, compared case-insensitively, trimmed of\nleading/trailing whitespace, and with any trailing dot removed before\ncomparison; explicitly forbid control characters and `@` and treat malformed\ninput as rejected; and specify how port suffixes are handled (either strip\n`:port` and validate separately or reject headers containing a port). Reference\nRISK-04 recommendations 1–4 in the text and include the four concrete\nnormalization rules (punycode, case-insensitive, forbidden characters, port\nhandling) so implementers know exactly how to normalize and validate header\nvalues.\n- Around line 1-47: Update the negative-2.1-unknown-host.md test suite to add\nadditional header-parsing edge cases: add scenarios for IDN/punycode vs Unicode\nequivalents, control characters and leading/trailing whitespace (CRLF, null\nbytes, spaces), userinfo syntax containing '@', homograph/lookalike characters,\nand trailing-dot FQDNs; ensure each case asserts a 400 with the same error type\nand includes the configured valid_hosts list as in the existing expected\nresponse. Reference the existing\nnegative-2.2-ip-address-when-hostname-expected.md and\npositive-3.2-case-insensitive-matching.md tests for structure and reuse their\nvalidation/assertion patterns (status 400, X-OAGW-Error-Source: gateway,\nproblem+json body) so the new cases follow the same request/response format and\nerror field checks.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-operations.md`:\n- Around line 27-43: Summary: The document misuses CP/DP labels—\"Calls DP for\nconfig resolution\" should refer to the Control Plane and the ownership\nstatements conflict with adr-request-routing.md. Fix: update the Data Plane\nsection to say \"Calls CP for config resolution (on cache miss)\" and reconcile\nthe ownership sentence by either changing \"DP owns configuration data and\ndatabase access\" in adr-request-routing.md to match this doc or add a clarifying\nnote here that Control Plane resolves and serves authoritative configs while\nData Plane may cache and read from L1/L2; ensure references to \"Resolves\nupstream by alias\", \"Returns config from L1 cache → L2 cache → DB\", \"Data\nPlane\", and \"Control Plane\" use consistent ownership language across both docs.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw-api/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **Crate-level `dead_code` suppression is acceptable for stubs—consider scoping or removing later.**\n\nThis blanket allowance is fine during initial development. Once the crate matures, consider removing this attribute or scoping `#[allow(dead_code)]` to specific items to avoid masking genuinely unused code.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw-dp/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-13`: **Naming inconsistency between crate documentation and module structure.**\n\nThe crate documentation states this implements the \"Control Plane\" (lines 1-9), but:\n1. The crate is named `oagw-dp` (conventionally \"Data Plane\")\n2. It declares `pub mod data_plane;` (line 13)\n\nThis creates confusion: is this crate the Control Plane or Data Plane? Consider either:\n- Renaming the module to `control_plane` to match the documented purpose, or\n- Clarifying in the documentation why a Control Plane crate contains a `data_plane` module\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested clarification</summary>\n\nIf the module truly implements Control Plane functionality:\n```diff\n-pub mod data_plane;\n+pub mod control_plane;\n```\n\nOr if the module name is intentional, expand the documentation to explain the relationship.\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-plugin-system.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`21-37`: **Consider documenting credential isolation between plugin types (RISK-09).**\n\nPer the security review (RISK-09), the execution order (Auth → Guard → Transform) means Transform plugins execute after auth credentials are injected. This raises a concern: Transform plugins can potentially read sensitive `Authorization` headers added by Auth plugins.\n\nConsider adding a note in the ADR about:\n- Whether `RequestContext` hides/redacts sensitive headers from Transform plugins\n- Plugin ordering guarantees and their security implications\n- Log-size limits for plugin outputs to prevent credential leakage via logs\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/docs/adr-request-routing.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`33-38`: **Add language identifiers to fenced code blocks.**\n\nThe flow diagram code blocks (lines 33-38 and 42-51) lack language identifiers. Use `text` for ASCII flow diagrams to satisfy markdownlint MD040.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>✍️ Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n Client\n → API Handler (auth, rate limit)\n → Control Plane (validate, write DB, invalidate cache)\n → Response\n ```\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 42-51\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/527#pullrequestreview-3778753151","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/527","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/527#pullrequestreview-3778753151"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/527"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-10T12:48:41Z","commit_id":"9f1410ff06eed7a636699d488d73f43928f784cf"}]}]}
